## filediff tree

```
src/
├── domain.objects/
│   ├── ActorEnrolled.ts                                  [+] create — the ON-DISK actor record (blocker-2 fix; the object F5 undercounted). fields: repoPath, hash, brain, roles: string[] — **NO `dir` field** (i006-r11-B2: the actor.via.hash path is a PURE deterministic transform of {repoPath, hash} via getActorEnrolledDir, so to ALSO store `dir` would be the SAME two-sources-of-truth trap i005-nitpick-2 fixed for Clone.socketPath — worse here, since a worktree/repo relocation (a named edge case elsewhere) would stale every stored `dir` at once. so `dir` is DERIVED FRESH via getActorEnrolledDir at every read site, never persisted). CONTENT-ADDRESSED + IMMUTABLE by construction (i005-nitpick-6): its identity IS its content — the hash === `genEnrollmentHash({brain,roles})` — and it never mutates in place; a roleset change mints a DIFFERENT hash === a DIFFERENT record, never an edit of this one. so it is a value the domain holds, not an identity assigned across mutation. **F6 (fulcrum, best-guess + clean rework):** shape it as a **DomainEntity** with primary=['repoPath','hash'], unique=['repoPath','hash'] (i037-r12-1 fix — REPO-SCOPED IDENTITY, the review's blocker #1): the earlier primary=['hash'] ALONE was a FALSE identity contract, since `hash = genEnrollmentHash({brain,roles})` carries NO repo dimension — so the SAME {brain,roles} enrolled in TWO different repos would COLLIDE on a bare `Ref<ActorEnrolled>`, and a cross-repo registry or a multi-checkout process would silently conflate two genuinely-distinct actors (each lives under its OWN repo's `.agent/.actors/` tree, with its OWN clones/sockets/history, so they ARE operationally distinct). every op today threads `repoPath` alongside the hash as a separate param, which MASKED the bug at the call sites but left the domain object's OWN declared identity untrue — a future consumer that trusts the bare ref inherits the collision. so `repoPath` joins the key: the actor's true identity is {repoPath, hash} — "this roleset, in THIS repo". this is a genuine IDENTITY dimension, NOT the derived `dir` the i006-r11-B2 decision removed — `dir` is a pure transform OF {repoPath,hash} (still derived fresh via getActorEnrolledDir, never stored), whereas `repoPath` is an INPUT dimension of the identity itself, so the two decisions stay consistent: STORE the identity inputs {repoPath, hash, brain, roles}, DERIVE the path. `Clone.actor` becomes a `RefByPrimary<ActorEnrolled>` over {repoPath, hash} — a cross-repo-truthful referent (the hydrator already holds repoPath in scope, so it builds the whole key, not a hash alone). primary≡unique because the {repoPath, content-address} pair IS the natural key AND the only stable identifier. the honest twin would be a **DomainLiteral** (identity-by-all-properties fits a content-addressed value exactly), and the reviewer is right that Entity-with-primary≡unique is the tell of a content-addressed value in an identity shape. it stays a DomainEntity for ONE concrete reason: `Clone.actor` needs a TYPED `RefByPrimary<ActorEnrolled>` (the blocker-2 fix — a real referent, not a bare `actorHash: string`), and domain-objects' key-based Ref is an entity concept. the clean rework, if the council prefers the literal: make ActorEnrolled a DomainLiteral and have Clone carry the actor hash via a hash-typed ref instead of RefByPrimary — one object + one Clone field, no downstream teardown. this is a SEPARATE concept from the extant `src/domain.objects/Actor.ts` — that Actor is the in-memory SDK composition (live `act`/`run`/`ask` methods + `brains: ActorBrain[]`, keyed `unique=['role.slug']`), NOT serializable and NOT a persisted record. F5's "reuse BrainCliEnrollmentManifest as the actor identity" holds — ActorEnrolled is the thin PERSISTED wrapper of that manifest's `{brain,roles}` + its `genEnrollmentHash`. the two-Actor split is "one concept, two layers" made concrete: `Actor` = runtime brain⊕role, `ActorEnrolled` = on-disk enrolled identity
│   └── Clone.ts                                          [+] create — DomainEntity, primary=['serial'], unique=['slug'] (blocker-1 fix — matches define.actor-clone-hierarchy.md: "serial = the clone's primary ref (RefByPrimary); its --as slug is a unique ref (RefByUnique)" AND asCloneRef's own uuid→primary / name→unique parse). NULLABLE-UNIQUE semantics called out explicitly: slug is null when enrolled without `--as`, and two slug=null clones must NEVER collide as a unique-key match — a null slug is "no unique key," never a shared key (getOneCloneByRef resolves a null-slug clone by serial alone, never by a null-slug lookup). fields: serial, slug (null-ok), actor: RefByPrimary<typeof ActorEnrolled> (keyed by the actor's FULL {repoPath, hash} composite primary — i039-r11-B1 fix: NOT the hash alone. `ActorEnrolled.primary` was corrected to `['repoPath','hash']` (i037-r12-1) precisely because the hash collides across repos, so the ref that POINTS at it must carry the SAME composite key, else a bare `Ref<ActorEnrolled>` re-opens the very cross-repo collision the object-level fix closed — the "one decision, two places, one updated" trap. points at the ActorEnrolled entity above, a typed Ref per the extant ContextBrain/Template convention, NOT a bare `actorHash: string`), **socketEligible: boolean** (i005-nitpick-2: NOT a stored socket PATH — the path is DERIVED from serial at read time (getCloneSocketPath, a communicator that composes serial + host env — i020-r04-1), so to ALSO store it would be two sources of truth: if the path algorithm ever evolves (e.g. a versioned XDG subpath), every clone dir written by an older build carries a stale stored path that no longer matches the recompute, and isCloneLive/connectToClone would misread a perfectly-upgraded clone as DEAD. so Clone stores only the ELIGIBILITY FLAG — `true` = this clone stood up a socket (interactive + capable), `false` = a plain-spawn FALLBACK clone (no-pty / non-capable / `--no-socket`) that never had one — and the path is DERIVED FRESH from serial at every read. this flag, not a null path, is what distinguishes NOT-REACHABLE from DEAD, blocker-5), **spawnedAt: IsoTimeStamp** (i005-r12-B: the SPAWN timestamp — an iso-time type per rule.require.iso-time, NOT a bare Date/number — captured at bake into identity.json. this is the data model behind the vision's `clone list` "since=2m ago" column AND the `__AGO__` snapshot mask the test-infra reserves; without it the vision's list render + the mask had no referent. it drives ONLY "since spawn". the HARDER "idle 20m" signal the vision's cron reasons over needs a `lastActivityAt` refreshed on every dispatch/output — that is per-dispatch activity capture, a clone LIFECYCLE concern SCOPED to the `rhx clone` dream, not this behavior; the vision's "idle 20m" is illustrative of the dream's richer signal, and this behavior delivers `since=<spawn>` as the honest subset), **hostHash: string** (i007-r11-B2 fix — a PERSISTED field, was drift: i006-r12-4 added hostHash to identity.json but not to this field list; getHomeHash at bake, for the cross-host ERROR-HINT only, never liveness), **hostPid: number** + **hostPidStartedAt: string** (i026-r12-4 + the PID-REUSE guard i027-r12-3: the pty child's process id AND its process start-time, BOTH captured at bake — the ORPHAN-DETECTION referent, NOT a liveness source: liveness stays socket-derived per F4. their SOLE use is the SIGKILL-orphan cost mitigation — a `kill -9`'d wrapper leaves a real, live brain that still draws credits yet reads socket-DEAD; `clone list` for a socket-DEAD clone checks, via isCloneOrphanProcess, whether `hostPid` is alive ON THIS host (only meaningful when hostHash === the current host) AND whether the live pid's start-time equals `hostPidStartedAt` (THE RECYCLED-PID GUARD: OS pids recycle, so a pid later reused by an UNRELATED process has a different start-time and is NOT flagged — a false positive must never point a human at the wrong process) and, if all hold, adds an ADVISORY `DEAD (orphan? pid <n> may still be this brain — verify with ps, then kill to halt charges)` — a VERIFY-FIRST detection for a real cost trap, ledger row 11, NEVER a kill-on-sight imperative. a cross-host DEAD clone skips the pid check (a foreign pid is not ours to probe); a host that cannot read a start-time suppresses the advisory (fail-safe — a hint that could misfire is withheld). **DERIVED-NOT-PERSISTED fields (i007-r11-B2):** `actor` and `historyDir` are NOT written to identity.json — they are ENRICHED at HYDRATION time when a Clone is read from disk: `actor` from the parent `actor.via.hash=<hash>` dir segment COMBINED with the `repoPath` already in hydration scope — the FULL {repoPath, hash} composite ref (i039-r11-B1: getOneCloneHydrated threads repoPath so the built ref MATCHES ActorEnrolled's composite primary; a hash-only ref here would re-enter the cross-repo collision i037-r12-1 closed, since the hydrator is the ONE place the ref is built), `historyDir` from `getCloneDir + '/history'`. the attach is owned by ONE seam — getOneCloneHydrated (i017-r11-2) — NOT re-authored per reader: getOneCloneByRef, getAllClonesForActor, and getAllClonesGroupedByActor ALL compose getOneCloneHydrated, so a Clone is never hand-assembled and the enrichment can never drift caller-to-caller (before the seam, each reader independently remembered to attach both fields — one omitted attach in a future hydrator would yield a Clone with an undefined `actor` ref, undetected by the type system). to STORE the two fields would instead reintroduce the exact stored-vs-derivable trap fixed twice (Clone.socketPath→socketEligible, ActorEnrolled.dir). so identity.json's persisted shape is `{serial,slug,socketEligible,spawnedAt,hostHash,hostPid,hostPidStartedAt}` and the Clone OBJECT additionally carries `actor`+`historyDir` derived on read. NOTE: `brain` + `roles` are NOT stored on Clone — they are the ACTOR's identity (the actor hash IS genEnrollmentHash({brain,roles})), reached via `Clone.actor` → ActorEnrolled; to store a per-clone copy would denormalize the actor and risk drift (single-source-of-truth, [rule.require.domain-driven-design]). `clone list` shows brain+roles at the ACTOR-group header (resolved via Clone.actor → the ActorEnrolled record), not on the clone line
│
├── domain.operations/
│   ├── actor/enrolled/                                    (i005-nitpick-3: the ON-DISK ActorEnrolled grain = hash namespace — a SUBFOLDER of actor/ (NOT a sibling), so the on-disk persisted-record ops sit UNDER the actor cluster yet in their own `enrolled/` leaf, never mixed into actor/'s own file list beside the runtime Actor's genActor. the vision names "two grains of actor" as a known reader-confusion hazard; the `actor/enrolled/` nesting keeps the on-disk grain visibly distinct at the folder layer WITHOUT a second top-level `actor`-word folder, and composes with the `rhx clone` dream's future `actor/declared/` (the slug namespace) as a peer leaf under the same actor/ parent. named for the ActorEnrolled grain they operate on, per rule.require.group-by-noun-not-verb + filename===opname. this nesting also makes the flagged runtime-`actor/`-rename MOOT — see the actor/ note below)
│   │   ├── getActorEnrolledDir.ts                        [+] transformer — repo + hash → actor.via.hash path (actor list needs THIS without a serial). ON-DISK KEY IS HASH-ONLY, BY CONSTRUCTION — NOT a contradiction of the composite {repoPath, hash} identity (i052-r11-4 scope note): the derived path is `<repoPath>/.agent/.actors/actor.via.hash=<hash>/`, so the `repoPath` dimension of the identity is ENCODED BY WHICH TREE the dir sits in, NOT by a path segment inside it — two DISTINCT repos have two DISTINCT `.agent/.actors/` trees (each tree lives under its own repo root), so a hash-only segment WITHIN one tree can never collide across repos. the composite key's SEPARATE `repoPath` dimension (ActorEnrolled.primary, i037-r12-1) governs a FUTURE CROSS-REPO REGISTRY that would hold actors from MANY trees in ONE comparison (where the tree-location context is absent) — that is the case a bare hash-key would conflate, and the composite key closes it. a SYMLINKED checkout is NOT a collision: getOneRepoPath canonicalizes via realpathSync, so a symlink resolves to the SAME canonical repoPath → the SAME identity → CORRECTLY the same actor (they ARE one repo). the ONE genuinely-pathological case: a deliberate BIND-MOUNT that points two DISTINCT canonical repoPaths at ONE physical `.agent/.actors/` tree — then two identities share one on-disk dir. this is OUT OF SCOPE (a hostile/exotic fs topology no ordinary checkout produces), named here as a scope note, NOT silently ignored; the cheap guard if it ever matters is a `.repo` marker file inside each actor dir checked against the canonicalized repoPath on read (fail-loud on mismatch) — flagged as a one-file clean-rework, not built now (the composite key + the per-repo tree location cover every non-bind-mount case)
│   │   ├── findsertActorEnrolled.ts                      [+] orchestrator — ensure the enrolled-actor dir (brain config + roles log). ATOMICITY PARITY WITH THE CLONE BAKE (i024-r12-3 — the review's actor-vs-clone asymmetry: setCloneIdentity got an atomic temp+rename + a corrupt-record guard, findsertActorEnrolled had no equivalent named): its two writes are ALREADY atomic by construction — genBrainCliConfigArtifact composes writeClaudeCodeSettings, whose dao writes temp+rename (the extant atomic-write discipline, so a mid-bake Ctrl-C leaves a WHOLE settings.json or NONE, never a torn half), and setActorEnrolledRolesLog appends with O_APPEND (a single append under PIPE_BUF is POSIX-atomic, so a concurrent enroll never tears a line). so a first-ever enroll interrupted mid-bake leaves either a complete actor dir or an absent/partial one whose MISSING file the reader treats as "not yet an actor" — never a half-written file that crashes a reader. this makes the actor bake's atomicity the SAME guarantee the clone bake's setCloneIdentity carries (i006-r12-5), now named rather than left implicit; the reader-side corrupt-record resilience is getAllActorsEnrolled' job (below). REASON THREADED (i046-r12-5 fix — the audit-trail "why" had no CLI input, the r012 blocker): the signature is `(input: { enrollment, repoPath, reason })` — the `reason` flows in from invokeEnroll's NEW `--reason` flag and is passed straight to setActorEnrolledRolesLog. WHY it was a real gap: setActorEnrolledRolesLog ALREADY persists a `reason` field (i024-r11-3, with its own unit test that the op honors a passed-in reason), BUT this op's OLD signature `(input: { enrollment, repoPath })` had NO `reason` param and invokeEnroll had NO `--reason` flag — so `reason` was forced to a constant on every real enroll, which made the audit log's "why" column ALWAYS identical, structurally defeating the vision's own stated purpose ("roles/enrollment.jsonl tracks the history of WHY and which roles were enrolled"). the CLI-supplied reason closes that gap end-to-end: a human/cron can now record WHY this enroll happened. a bare enroll with no `--reason` defaults `reason` to null (the field stays present, honestly empty, never a fabricated motive) — so the audit trail is truthful whether or not a reason is given
│   │   ├── setActorEnrolledRolesLog.ts                   [+] communicator — append one `JSON.stringify({ schemaVersion, at, roles, delta, reason })` line to enrollment.jsonl (i045-r11-2/r12-2: `schemaVersion` = the SAME monotonic-integer forward-compat stamp identity.json carries, off the shared `CLONE_IDENTITY_SCHEMA_VERSION` const — so the actor-side persisted record gets the identical version-aware read discipline the clone-side record does, the review's "neither persisted file carries a schema-version" gap closed on BOTH sides. WHY: getAllActorsEnrolled reads the LATEST enrollment.jsonl line for `roles`; the moment a future behavior adds a field to that line shape, a version-blind strict read would fail-loud on every extant actor's log the SAME way the clone record would — so the line carries its version, and getAllActorsEnrolled reads it version-aware (below), never a strict-exact-shape crash on a field addition) (i024-r11-3 fix — the persisted shape now PINS the `reason` field, which closes the spec gap the review named: findsertActorEnrolled threads `reason` into this op's signature to capture the who/when/why [1.vision frame], but the earlier persisted shape `{ at, roles, delta }` gave `reason` NO on-disk home, so an executor could infer the input was unused and drop it. the field is now DECLARED in the line shape — `at` = the enroll IsoTimeStamp, `roles` = the resolved roleset, `delta` = the `+role`/`-role` tokens, `reason` = the enroll motive — so the "why" the vision demands is genuinely captured, not silently discarded. the extant append-log convention — same shape as the brain-transcript `.jsonl`, NOT a yaml doc; yaml is the repo's whole-manifest format, not its append-log format). SKIPPED ON A PURE LIVE-SLUG REUSE (i050-r11-3/r12-3 fix — the cron-appends-forever mainline the review named): this append is an AUDIT record of a real ENROLLMENT EVENT (a bake or a rebind), NOT a heartbeat. but the i045/i052 live-slug-reuse path (genClone: a second `enroll --as @:driver` while `driver` is LIVE for the actor → reuse the extant clone, returns `{outcome:'reused'}`, no fresh bake) produces NO new enrollment — no fresh clone, no fresh socket, no state change. so if this append ran UNCONDITIONALLY on that path, the wish's OWN headline caller — a cron that re-runs `rhx enroll <brain> --as @:driver` every 5 minutes to keep a clone reachable — would append ONE enrollment.jsonl line per tick FOREVER, pure audit noise that also inflates getAllActorsEnrolled's latest-line read cost. so the append is GATED on the enroll outcome: invokeEnroll determines the reuse-vs-bake decision (via genClone's computeCloneSlugDecision result) and SKIPS setActorEnrolledRolesLog when the outcome is a pure `reuse` (the live-slug reuse render), appending ONLY on `bake-fresh` / `rebind` (a genuine new-clone event). so a re-enroll that spawns a clone records its motive; a re-enroll that merely re-finds a live clone records no line — the audit log grows with real events, not with cron ticks. a clone.acceptance case asserts a second `--as @:driver` against a LIVE owner appends NO new enrollment.jsonl line (the log line-count is unchanged after the reuse), while a bake/rebind DOES append. this closes the unbounded-append at the SOURCE for the common case; the RESIDUAL unbounded growth of a genuinely long-lived actor's audit log (many real bakes/rebinds over a repo's life) is named as the 7th deferred cleanup-family item (rotation/truncation), below. PIPE_BUF-ATOMICITY ENFORCED, NOT ASSUMED (i052-r11-2/r12-1 fix — the atomicity claim was asserted but the line was unbounded): the O_APPEND guarantee above is POSIX-atomic ONLY for a single write UNDER PIPE_BUF (4096B on Linux), and NO CAP bounded the composed line — a long `--reason` (the one unbounded caller-supplied field) plus a many-entry `roles` array could serialize past 4096B and TEAR under a concurrent enroll, the exact torn line the claim forbids. so the line length is now ENFORCED, off ONE `ENROLLMENT_LINE_MAX_BYTES` const (PIPE_BUF minus newline headroom, in utils/enrollmentLineMax.ts): the `reason` field is TRUNCATED to fit with a visible `…[+<n>B elided]` marker (so the enroll SUCCEEDS — the clone spawn is the point, an over-long motive must not block the hot path — and the audit stays honest, the elision is marked, never silent), and ONLY the pathological structural case (the `roles` array ALONE, with an empty reason, still over the ceiling) FAILS LOUD (a ConstraintError that names the byte count + the fix, since a roleset that large is a genuinely malformed enroll). so the atomicity guarantee is VERIFIED at write time, never merely claimed. RUNE-SAFE + JSON-ESCAPE-SAFE TRUNCATION (i059-r12-4 fix — the byte-cut-corrupts-the-record hazard the arch-defects lens named): the truncation cuts the DECODED `reason` STRING at a whole-rune boundary BEFORE serialization, NOT the serialized bytes AFTER. WHY it matters: a naive byte-level cut of the composed line (or of the post-`JSON.stringify` reason) could land MID-UTF-8-RUNE (a multi-byte codepoint torn into an invalid partial sequence) OR MID-JSON-ESCAPE (a `\uXXXX` / `\n` / `\"` escape cut in half) — either produces INVALID JSON, which then FAILS the schemaVersion-tolerant reader (getAllActorsEnrolled, i045) as a genuine "corrupt" record, a SELF-INFLICTED fail-loud in the exact append-log the schemaVersion discipline exists to keep upgrade-safe. so the algorithm drops whole runes from the END of the decoded `reason` until `JSON.stringify({...line, reason: decoded + '…[+<n>B elided]'})` measures ≤ `ENROLLMENT_LINE_MAX_BYTES` (the fit is checked on the SERIALIZED byte-length of the WHOLE line, so a rune whose escaped form is long is accounted for; the CUT is on the decoded string at a rune boundary, so the result is always a valid string that serializes to valid JSON). the `…[+<n>B elided]` marker's `<n>` counts the elided DECODED bytes. so a `reason` full of multi-byte runes (emoji, CJK) or JSON-escape-heavy content (embedded quotes, newlines, control chars) truncates to a still-VALID JSON line, never a torn escape the reader rejects. setActorEnrolledRolesLog.integration gains a row: a line under the limit appends whole; an over-long reason truncates with the `…[+<n>B elided]` marker AND the enroll still succeeds (the appended line stays under PIPE_BUF, asserted); a pathological over-limit roles-array-alone fails loud that names the byte count; AND (i059-r12-4) an over-long reason built of MULTI-BYTE RUNES (a run of emoji / CJK) truncates to a line that `JSON.parse` round-trips CLEAN (asserted the truncated reason is a valid string, never a partial rune), AND an over-long reason dense with JSON-ESCAPE chars (embedded `"`, `\n`, control bytes) likewise truncates to valid JSON — dogfooded: a byte-level cut of the serialized line at the same length yields invalid JSON that the getAllActorsEnrolled reader flags corrupt + the assertion goes red, which proves the rune-boundary cut is essential, not incidental
│   │   └── getAllActorsEnrolled.ts                       [+] communicator — read enrolled actors on disk (for `actor list`) — reuses getAllFilesFromDir [←] (src/infra/filesystem), NOT raw readdir; enumerates ONLY `actor.via.hash=*` entries (i011-r11-2: an explicit hash-prefix filter — the `.agent/.actors/` parent now ALSO holds the sibling `.slugs/` index dir (blocker-1's global slug index) + potentially `.this`-namespace dirs, so an unfiltered walk would list `.slugs` as a bogus "actor". SAME hazard class + SAME fix as getAllClonesForActor's `serial=*` filter (i007-r11-B3) — applied consistently now, was the inconsistency the review named); SORTS the result by hash ASC (i008-r12-3: getAllFilesFromDir / readdir order is NOT stable across filesystems/OSes/CI runners, and this op feeds the mandatory `actor list` + grouped `clone list` snapshots — an unsorted return would flake those snapshots; hash-asc is a deterministic, stable key). MALFORMED-DIR RESILIENCE, ABSENT-vs-CORRUPT (i024-r12-3 — parity with getCloneIdentity's i015-r12-4/i018-r07-2 record guard, the review's actor-side gap): each enumerated `actor.via.hash=*` dir is hydrated into an ActorEnrolled by a read of its identity data (the latest enrollment.jsonl line for `roles` + the brain config). that read applies the SAME absent-vs-corrupt policy the clone read uses: an ABSENT expected file (a mid-bake dir that lost its config/log to an interrupted first-ever enroll) → the dir is SKIPPED as "not yet a complete actor" (the same discipline as the `serial=*`-dir null-record skip, NOT a crash); a PRESENT-but-CORRUPT file (invalid-JSON settings.json or a torn enrollment.jsonl line that is newline-terminated yet unparseable) → FAIL-LOUD MalfunctionError that NAMES the bad file path + the fix ("remove or repair <path>; it is host-local runtime, safe to delete"), NEVER a silent skip (rule.forbid.failhide — a swallowed corrupt actor would make `actor list` + `clone list` under-report). so a torn actor-dir write can no longer crash BOTH surfaces the way an un-guarded torn identity.json would have before the clone-side fix — the actor read now carries the identical resilience. VERSION-AWARE, TOLERANT-SUPERSET (i045-r11-2/r12-2 — the SAME three-way schemaVersion read getCloneIdentity gained): the enrollment.jsonl line is read version-aware — a KNOWN-or-older/absent `schemaVersion` reads through this version's required-field set (defaulting fields added since, TOLERATING extra/unknown fields), a NEWER-unknown version fails loud with the upgrade hint — so a future field ADDITION to the roles-log line (the actor-side twin of the dream's `lastActivityAt` on identity.json) no longer reads every extant actor's log as corrupt on a local rhachet upgrade. the corrupt-fail-loud stays reserved for a genuine parse failure / a required-field-for-its-version miss / a newer-unknown version, exactly as on the clone side
│   ├── actor/                                            (the RUNTIME brain⊕role = in-memory Actor grain — the runtime ops (genActor, actorAct/Ask/Run, findActorBrainInAllowlist, …) sit DIRECTLY under actor/, and the on-disk grain lives in the `actor/enrolled/` SUBFOLDER above. NAME-AMBIGUITY, NOW MOOT (i017-r11-4 — the earlier nitpick-4 concern, closed by the wisher-approved nesting): an earlier draft had the on-disk grain as a SIBLING top-level folder, so two folders both answered to "actor" and a reviewer proposed a `runtimeActor/` rename to disambiguate. the `actor/enrolled/` NESTING closes that hazard at the source (rule.require.solve-at-cause): there is now ONE `actor/` parent, with the runtime ops as its own files and the on-disk ops under the `enrolled/` leaf — no second `actor`-word folder to confuse with this one. so the `runtimeActor/` rename is UNNEEDED — the folder layer disambiguates by nesting, the object layer by name (Actor vs ActorEnrolled), and the extant 17-file `actor/` folder with its cross-module imports stays untouched (no rename ripple, no scope-leak per rule.forbid.scope-leaks). ledger row 4 records this as resolved-by-nesting, not deferred)
