│   │   ├── isSafeCloneSlug.ts                            [+] transformer (i008-r11-B2) — `slug → boolean`: true iff the slug matches `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-_]*` (filesystem-path-safe, no `.`/`/`/`..`). the PATH-TRAVERSAL guard: the slug builds a real path in setCloneSlugIndex (`.agent/.actors/.slugs/<slug>`), so an unvalidated `../../evil` would escape the index dir. invokeEnroll calls this at the SAME `--as` validation boundary as the marker + uuid-shape checks; is* per get-set-gen-verbs; pure + unit-testable (data-driven accept/reject rows)
│   │   ├── asCloneRef.ts                                 [+] transformer (pure) — PARSE a raw `@:<slug|serial>` CLI address → a TYPED clone ref: a uuid body → RefByPrimary (serial), else → RefByUnique (slug). VALIDATES the `@:` marker is present, and REJECTS (fail-loud) a bare `@<actor>` (wrong-grain — an actor address to a clone verb) AND a no-sigil bare string. DID-YOU-MEAN CORRECTION (i012-r12-5): the wrong-grain reject on a bare `@<x>` proposes the CORRECTED clone form in its fix text — `"@driver is an actor address; a clone verb wants a clone address — did you mean @:driver?"` — because the one-character `@`-vs-`@:` difference is an easy typo, and rule.require.errors-name-the-fix wants the error to name the exact correction, not merely reject. DROPPED-SIGIL CORRECTION (i015-r12-1 fix — the MORE COMMON typo): a NO-sigil bare string (`driver`, no `@:` at all — `rhx clone say driver …`) ALSO proposes the corrected form — `"driver lacks the clone sigil — did you mean @:driver?"` — NOT just a bare "no marker" reject. so BOTH the swapped-sigil AND the dropped-sigil typo name their exact fix (the dropped case is the likelier slip, since a user new to the address grammar simply forgets the sigil). this is the address-parse seam every clone verb (say/get) needs BEFORE getOneCloneByRef; without it the `@:` grammar + wrong-grain guard have no owner
│   │   ├── asActorRef.ts                                 [+] transformer (PURE — i006-r11-B1 corrected: this op is a pure PARSE only, no disk) — PARSE a raw `@<actor>` CLI scope address → a typed actor ref carrying the hash-or-PREFIX LITERAL. VALIDATES `@` present and NO `:` (reject a `@:<clone>` where an actor scope is required), extracts the body. DID-YOU-MEAN CORRECTION — BOTH sigil typos (i012-r12-5, extended i024-r12-2 for symmetry with asCloneRef): (a) the WRONG-GRAIN reject on a `@:<x>` proposes the corrected actor form — `"@:driver is a clone address; this wants an actor scope — did you mean @driver?"` — the symmetric twin of asCloneRef's grain hint; (b) the DROPPED-SIGIL reject on a no-`@` bare string (`rhx clone list mechanic`, no sigil at all) proposes the corrected form too — `"mechanic lacks the actor sigil — did you mean @mechanic?"` — NOT a plain "no marker" fail-loud. this closes the asymmetry the review named: asCloneRef hardened BOTH its typos (wrong-grain AND dropped-sigil, i015-r12-1 which flags the dropped case as "the MORE COMMON typo"), and asActorRef sits on the SAME command surface (`clone list [@<actor>]`), so a `clone list mechanic` dropped-sigil slip is equally plausible and now gets the same corrected suggestion, not a dead-end reject (rule.require.errors-name-the-fix). so a sigil typo in EITHER direction, on EITHER parser, names its one-character fix. blocker-4: since enroll mints ONLY anonymous hash actors (no slug actors — actors.yml is the clone dream), the `<actor>` body here is the anonymous actor's HASH (or a git-style PREFIX of it) — so `@<hash>` ≡ `actor://<hash>`, the sanctioned hash-literal form of the `@` sigil for an anonymous actor (consistent with define.address-sigils.md: `@<name>` ≡ `actor://<name>`, and the anonymous actor's "name" IS its hash). the address-sigils brief gains a note that admits this hash-literal form (see reconciliation); `actor list` + `clone list` render a COPYABLE hash so a human can type it. NOTE — the PREFIX MATCH against on-disk hashes is NOT here (it is disk I/O, not a pure parse); it lives in getOneActorEnrolledByRef below, mirroring the extant asCloneRef(pure)/getOneCloneByRef(disk) split, so this op stays genuinely unit-testable
│   │   ├── getOneActorEnrolledByRef.ts                   [+] communicator (i006-r11-B1 fix) — LOOK UP an asActorRef against the on-disk actor set. it COMPOSES getAllActorsEnrolled [←] for the enumeration (i008-r11-B3: it does NOT independently re-walk `.agent/.actors/actor.via.hash=*` — a second walk could drift from `actor list`'s if a filter is ever added to one and not the other; ONE enumerator, both read paths), then matches the ref against that one list: a FULL 64-char hash → exact match; a git-style PREFIX (`9c1e…`) → the actor it UNIQUELY prefixes. an AMBIGUOUS prefix (matches two actors) → fail-loud ConstraintError that NAMES the candidate hashes + the fix (lengthen the prefix). BOUNDED CANDIDATE LIST (i017-r12-5 fix — the review's unbounded-list gap): because actor-dir prune is deferred (the 5-member cleanup family) and every `{brain,roles}` permutation mints a permanent dir, a long-lived checkout could make a short ambiguous prefix match DOZENS of actors — an error that dumps 40 hashes is itself unreadable. so the list is CAPPED: it names at most the FIRST 8 candidate hashes (hash-asc, the deterministic order getAllActorsEnrolled already returns), then, if more remain, appends `… and N more — lengthen the prefix to narrow` — the fix stays the same (a longer prefix), and the error stays scannable no matter how many actors accrue. a ≤8-candidate ambiguity lists them all (no truncation line); a no-match → fail-loud. this is the owner of the prefix-match's two core error paths (ambiguous / no-match) — both now have a real home + tests (was the coverage omission r11-B1/r12-2 flagged). SLUG-SHAPED SCOPE HINT (i015-r12-2 fix, SHARPENED i037-r12-2 — the hex-collision misfire the review named): a no-match `@<actor>` scope means the body prefixed ZERO on-disk actor hashes, so the fail-loud ALWAYS names BOTH recovery paths regardless of the body's shape — `"@cafe matched no actor. if you meant a clone, clone list groups clones under their actor automatically — run a bare 'rhx clone list' to see every clone (@:cafe among them); if you meant an actor, pass a longer hash prefix (e.g. @9c1e4d2a)"`. the EARLIER form GATED the helpful slug-scope correction on whether the body looked hex-shaped, but plenty of legit human slugs ARE valid hex — `cafe`, `dad`, `bee`, `face`, `fed`, `deed`, `bad`, `ace` — so a plausible `rhx clone list @cafe` slug-as-scope typo routed to the LESS-helpful bare "no such actor" branch precisely when the more-helpful one was available for it (a direct discoverability regression a real user hits, the review's core point). the SHARPENED form DROPS the hex-shape gate entirely: since a no-match ALREADY proves the body prefixed no real hash, the dual-path hint is at least as correct for a hash-prefix miss AND strictly better for a slug-as-scope typo, so it fires for EVERY no-match — a hex-like slug (`@cafe`) and a real hash-prefix typo BOTH get the two-path recovery, never a shape-guess that can misfire (rule.require.errors-name-the-fix + discoverability)
│   │   ├── getCloneDir.ts                                [+] transformer — actor dir + serial → clone DIR (identity + history only; the socket is NOT here — see getCloneSocketPath)
│   │   ├── getCloneSocketPath.ts                         [+] communicator (i020-r04-1 fix — was mislabeled transformer) — serial → `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clone.$serial.$homeHash.sock` — MIRRORS getKeyrackDaemonSocketPath; composes infra/host/getHomeHash [←]; keeps the path SHORT (avoids the ~104-char unix-socket limit a repo-nested path would blow) + host-scoped + tmpfs auto-clean. GRAIN = COMMUNICATOR, not transformer (i020-r04-1): it READS host/environment state — `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` off `process.env` AND `getHomeHash` (which reads `os.homedir()`) — so it is NOT deterministic on its `serial` input alone; its output varies with the host env. per define.domain-operation-grains a transformer must be pure (input → output, no i/o), and an env/system read is impure i/o, so this is a communicator. NOTE the DERIVED-NOT-STORED argument for Clone.socketEligible (i005-nitpick-2) is UNAFFECTED by this relabel: the path is still recomputed fresh at every read rather than persisted — the point there is "single source of truth," which holds whether the recompute is pure or reads host state; only the earlier "PURE, deterministic transform of serial" wording was imprecise (the recompute reads the host env), corrected here + at the Clone.ts prose. XDG_RUNTIME_DIR FALLBACK (i017-r12-6 fix — the review's undefined-var gap): `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is NOT set on every POSIX host (macOS does not set it by default), so this reads it with a FALLBACK to `os.tmpdir()` — the SAME fallback getKeyrackDaemonSocketPath uses, so a macOS dev box (the common case) resolves a real tmp path, never an `undefined/clone.….sock`. POSIX-ONLY SCOPE, ACKNOWLEDGED (i017-r12-6): the whole talk surface rests on UNIX DOMAIN SOCKETS — a POSIX mechanism, the SAME assumption the extant keyrack daemon already makes (rhachet's socket IPC is POSIX-native throughout). WINDOWS is OUT OF SCOPE for the socket-reach surface: a non-POSIX host has no `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` and no unix-socket support, so a Windows enroll FALLS BACK to the plain-spawn path (socketEligible=false, `clone list` → NOT-REACHABLE), the SAME graceful degradation as the missing-addon / non-capable-brain cases — enroll STILL WORKS (the brain opens), it simply carries no reach socket. this is a declared scope note, not a silent hole: the socket is a POSIX-host capability, and a Windows box degrades to enroll-without-reach exactly like any host that cannot stand up a socket
│   │   ├── getOneCloneByRef.ts                           [+] communicator — @:<slug|serial> → clone dir. a SERIAL ref does a FULL SCAN across every actor's clones — and it COMPOSES the shared enumerators (i008-r11-B3: getAllActorsEnrolled + getAllClonesForActor-per-actor, the SAME leaves getAllClonesGroupedByActor + `clone list` walk — NOT an independent re-walk of `actor.via.hash=*/clones/serial=<uuid>`, so the `serial=*` filter and any future filter live in ONE place and the lookup can never disagree with the list about what exists). (i007-r11-N1 corrected: say/get `@:<serial>` supply NO actor scope, so it CANNOT look up "within its actor dir" — the earlier claim was wrong; a serial is a globally-unique uuid, so the first match IS the clone. the scan is fine at the expected actor counts; a global `.clones/<serial>` index symmetric with `.slugs/` is a clean future optimization if scale ever demands, flagged not built). a SLUG ref (no actor in scope) uses the GLOBAL slug index `.agent/.actors/.slugs/<slug>` (blocker-1) — an O(1) symlink to the owning clone dir. a null-slug clone is NEVER in the index, so it is reachable only by serial, which is the nullable-unique semantics made real. the MATCHED clone is returned via getOneCloneHydrated [+] (i017-r11-2), the SAME single hydration seam getAllClonesForActor + getAllClonesGroupedByActor compose — so a serial-lookup Clone and a list Clone are enriched identically, never a per-caller attach that could drift
│   │   ├── getAllClonesForActor.ts                       [+] communicator — read clones of ONE actor (by actorDir) — reuses getAllFilesFromDir [←], NOT raw readdir; enumerates ONLY `serial=*` entries (i007-r11-B3: an explicit serial-prefix filter, so a clone is listed EXACTLY once — belt-and-suspenders now that the `slug=` alias symlink is dropped, and a guard against any future non-serial sibling in `clones/`); SORTS by spawnedAt ASC then serial ASC (i008-r12-3: deterministic clone order within an actor group so the mandatory `clone list` + journey-t6 snapshots do not flake on filesystem readdir order; spawnedAt-asc reads chronologically to match the `since=` column, serial-asc is the stable tiebreak); single-actor, kept thin. EACH clone is built via getOneCloneHydrated [+] (i017-r11-2 — the ONE hydration seam), NOT a hand-assembled attach of getCloneIdentity + a per-caller attach of `actor`/`historyDir`; so this reader never independently remembers the enrichment step. SLUG RECONCILED AT HYDRATION via getOneCloneSlugReconciled [+] (i012-r11-2 fix — the SLUG-AUTHORITY gap, composed INSIDE getOneCloneHydrated): each hydrated Clone's DISPLAY slug is NOT trusted from its own `identity.json.slug` — it is RECONCILED against the global `.slugs/` index, which the blueprint's own crash-consistency note names the SOLE authority for slug lookup. so a clone shows slug=X only if `readlink(.slugs/X)` targets THIS clone's serial; else its display slug is null. this closes the two-representations gap the review named: after a dead-owner REBIND re-points `.slugs/driver` at the FRESH serial, the OLD orphan's identity.json still reads `slug:driver`, so trusting the stored field would show TWO clones claiming `driver` while only one resolves via `@:driver` — reconciliation makes the orphan display NO slug (the index disowns it), so display + lookup agree. this is the SAME "the index is authority, identity.json.slug is a display hint" discipline the getOneCloneByRef slug path already