│   │   ├── setCloneIdentity.ts                           [+] communicator — write identity.json `{ schemaVersion, serial, slug, socketEligible, spawnedAt, hostHash, hostPid, hostPidStartedAt }` ATOMICALLY (i045-r11-2/r12-2: `schemaVersion` = a monotonic integer (`1` for this behavior) written FIRST, off ONE `CLONE_IDENTITY_SCHEMA_VERSION` const in utils/cloneIdentitySchema — the FORWARD-COMPAT stamp that lets getCloneIdentity tell "an OLDER record this build can still read" from "a NEWER record this build must not guess at". WHY it is load-bearing NOW, not speculative: the blueprint's OWN open-decisions ledger signposts the `rhx clone` dream ADDING a REQUIRED `lastActivityAt` field to power the "idle 20m" cron — and under the earlier strict-EXACT-shape read, the instant that field ships REQUIRED, EVERY clone dir baked by THIS build reads as CORRUPT (a hard MalfunctionError crash on the next `clone list`/`get`/`say`, self-inflicted on the very local-dev-upgrade workflow howto.test-local-rhachet describes). the schemaVersion + the tolerant-superset read (getCloneIdentity, below) close that self-inflicted upgrade break: a v1 record stays readable after v2 adds a field, and a v2 record read by a v1 build fails loud with an UPGRADE hint, never a mis-guess) (i026-r12-4: `hostPid` = the pty child's process id captured at bake — the orphan-detection referent for the SIGKILL cost mitigation, ledger row 11; written for a socket-eligible clone only, `null` for a fallback clone that has no pty child) (i006-r12-4: `hostHash` = getHomeHash at bake — recorded for the ERROR-HINT ONLY, NOT for liveness (liveness stays socket-derived per F4). a DEAD clone whose recorded hostHash ≠ the current host's getHomeHash → say/get name the CROSS-HOST cause instead of the wrong same-host "re-enroll" hint; this restores the named-fix guarantee F4's status.json-drop left open, without re-introducing a host-field liveness check) (i006-r12-5 fix: temp-file + rename, the SAME atomic-write discipline writeClaudeCodeSettings already uses — `rhx enroll` is a long-lived interactive command a human may Ctrl-C mid-bake, and a torn identity.json would crash the next `clone list`/`get`/`say` with an unhandled parse error; atomic write means a reader sees a WHOLE file or none, never a half). ATOMIC UNIT = THE WHOLE SERIAL DIR, NOT JUST THE FILE (i018-r07-1 fix — the mkdir-vs-rename TOCTOU the review found: if the `serial=<uuid>/` dir were mkdir'd FIRST and identity.json rename'd into it SECOND, an enumerator that lists `serial=*` in that window finds the dir but reads ENOENT on its identity.json): so the ATOMIC boundary is the ENTIRE clone dir. setCloneIdentity builds the dir under a TEMP name (`.serial=<uuid>.<rand>.tmp/` with identity.json already inside), then `rename(2)`s the whole temp dir → `serial=<uuid>/` (a POSIX-atomic directory rename on one filesystem — the same temp+rename discipline, lifted from the file to the dir). CONSEQUENTLY the `serial=<uuid>/` dir NEVER exists without its identity.json — an enumerator sees a COMPLETE dir or NO dir, never a half-materialized one. this closes the writer-vs-reader race at the SOURCE (rule.require.solve-at-cause), the same way the i011-r11-5 + i017-r11-3 orderings close their races. the socket is bound (LISTENING, in XDG_RUNTIME_DIR — OUTSIDE the clone dir) BEFORE the rename, so `socketEligible=true` inside the renamed identity.json stays truthful the instant the dir appears; history/ symlinks are added post-spawn into the now-present dir (an empty history/ is valid). i005-nitpick-2: identity.json is the on-disk HOME of the eligibility flag — it carries `socketEligible: boolean`, NOT a socket path; the path is always derived fresh from serial, never persisted; i005-r12-B: `spawnedAt` (IsoTimeStamp, via `now()` from iso-time) is captured here — the referent for the `clone list` "since=" column + the `__AGO__` mask); then setCloneSlugIndex for the GLOBAL slug uniqueness (blocker-1). NO per-actor `slug=<slug>` symlink (i007-r11-B3 fix — DROPPED: no op ever READ it — getOneCloneByRef looks up slugs ONLY via the global `.slugs/` index — so its only effect was a THIRD write to keep crash-consistent for zero payoff, AND it risked double-listing every named clone in `clone list` (once as its `serial=` dir, once as the `slug=` alias sibling in the same `clones/` dir). so the slug fact is now TWO representations: `identity.json.slug` + the global `.slugs/` index. CRASH-CONSISTENCY (i006-r11-N3, now simpler): the GLOBAL `.slugs/` index is written LAST — it is the SOLE authority getOneCloneByRef consults for slug lookup, so a crash BEFORE it leaves the slug simply UN-looked-up (a clean, consistent "unknown slug" fail-loud), never a false claim; and because genClone is FINDSERT, a re-`enroll --as @:<slug>` RE-WRITES both representations — so a partial write SELF-HEALS on the next enroll
│   │   ├── getCloneIdentity.ts                            [+] communicator (i015-r12-4 fix — the malformed-record resilience gap): the ONE read+parse seam for `identity.json` (serial dir → the parsed `{schemaVersion,serial,slug,socketEligible,spawnedAt,hostHash,hostPid,hostPidStartedAt}`), composed by EVERY hydrator (getAllClonesForActor, getOneCloneByRef, getAllClonesGroupedByActor). TOLERANT-SUPERSET SHAPE CHECK, KEYED ON schemaVersion (i045-r11-2/r12-2 fix — the self-inflicted-upgrade-break the review named): the guard is NOT a strict EXACT-shape match (which would read every extant record as corrupt the instant the dream adds its signposted required `lastActivityAt`). instead it is a THREE-way version-aware read: (a) a record whose `schemaVersion` is KNOWN-and-current → assert the REQUIRED fields for that version are present + well-typed, and TOLERATE any UNKNOWN/EXTRA fields (they default, never reject — so a record written by a LATER-minor build that added an optional field still reads clean on this build); (b) a record whose `schemaVersion` is ABSENT or OLDER → read it through this version's required-field set, defaulting the fields added since (the forward-compat read of a legacy/older record — a pre-schemaVersion identity.json is treated as v0 and upgraded-on-read); (c) a record whose `schemaVersion` is NEWER than this build knows → FAIL-LOUD with an UPGRADE hint ("this clone record was written by a newer rhachet (schema vN); upgrade rhachet, or remove <path> — it is host-local runtime, safe to delete"), NEVER a guess at a shape this build cannot understand. so the ONLY corruption that fails loud is a genuine one — a `JSON.parse` failure, a missing REQUIRED field for the record's OWN version, or a NEWER-unknown version — and a mere field ADDITION by a future behavior no longer reads every extant clone as corrupt (the exact hard-crash-on-local-upgrade the review flagged). a CORRUPT / foreign identity.json (a `JSON.parse` failure, or a required-field-for-its-version miss — a hand-edited file, or a leftover from an aborted pre-atomic-write bake) → FAIL-LOUD MalfunctionError that NAMES the exact bad file path + the fix ("remove or repair <path>; it is host-local runtime, safe to delete"), NOT a silent skip (rule.forbid.failhide — a swallowed corrupt record would hide a real state fault AND make `clone list` under-report). MalfunctionError (exit 1, server/state-must-fix) because a corrupt on-disk record is a state fault, not a caller input error. one parse seam = one malformed-record policy for the whole read surface (decompose-for-recompose), never re-authored per hydrator. ABSENT vs CORRUPT — TWO DISTINCT OUTCOMES (i018-r07-2 fix — the review's ENOENT gap): an ABSENT identity.json (ENOENT — a serial dir with no record: a mid-bake temp dir that lost its rename, or an externally-torn-down dir) is NOT the same as a corrupt one. getCloneIdentity returns `null` on ENOENT (a typed "NO RECORD here" signal), and FAILS-LOUD MalfunctionError only on a PRESENT-but-unparseable/wrong-shape file (genuine corruption). the two callers handle null cleanly: an ENUMERATOR (getAllClonesForActor) SKIPS a null-record serial dir (the same discipline as its `serial=*` prefix filter — a dir with no record is not-yet-a-clone, not an error to hide), and a DIRECT serial lookup (getOneCloneByRef) treats null as UNKNOWN-SERIAL → its own fail-loud. this is NOT failhide: an absent file is a genuine "no record" (returned as null, a real signal the caller acts on), whereas a corrupt PRESENT file is a state fault (thrown). combined with the atomic-dir-rename in setCloneIdentity (i018-r07-1) the ENOENT window is closed at the source anyway — the null-on-ENOENT branch is defense-in-depth for a tampered/hand-cleared dir, so a raw untyped ENOENT never escapes the seam (rule.require.errors-name-the-fix honored: absent → typed null the caller narrates, corrupt → named MalfunctionError)
│   │   ├── getOneCloneSlugReconciled.ts                          [+] communicator (i012-r11-2 fix) — `{ clone } → string | null`: the DISPLAY-slug authority check. reads the clone's STORED `identity.json.slug`, then `readlink(.agent/.actors/.slugs/<storedSlug>)` and returns the stored slug ONLY IF the index entry targets THIS clone's serial; else null. so the global `.slugs/` index — the SOLE authority the crash-consistency note already names — governs the DISPLAYED slug too, not just lookup. called at hydration by getAllClonesForActor (+ getOneCloneByRef), so an ORPHAN whose identity.json still reads `slug:driver` after a rebind re-pointed `.slugs/driver` at the fresh serial displays NO slug — display + lookup can never disagree. O(1) per clone (one readlink of the stored slug); a null stored slug → null (no index entry, unreachable by slug); a `.slugs/<slug>` that is absent (crash before the LAST write) → null (the clean "un-owned" display, matching the un-looked-up fail-loud on the lookup side)
│   │   ├── getOneCloneHydrated.ts                                 [+] communicator (i017-r11-2 fix — the SINGLE hydration seam, the enrichment twin of getCloneIdentity's parse seam) — `{ repoPath, actorDir, serial } → Clone`: the ONE place a raw on-disk clone record becomes a whole `Clone` domain object. it COMPOSES getCloneIdentity (read+parse the persisted `{serial,slug,socketEligible,spawnedAt,hostHash,hostPid,hostPidStartedAt}`) + getOneCloneSlugReconciled (the display-slug authority check) and ATTACHES the two DERIVED-NOT-PERSISTED fields the record does not carry: `actor` (a `RefByPrimary<ActorEnrolled>` built from the FULL {repoPath, hash} COMPOSITE key — the `hash` off the parent `actor.via.hash=<hash>` dir segment of actorDir, the `repoPath` threaded in EXPLICITLY as an input (i039-r11-B1), so the ref MATCHES ActorEnrolled's composite primary and a hash-only ref can never re-open the cross-repo collision i037-r12-1 closed — the hydrator is the ONE place the ref is built, so the composite key belongs HERE) + `historyDir` (getCloneDir + '/history'). EVERY reader composes THIS — getAllClonesForActor (per serial), getOneCloneByRef (the matched serial), getAllClonesGroupedByActor (via getAllClonesForActor) — so the enrichment step has ONE owner, never re-authored per caller. this closes the exact defect class the blueprint extracts getCloneIdentity, computeCloneReachState, computeCloneSlugDecision, and computeCloneUnreachableHint for — "one decision re-derived per caller, eventually drifts" (the vision's "one decision, two places" recurrence): before this, each reader independently remembered to attach BOTH `actor` and `historyDir` after getCloneIdentity, so a future hydrator (the dream's `wake`/`fork`) was one omitted attach-step from a Clone with an undefined `actor` ref — SILENTLY, since no type-checker guards a field populated by convention rather than construction. with getOneCloneHydrated the ONLY way to make a Clone from disk, the type system guarantees `actor`+`historyDir` are always present (the object is never hand-assembled). decompose-for-recompose: the read seam (getCloneIdentity) parses, the hydration seam (getOneCloneHydrated) enriches, and no reader owns either policy
│   │   ├── setCloneSlugIndex.ts                           [+] communicator (blocker-1 fix) — write the GLOBAL slug index `.agent/.actors/.slugs/<slug> -> ../actor.via.hash=…/clones/serial=…` via an ATOMIC EXCLUSIVE symlink (i017-r11-1: the CONCURRENT-BAKE winner-election primitive). `symlink(2)` FAILS with EEXIST if the path already exists — it is atomic + lock-free, so it IS the serialization point for two bakes that race: the FIRST to create `.slugs/<slug>` WINS, the second gets EEXIST and re-reads the owner to classify. this closes the same-actor bake-vs-bake TOCTOU the review found (two concurrent `enroll --as @:driver` of the SAME actor both see "no owner" in genClone's find-first, both fall through to bake-fresh, both reach here): the loser's EEXIST is NOT last-write-wins — it triggers the loser-reap in genClone (below). FAIL-LOUD ONLY on a DIFFERENT-actor collision (an EEXIST whose owner is a DIFFERENT actor → ConstraintError that names the owner actor + an IN-SCOPE fix, never a silent overwrite). the fix text NAMES A REAL, IN-SCOPE ACTION (i010-r12-A: the earlier "+ fix" could read as a deferred rebind op — corrected): `"the name @:driver is held by a clone of a different actor (roles=<owner roles>). pick a different --as name for this enroll (e.g. --as @:driver-2)"` — because slug uniqueness is GLOBAL-cross-actor by design, and a cross-actor slug reassignment (the "tweak my roles, re-enroll under the SAME handle" workflow) requires the `rhx clone` dream's slug-reassign lifecycle. so the in-scope fix is ALWAYS "pick a new name," a real action available today, NOT a pointer at an unbuilt op. the role-tweak-same-handle dead-end is now named in the edge-case table (i010-r12-A), not left implicit. a SAME-actor re-`--as` is the findsert reuse-or-rebind that genClone's find-first handles (i004 blocker-1), never a collision. this makes `unique=['slug']` a real cross-actor invariant while keeping `--as` genuinely idempotent — the flagship case=4 "a cron hardcodes @:driver" promise has one unambiguous owner AND converges on re-enroll. skipped for a null slug (no index entry). NOTE — slug-RELEASE-on-death is a SCOPED DEFERRAL (nitpick-3 i004): a dead owner's slug is re-bindable by the SAME actor (find-first sees DEAD → re-point), but a cross-actor takeover of a dead slug, and an explicit `del`-slug release, belong to the `rhx clone` dream's lifecycle (the same deferral shape as `delClone`), flagged so a reviewer reads the absent release path as scoped, not omitted
│   │   ├── genCloneHistoryLink.ts                            [+] communicator (FINDSERT — idempotent) — discover the brain's newest UNCLAIMED transcript for this cwd (NOT parse the pty stream), CLAIM it atomically, symlink history/<exid>.jsonl → it (via symlinkFile [←]). CONCURRENT-CLAIM ELECTION (i021-r07-1 fix — the same-cwd history-link race, now closed at SOURCE, no longer a playtest deferral): two clones spawned in the SAME cwd within one filesystem-timestamp tick could BOTH discover the same candidate `<exid>.jsonl` by mtime + spawn-window and BOTH link it, so each would reference the other's transcript — the exact double-link the review names. the ROOT-CAUSE fix is a GLOBAL exid-claim index `.agent/.actors/.exids/<exid> -> owner serial`, SYMMETRIC with the `.slugs/` index and written via the SAME atomic EXCLUSIVE symlink primitive (symlink(2), which FAILS with EEXIST if the path exists — atomic + lock-free, the proven concurrent-bake winner-election mechanism i017-r11-1 uses for slugs). so genCloneHistoryLink, per discovered candidate, attempts the atomic `.exids/<exid>` claim FIRST: on SUCCESS this clone OWNS the exid → create the `history/<exid>.jsonl` link; on EEXIST a PEER clone already owns it → SKIP that candidate and consider the next-newest. because a brain writes ONE new transcript per spawn, N concurrent clones produce N DISTINCT files, and the atomic claim SERIALIZES the election — so no two clones EVER link the SAME exid. the double-link is now IMPOSSIBLE by construction (rule.require.solve-at-cause), not a mtime-heuristic guess that admits a residual race. the `.exids/` index (like `.slugs/`) is host-local runtime under the gitignored `.agent/.actors/` tree, and getAllActorsEnrolled' `actor.via.hash=*` filter already excludes it from actor enumeration. RESIDUAL — TWO CLASSES, one benign, one a wisher-flagged cross-actor content-bleed (i059-r11-1 fix — the arch-defects lens rightly caught that the earlier "never crosses an actor/identity boundary" claim was FALSE for the different-actor-same-brain-same-cwd case): the atomic claim guarantees each concurrent clone links a DISTINCT exid, but NOT WHICH clone links WHICH transcript (claim-first-wins), and the per-brain gate prevents only a CROSS-BRAIN mislink — it does NOT scope by actor. so the residual splits into two classes: (1) SAME-actor, same cwd, same tick — a swapped attribution between two SAME-`{brain,roles}` sessions, functionally interchangeable, never a double-link → BENIGN (as before). (2) DIFFERENT-actor-SAME-brain, same cwd, same tick — two clones of two DISTINCT actors (e.g. a `-driver`/`-foreman` derived-actor pair co-enrolled in ONE working dir, which no rule enforces to be separate worktrees) both run the IDENTICAL claude-transcript discovery over the IDENTICAL `~/.claude/projects/<cwd-slug>/`, and the `.exids/` claim carries NO actor dimension, so actor A's clone CAN claim actor B's transcript → a `rhx clone get @:A` could surface @:B's conversation content — a real CROSS-ACTOR CONTENT-BLEED, NOT benign. the earlier prose OVERCLAIMED cross-actor safety and is corrected here. the CONTENT-BLEED itself is now CLOSED AT SOURCE by an AMBIGUOUS-REFUSE guard (i059-r12-1, the behavior-intent lens's "ambiguous → refuse, don't guess" recommendation — the SAME rule.forbid.failhide discipline the rest of the blueprint applies): genCloneHistoryLink links ONLY when EXACTLY ONE unclaimed candidate transcript sits in this clone's spawn-discovery window; when TWO-OR-MORE unclaimed candidates are visible (the ONLY condition under which a cross-actor swap is possible), the clone CANNOT uniquely identify its own, so it REFUSES to link — leaves `history/` EMPTY for that episode and logs the ambiguity — rather than claim-first-wins a possibly-WRONG-actor transcript. so a `rhx clone get @:A` in that race returns EMPTY, NEVER @:B's content: the wrong-actor link is impossible by construction, the privacy leak closed. the COST is completeness, never safety — and the ambiguity does NOT self-clear (i061-r12-1 fix — the earlier "clears to a single candidate, links normally via the re-link trigger" claim was FALSE, the deadlock the behavior-intent lens caught): the discovery window (`transcriptMtime >= spawnedAt`) only GROWS and a REFUSAL never CLAIMS, so two co-located different-actor clones that first collide stay ambiguous on EVERY later poll (neither unclaimed set ever shrinks to one — a mutual-refusal deadlock, not a transient race), so `history/` stays EMPTY for the co-located pair until they are placed in SEPARATE cwds — NOT merely for the first episode. this is now an HONEST, SIGNALED, DOCUMENTED limitation of the same-cwd-different-actor topology, never a silent forever-empty: (a) SIGNALED — the refuse records the ambiguous exids into an `exidsAmbiguous` list (parallel to the vanished-symlink `exidsUnreadable`, i017-r12-2) threaded through getCloneOutput → invokeCloneGet's stderr WARN (`⚠ get: history unavailable — N episode(s) ambiguous — this clone shares a cwd with another actor's clone, so rhachet cannot attribute the transcript safely; enroll co-located actors in SEPARATE worktrees`), so a `get` on an affected clone names WHY it is empty + the fix, never a silent empty (rule.forbid.failhide — the SAME surfaced-signal discipline the vanished-symlink case already carries; the getCloneOutput return widens to `{ lines, exidsUnreadable, exidsAmbiguous, total, truncated }`); (b) DOCUMENTED — the resolution is one-worktree-per-actor (the worktree-crew inventory case's OWN assumption), and PERFECT attribution (each clone its OWN history in one shared cwd) needs the deferred per-brain session-id (topic-2 unconfirmed), FLAGGED wisher-visible (ledger row 1(i061)). the SINGLE-candidate common case (the vast majority — a lone clone, or clones in separate cwds) links EXACTLY as before, no behavior change. what REMAINS deferred is ONLY the PERFECT attribution — getting clone A its OWN history in that rare race rather than an empty one — which needs a caller-assigned deterministic session-id at spawn (a per-brain `--session-id` capability research did NOT confirm, topic-2), FLAGGED wisher-visible (ledger row 1(i059) + a council fulcrum) as an attribution-COMPLETENESS enhancement — no longer a privacy fix, because the privacy fix is DONE. the interim ergonomic mitigation for the completeness gap: co-enroll different actors in SEPARATE working dirs (the worktree-crew inventory case's own assumption — one worktree per actor). the test-coverage gap the lenses named is CLOSED: genCloneHistoryLink.integration gains a CROSS-ACTOR-SAME-CWD case — two different-actor clones race in one cwd with two candidate transcripts → assert NEITHER clone links the OTHER's transcript (each `history/` is either its own correct link OR empty, NEVER the wrong actor's exid), dogfooded by a variant that reverts the ambiguous-refuse guard → a wrong-actor link appears + the assertion goes red; AND (i061-r12-1) assert the deadlock is SIGNALED not silent — the refuse records the ambiguous exid into `exidsAmbiguous`, so a `get` on an affected clone surfaces the stderr WARN naming the shared-cwd cause + the separate-worktree fix, NEVER a silent empty. PER-BRAIN, GATED (i011-r11-3 fix — the observe-side twin of the input gate): the transcript-discovery path is BRAIN-SPECIFIC (claude → `~/.claude/projects/<cwd-slug>/`), so it is SELECTED off the SAME getSupportedBrainCommand `.socket` capability map that gates say+get (i009-r11-2), NOT invoked brain-blind. a brain with NO observe adapter → genCloneHistoryLink is a NO-OP (the clone simply has an empty `history/`, consistent with the `.socket`-gated talk contract), never a silent claude-path probe. this closes TWO hazards the earlier brain-blind invocation carried: (a) a non-claude clone silently getting no history is now the DECLARED per-brain-capability outcome, not an accident; (b) MORE IMPORTANT — in a mixed-brain repo, a non-claude clone in the same cwd could have mis-linked a CLAUDE clone's `<exid>.jsonl` (the spawn-window+mtime discrimination never considered WHICH brain wrote the file), a cross-brain misattribution; the per-brain gate means only a claude clone runs claude's discovery, so a non-claude clone never reaches claude's projects dir. a second socket-capable brain gets its OWN discovery adapter off the same map — the SAME reactive-lift seam as asCloneOutputText (claude is the sole socket-capable brain shipping, so a second discovery adapter is a speculative build, flagged not built). BOTH-BRANCH INITIAL LINK (i007-r12-1 fix): the FIRST link runs after EITHER spawn branch — the pty branch AND the plain-spawn FALLBACK branch — because a plain-spawn claude writes its OWN `<exid>.jsonl` to `~/.claude/projects/**` regardless of the pty; without this a `get` on a fallback clone returns no output, contradicting the tested say/get-asymmetry edge case (get reads a non-socket clone's history). RE-LINK TRIGGER (i007-r11-B4: a clone's series accretes multiple episodes over its life — a NEW transcript appears at each COMPACTION/episode boundary WHILE the clone is alive): the pty branch re-invokes it ONCE PER EPISODE BOUNDARY thereafter (genBrainCliPtyClone polls the projects dir for this cwd, findsert so a re-link of an already-linked exid is a no-op); the FALLBACK branch links only the first episode (its later-episode links are a flagged fallback-path gap — a fallback clone is already a degraded, non-managed path). so `history/` holds ONE symlink per episode of the whole series for a managed clone, at least the first for a fallback clone — otherwise getCloneOutput would miss every post-compaction episode. HERMETIC PROJECTS-DIR SEAM (i047-r11-1 fix — the host-pollution hazard the fresh holistic pass found, a genuine new structural defect the prior 46 rounds missed): the transcript-discovery scans claude-code's REAL, HOST-GLOBAL `~/.claude/projects/<cwd-slug>/`, and the stub brain (genStubBrainLive) MUST write there for the brain-keyed discovery to find it — so EVERY acceptance/integration/journey test, each in a randomly-suffixed hermetic temp-repo cwd, mints a PERMANENT, never-reused dir under the developer's OWN `~/.claude/projects/` with NO teardown. this violates rule.forbid.bare-host-deps + rule.require.hermetic-tests (the repo's own mechanic briefs), is unbounded growth OUTSIDE the `.agent/.actors/` tree the ledger's 6-member prune family tracks, and is an ergonomic hazard for any contributor who runs this repo's OWN suite locally (howto.test-local-rhachet): their daily-driver `~/.claude/projects/` state silently bloats with fake test sessions on every `npm run build && rhx git.repo.test`. FIX (decompose-for-recompose, mirrors the extant getCloneSocketPath XDG extraction): the `~/.claude/projects` path resolution — currently IMPLICIT inside genCloneHistoryLink — is EXTRACTED into ONE named injectable communicator `getClaudeCliProjectsDir({}, context)` [+] (infra/host/) that reads `process.env.CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR ?? '~/.claude'` + '/projects', so PROD uses the default host path and TEST infra OVERRIDES it (via a `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` env pointed at the per-test `genTempDir`, the SAME isolation genTempDir already gives every other host path). genCloneHistoryLink composes this seam instead of a hardcoded path; genStubBrainLive writes to the SAME overridden dir, so discovery still finds it — but INSIDE the temp tree, torn down with the rest of the hermetic repo. the leak is closed AT THE SOURCE (rule.require.solve-at-cause), one seam reused by prod (default) + test (override), not a cleanup-after-the-fact. NOTE `getCloneOutput`'s symlink-FOLLOW needs no seam — only the DISCOVERY step here resolves the projects dir; the symlinks it plants already point into that (now-overridable) location
│   │   ├── isCloneLive.ts                                [+] communicator — socket connectable? the boolean reader — composes connectToClone [←] (the shared adapter, nitpick-4), which DERIVES the path fresh from serial (i005-nitpick-2 — no stored path); returns connectable?
│   │   ├── computeCloneReachState.ts                     [+] transformer (PURE — i007-r11-B1 fix, was mislabeled) — `{ socketEligible: boolean, live: boolean } → CloneReachState`: `socketEligible === false → 'NOT-REACHABLE'`, `socketEligible + live → 'LIVE'`, `socketEligible + !live → 'DEAD'`. GENUINELY pure + unit-testable (data-driven tri-state table), because the socket-I/O `live` boolean is an INJECTED INPUT, not computed inside — this is the same pure/impure split the blueprint uses for asCloneRef(pure)/getOneCloneByRef(disk) and asActorRef(pure)/getOneActorEnrolledByRef(disk). EXPORTS the shared tri-state type (i012-r11-3 fix): `export type CloneReachState = 'LIVE' | 'DEAD' | 'NOT-REACHABLE'` is declared ONCE HERE and IMPORTED by every consumer — computeCloneUnreachableHint's `reachState` input, getCloneReachState's return, the `clone list` tree renderer, and the `--output json` shape — so the vocabulary has ONE owner. previously the literal union was inlined in this signature AND independently re-typed by three consumers, a compiler-invisible spelling-drift risk (`NOT-REACHABLE` vs `NOT_REACHABLE`); one exported type makes a drift a type error. this is the SAME decompose-for-recompose discipline the blueprint applies to the Clone/ActorEnrolled objects — a shared vocabulary owns its type
│   │   ├── getCloneReachState.ts                         [+] communicator (i007-r11-B1 — the IMPURE caller, was wrongly the whole thing) — `clone → CloneReachState` (the type IMPORTED from computeCloneReachState.ts, i012-r11-3, never re-declared): derives the fresh socket path from serial (getCloneSocketPath), calls isCloneLive (the socket I/O), then computeCloneReachState({ socketEligible: clone.socketEligible, live }). the path is recomputed from serial here, never read from the record — so a path-algorithm change can never strand an eligible clone as a false-DEAD. this is what `clone list` renders (a fallback clone reads NOT-REACHABLE, a crashed clone reads DEAD — distinct, per the snapshot + matrix)
