│   │   ├── computeCloneSlugDecision.ts                   [+] transformer (i008-r11-N1) — the PURE reuse/rebind/collision classifier: `{ owner: Clone|null, ownerLive: boolean, actorHashCurrent } → 'reuse' | 'rebind' | 'collision' | 'bake-fresh'`. same-actor + live owner → reuse; same-actor + dead owner → rebind; different-actor owner → collision (fail-loud upstream); no owner → bake-fresh. EXTRACTS genClone's find-first decision out of the orchestrator so genClone reads as narrative (rule.require.orchestrators-as-narrative — the 3-way branch was inline decode-friction), AND the dream's `make`/`fork`/`wake` derive-owner flow composes this ONE classifier instead of re-deriving the rule (decompose-for-recompose). pure + unit-testable (data-driven rows over the four outcomes). DANGLING-CLAIM SELF-HEAL — a SAME-PROCESS crash, not only a race (i048-r11-2 fix — the review's fresh moderate finding, distinct from the concurrent-bake race i017-r11-1/i030-r07-1 already close): `setCloneSlugIndex`'s atomic `.slugs/<slug>` claim and `genClone`'s subsequent temp-dir→`serial=<uuid>/` rename are two separate syscalls in sequence, so if the SAME process dies BETWEEN them (Ctrl-C mid-spawn, OOM-kill, laptop sleep — an ordinary interrupted enroll, no peer race), `.slugs/<slug>` is left as a link to a serial whose dir was NEVER published. this classifier already routes that case to SELF-HEAL, and the routing is now EXPLICIT: getOneCloneByRef(slug) resolves `.slugs/<slug>` → the serial, then getCloneIdentity reads that serial's identity.json → ENOENT → null (the i018-r07-2 absent-vs-corrupt seam), so getOneCloneByRef returns `owner: null` for a dangling claim — IDENTICAL to a never-claimed slug. so a dangling-claim slug lands in the `no owner → bake-fresh` bucket, and an ordinary re-`enroll --as @:<slug>` bakes a fresh serial + RE-POINTS the claim (setCloneSlugIndex re-writes the link atomically) — the stuck claim self-heals via re-enroll, NEVER a permanent brick that needs a manual `.s