│   │   ├── genClone.ts                                   [+] orchestrator — findsert clone dir + identity + socket + spawn; the find-first step calls getOneCloneByRef(slug) → computeCloneSlugDecision (i008-r11-N1, the extracted classifier) → narrates reuse/rebind/collision/bake-fresh; unlinks a stale orphan socket via delFileSync [←] (the startKeyrackDaemon unlink-own-files pattern). CONCURRENT-BAKE LOSER-REAP (i017-r11-1 fix — the same-actor bake-vs-bake race): the find-first `computeCloneSlugDecision` is a TOCTOU read (two concurrent same-actor `--as @:driver` bakes can BOTH classify bake-fresh), so the AUTHORITATIVE election is the atomic exclusive `.slugs/<slug>` symlink in setCloneSlugIndex, not the find-first read. genClone bakes its fresh serial into a TEMP-named dir + binds its socket, THEN attempts the atomic `.slugs/<slug>` claim (setCloneSlugIndex) BEFORE the final serial-dir rename (i018-r07-1): on SUCCESS it is the winner → it renames its temp dir to the published `serial=<uuid>/` (done); on EEXIST-same-actor it is the LOSER of a concurrent same-actor race → it REAPS its own just-baked clone via delCloneSpawn (i042-r11-3, the extracted sequence): FIRST it KILLS its pty child via pty.kill() (the brain process genBrainCliPtyClone already spawned + RETURNS a handle to, precisely so the reap can terminate it; i033-r07-1 fix — WITHOUT this kill the loser would abandon a live ORPHAN BRAIN that still draws credits with NO clone record left to detect it, since its dir is removed in the same reap — the exact hidden-side-effect + unpredictable-resource-drain hazard rule.forbid.behavior-hazards forbids), THEN it unlinks its socket via delFileSync + removes its STILL-TEMP-named dir (which the `serial=*` enumerator NEVER matched) — and REUSES the winner the index now names (re-reads `.slugs/<slug>` → the winner's serial → returns that live clone), so `--as @:driver` still converges to ONE reachable handle; on EEXIST-different-actor → the cross-actor collision fail-loud. so the loser leaves NO orphan of ANY kind — NEITHER a live-but-unreachable clone record NOR a live orphan brain process: the pty child, the socket, and the temp dir are ALL reaped in ONE sequence (kill-child → unlink-socket → remove-dir), the review's exact hazard closed. the socket bind + pty spawn BEFORE the index attempt is safe: a reaped loser's child is killed and its socket unlinked in the same reap, so neither an orphan process nor an orphan socket survives. TRANSIENT-GHOST ELIMINATION via STRUCTURAL INVISIBILITY (i030-r07-1 fix — ADOPTED as the DEFAULT, was the flagged clean-rework the review escalated nitpick→blocker under rule.forbid.behavior-hazards, deterministic output): the `.slugs/<slug>` atomic claim runs BEFORE the final serial-dir rename, so a LOSER reaps its STILL-TEMP-named dir — which the `serial=*` enumerator NEVER matches — and is therefore NEVER enumerable. so there is NO transient ghost row: a concurrent `clone list` can only ever see the WINNER (a published `serial=<uuid>/`) or no row at all, so its output is DETERMINISTIC — the same inputs yield the same rows, never a sometimes-there-sometimes-gone LIVE slug=null row. this REPLACES the earlier order (bake+rename, THEN claim) whose loser was briefly published+enumerable before its reap; the reviewer is RIGHT that a short-lived unreachable LIVE row violates deterministic-output, so the structural fix is now TAKEN, not deferred. the ONE consequence: this INVERTS the i017-r11-3 "slug-resolvable ⟹ enumerable-by-serial" invariant into its truthful reverse — a `.slugs/<slug>` claim can exist for a micro-window BEFORE its winner's serial dir is published, so a `say @:<slug>` that races the winner's OWN in-flight bake in that instant fails-loud "unknown" and SELF-HEALS on retry once the rename lands. this reverse is BENIGN and strictly better than the ghost it erases: an absent-serial-for-a-claimed-slug is a TRUE negative (the handle genuinely is not yet reachable by serial), never a published lie, and it is unobservable except to a caller that races enroll's own in-flight bake — whereas the ghost row was observable to ANY concurrent `clone list`. so the trade is a non-deterministic enumerable ghost (bad) → a deterministic enumeration + a benign self-heal say-lag on a self-race (good)
