│   │   │   ├── genBrainCliPtyClone.ts                    [+] communicator — node-pty spawn + human mirror + socket + exid capture; the managed-pty spawn genClone runs when a clone is socket-eligible. PTY-CHILD LIFECYCLE — EXIT + SIGNAL + TTY-RESTORE (i025-r11-1 blocker / i025-r12-1 blocker, the gap BOTH l3 lenses converged on: the wrapper `rhx enroll` process lifecycle around the pty child was never specified, and it directly threatens uc1's "invisible, exactly as today" promise — the plain-spawn fallback's `stdio:'inherit'` gets exit-code forward + signal delivery + cooked-mode FREE from the OS; the managed pty must do all three DELIBERATELY). the three named steps, each with a test: (1) EXIT + EXIT-CODE PARITY — a `pty.onExit(code)` handler is the teardown seam: it FIRST DRAINS the write-queue (step 1a, below), then CLOSES the socket server (`net.Server.close()`, so the event loop no longer stays alive on a listening socket — without this the Node process hosting `rhx enroll` would never return control to the shell, the exact uc1 "return-to-shell" break the byte-fidelity test cannot catch), UNLINKS the socket file via delFileSync [←] (no orphan socket left in `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`), restores the tty (step 3), THEN `process.exit(code)` with the CHILD'S OWN exit code — so `rhx enroll` exits with the brain's code exactly as the plain-spawn fallback does today (exit-code parity). (1a) WRITE-QUEUE DRAIN ON EXIT (i050-r11-2/r12-2 fix — the pty-death-vs-in-flight-dispatch gap BOTH l3 lenses named): `net.Server.close()` stops NEW connections but does NOT touch already-open ones, so a `say` sitting `queued`-but-not-`delivered` in genCloneWriteQueue when the brain exits would get NO signal at all — it would burn its WHOLE WEDGED-bound timeout (sayClone) before it learned the truth. WORSE, under the deliberately-unbounded queue (a same-user cron that floods `say`), N held connections each expire their OWN WEDGED timer independently — a "thundering timeout" where one exit event should have fanned out N immediate rejections (the resource hazard r11 named, distinct from the mere UX delay). so pty.onExit drains genCloneWriteQueue BEFORE it closes the server: it walks every PENDING entry (both the in-flight head and every still-`queued` tail entry, each of which already got its `queued` ack) and writes back an `asCloneDispatchAckFrame({ phase: 'rejected', reason })` naming the exit cause, then closes each connection — so all N callers get an IMMEDIATE `rejected` on the ONE exit event, never N redundant WEDGED timeouts. sayClone already handles a `rejected` phase (fail-loud with the reason), so this reuses the extant two-phase wire, no new client path. the drain is genCloneWriteQueue's own method (it owns the pending set), invoked by the pty.onExit seam — so the queue's single-writer discipline and its drain live in one owner. SHARED "CAN'T-REACH-IT" HINT VOCABULARY, NOW EXTRACTED (i050-r11-2 corollary — the THIRD instance the ledger anticipated): this drain needs its own advisory text ("the clone's brain exited while your message was queued"), and the ledger (row: computeCloneUnreachableHint wording-consistency, i045-r11-5) already held computeCloneUnreachableHint's "can't reach it" advisory + sayClone's wedged-hint WET-by-design pending a THIRD instance to trigger extraction. this drain-on-exit hint IS that third instance, so per rule.prefer.wet-over-dry's rule-of-three the three collapse NOW into a shared `computeCloneUnreachableHint` vocabulary — the drain `rejected` reason, computeCloneUnreachableHint's DEAD/NOT-REACHABLE text, and sayClone's wedged-timeout MalfunctionError hint all draw from ONE hint owner (a `CloneUnreachableCause` union `'dead-same-host' | 'dead-cross-host' | 'not-reachable' | 'exited-mid-dispatch' | 'wedged'` → `{ message, hint }`), so the five "can't reach it" phrasings stay consistent + the dream's `wake` flips them all in one place. computeCloneUnreachableHint's input widens to the union; asCloneDispatchAckFrame's drain reason + sayClone's wedged hint compose it instead of inlining a fourth/fifth ad-hoc string. (2) SIGNAL FORWARDING — the wrapper installs `process.on('SIGINT')` + `process.on('SIGTERM')` handlers that FORWARD the signal to the pty child (`pty.kill(signal)`), so Ctrl-C reaches the brain (uc1's most common interrupt gesture — without forwarding, a `SIGINT` would hit the wrapper and NOT the brain, breaking "zero added friction") AND the child dies WITH the wrapper on a catchable term signal (no orphan). (3) TTY RESTORATION — the classic node-pty footgun: the wrapper puts the human's stdin in raw mode to pass keystrokes through, so if it exits WITHOUT restoration of cooked mode the human's shell is left broken after `rhx enroll`. a cleanup that restores `process.stdin` to its prior mode (via the saved `isRaw` + `setRawMode(false)`) is registered on the exit path AND the signal handlers AND a `process.on('exit')` last-resort, so a normal exit, a catchable signal, or an uncaught exception all restore the terminal. RESIDUAL, NAMED (i025-r11-nitpick + i025-r12): a `SIGKILL` (`kill -9`) on the wrapper CANNOT be caught, so a -9'd wrapper can still leave the tty in raw mode AND orphan the pty child (its socket dies with the wrapper, so `clone list` shows it DEAD though the brain neither crashed nor exited) — this is an inherent OS limit shared with every pty wrapper (tmux, screen, and the like), flagged in the open-decisions ledger (row 9) + reconciled with the tri-state legend (a -9-orphaned brain IS unreachable, so DEAD-by-unreachability is the honest classification; the legend note admits "DEAD = crashed, exited, OR unreachable via a dead socket"). RETURNS THE PTY HANDLE TO genClone (i033-r07-1): the spawn yields its pty child handle back to the genClone orchestrator, so the concurrent-bake loser-reap can pty.kill() the very child it just spawned — the SAME pty.kill() the signal-forwarding path already uses, now ALSO reachable on the reap path, so a loser-reap terminates its brain rather than abandon a live orphan that draws credits with no clone record to detect it. SELF-IDENTIFICATION ENV (i040-r12-1 fix — the self-management gap the review named, wish-motivated): the spawned brain's ENV carries `RHACHET_CLONE_SERIAL=<serial>` (and, for a socket-eligible clone, `RHACHET_CLONE_SOCKET=<path>`), injected into the pty child's environment at spawn. WHY: the wish LEADS with "a clone can enumerate + message its peer clones… instead of full-terminal control", and self-dispatch (a clone `say`s its own serial) is DECIDED allowed — but without its own serial IN ITS ENV, a self-managing clone had NO way to learn its OWN address short of `clone list` + fragile pid/cwd matching, and a human whose F7 stderr hint scrolled off had no recall path either. so the serial is now in the child's env, and `rhx clone whoami` (below) reads it back — a clone (or a human inside one) learns its own address in one read, never a heuristic. the enrollBrainCli plain-spawn fallback sets the SAME env var (a fallback clone still has a serial, reachable by `whoami` + `get` even though not by `say`). ENV-INHERITANCE WIDTH, ACKNOWLEDGED (i046-r11-nit — the env-var is inherited by EVERY subprocess the brain spawns, not just the brain itself, a wider surface than the socket's own same-user threat model): `RHACHET_CLONE_SOCKET` names a LOCAL IPC path, so a brain tool-call that echoes its `env` into a log or a comms message could leak that path. this is bounded + acceptable, NOT silently ignored: (a) the socket already enforces isCallerSameUser (i005-blocker-1), so a leaked path is unusable by any OTHER user — the path is not a secret, it is a same-user-only handle; (b) self-dispatch is DECIDED-allowed + unguarded by design, so a subprocess that reaches the socket is in-scope, not an escalation. so the width is a one-line NAMED acknowledgment, not a new guard: the leak surface is a same-user local path behind a same-user gate. a tighter scope (inject only into the brain, strip from grandchildren) is a flagged clean-rework if a future threat model narrows past same-user, NOT built now
