│   │       ├── asCloneDispatchFrame.ts                   [+] transformer — message → dispatch frame (bracketed-paste + submit bytes; client-side frame). IMPORTS `CLONE_DISPATCH_FRAME` from utils/cloneDispatchFrame (i045-r11-1/r12-1): it WRAPS the body as `pasteStart + body + pasteEnd + submit` off the shared const, NEVER a bare `'\e[201~'` literal — so the write side of the wire protocol reads the SAME sentinel the reassemble + forgery-guard sides read. name is DISPATCH-shaped, not mechanism-shaped (nitpick-7 fix): the bracketed-paste framing is today's pty transport, but the domain concept is "frame a message for dispatch" — so a future non-pty transport under isBrainSocketCapable's per-brain gate reuses the name without a misdescribe (rule.require.ubiqlang)
│   │       ├── isSafeCloneDispatchInput.ts               [+] transformer — the SERVER-side content gate; the safety contract. dispatch-shaped name for the same reason. IMPORTS `CLONE_DISPATCH_FRAME` from utils/cloneDispatchFrame (i045-r11-1/r12-1): the forbidden-mid-body terminator it rejects is `CLONE_DISPATCH_FRAME.pasteEnd`, off the shared const — so a terminator change in the frame protocol re-tightens the forgery-guard automatically, never a stale `'\e[201~'` literal that a write-side edit could out-run. CLASS-SCOPED REJECT, NOT A BLANKET ESCAPE BAN (i042-r12-A fix — the comms-relay false-positive the review named + first raised at i035, then dropped): the wish's HEADLINE comms usecase is "a slack reply becomes a message… piped via `say --what @stdin`", and relayed text (CI log tails, terminal captures, colorized diff output) COMMONLY carries harmless ANSI SGR COLOR codes (`\e[31m`, `\e[0m`, …) — so a blanket "reject any escape sequence" would SILENTLY break exactly the usecase the socket exists to serve. so the gate is CLASS-SCOPED: it ALLOWS SGR color/style sequences (`CSI … m` — the display-only class, no terminal-state effect a paste can weaponize), and REJECTS only the DANGEROUS classes — cursor-movement / screen-manipulation CSI (`H`, `J`, `K`, cursor addressing), OSC sequences (`\e]…` — window-title / clipboard / hyperlink injection), and the bracketed-paste TERMINATOR (`\e[201~`, the frame-boundary forgery that could split one dispatch into two). the boundary is "does the sequence alter terminal STATE or forge a frame boundary?" — color does not (pass), cursor/OSC/paste-terminator do (reject). tests assert BOTH sides: a legitimate SGR-colored relay payload PASSES (the positive case the earlier spec lacked), a cursor/OSC/paste-terminator payload is REJECTED — so the intended boundary is proven, not just "plain passes, raw-escape fails" which conflated color with control
│   │       ├── asCloneDispatchFrameSplit.ts              [+] transformer (i039-r07-1 fix — the SOCK_STREAM frame-reassembly seam the review named) — PURE: `{ buffered: string, chunk: Buffer } → { frames: string[], rest: string }`. IMPORTS `CLONE_DISPATCH_FRAME` from utils/cloneDispatchFrame (i045-r11-1/r12-1): it splits on `CLONE_DISPATCH_FRAME.pasteEnd + CLONE_DISPATCH_FRAME.submit` off the shared const, so the reassemble side reads the EXACT terminator the write side (asCloneDispatchFrame) emits — a frame-format change is one const edit, never a split-on-a-stale-literal desync. a unix domain socket is a BYTE STREAM (SOCK_STREAM) with NO message boundaries, so a large bracketed-paste `say` payload can arrive SPLIT across multiple `data` events — and equally, two back-to-back small dispatches can arrive COALESCED in one event. genCloneSocketServer holds a per-connection `buffered` tail and feeds EACH raw `data` chunk through this transformer, which appends the chunk to the buffer, splits on the bracketed-paste END-OF-FRAME sentinel (`CLONE_DISPATCH_FRAME.pasteEnd` + the submit `CLONE_DISPATCH_FRAME.submit`), and returns the list of COMPLETE frames plus the still-incomplete `rest` (carried to the next chunk). ONLY a complete frame reaches isSafeCloneDispatchInput → the write-queue → pty.write. this closes the review's hazard at the SOURCE (rule.require.solve-at-cause): a complete-but-split frame is REASSEMBLED into one whole frame (never mis-read as torn/corruption by asCloneDispatchAck's torn-frame guard, never a partial handed to the content gate), and a coalesced pair is SPLIT into two whole frames (never one garbled write). pure + unit-testable (data-driven: a single whole frame in one chunk; a frame split across 2/3/N chunks; two frames coalesced in one chunk; a partial with no sentinel → 0 frames + the whole chunk as `rest`; an oversized multi-KB frame chunked byte-by-byte → one whole frame). the client-side asCloneDispatchFrame FRAMES the message with the sentinel; this SERVER-side op is its inverse — the sentinel is the contract both honor. BOUNDED BUFFER — MAX-FRAME CAP (i043-r07-1 fix — the unbounded-reassembly-buffer hazard the review named): the input widens to `{ buffered, chunk, maxFrameBytes }`, and when `buffered + chunk` exceeds `maxFrameBytes` with NO sentinel yet seen, the transformer returns `{ frames: [], rest: '', overflow: true }` — a NACK-and-close SIGNAL, never an unbounded grow. genCloneSocketServer sets `maxFrameBytes` from ONE constant set generously above any legitimate payload (a comms-relay log-tail is KBs, so the cap sits well clear of the real usecase) and, on an `overflow: true`, writes a NACK ack-frame that names the cap + CLOSES the connection (the write-queue never sees a partial). WHY it matters even under same-user trust: this per-connection reassembly buffer is a DISTINCT resource from the write-queue depth — an uncooperative/adversarial client that streams bytes forever WITHOUT a sentinel would grow the buffer without bound (an OOM whose symptom — memory, latency — is hard to trace, the exact hidden behavior-hazard rule.forbid.behavior-hazards forbids), so the buffer is capped here. BOTH memory resources are now bounded (i054-r07-1): this reassembly buffer by `maxFrameBytes`, AND the write-queue depth by `CLONE_WRITE_QUEUE_MAX_DEPTH` at genCloneWriteQueue — each by its own generous cap, so the earlier one-capped-one-open asymmetry is closed. still PURE (the cap is an input, the overflow a return flag — no i/o); the unit test gains an oversized-no-sentinel row → `overflow: true`
│   │       ├── asCloneDispatchAckFrame.ts                 [+] transformer (i012-r11-1; phase widened i045-r11-3/r12-3) — `{ phase: 'queued'|'delivered'|'rejected', reason?: string } → bytes`: the SERVER-side response frame the server writes back. TWO-PHASE, NOT single-shot (i045-r11-3/r12-3 fix — the false-positive-wedged interaction the review named): the server emits `phase:'queued'` IMMEDIATELY on enqueue (the message was accepted onto the write-queue, the server is alive), then `phase:'delivered'` once the dispatch reaches the queue HEAD and pty.write lands, OR `phase:'rejected'+reason` on a gate/overflow rejection. WHY two phases: the earlier single ACK (written only AFTER pty.write) meant sayClone's fixed 5s timeout counted the WHOLE queue-wait — so a dispatch queued behind several legitimately-queued dispatches (two crons that hammer one clone, or one large multi-KB paste ahead in line) would trip the "wedged brain" MalfunctionError while the clone is perfectly HEALTHY, just busy — the exact false-positive the review found where the unbounded-queue decision (genCloneWriteQueue) and the fixed-timeout decision (sayClone) interact badly because they were reasoned about in separate rounds. the `queued` phase decouples the two: it proves liveness + acceptance the instant the message enqueues (a short connect-liveness bound), so the WEDGED timeout no longer has to cover queue-wait — it applies ONLY to the `queued → delivered` transition measured from when the dispatch becomes IN-FLIGHT (reaches the head, handed to pty.write), never from connect. so a busy-but-healthy clone streams `queued` at once + `delivered` when its turn comes, never a false wedged; only a genuinely stuck in-flight pty.write trips WEDGED. pure + unit-testable (queued/delivered/rejected rows). PAIRED with asCloneDispatchAck below (frame ↔ parse), the exact frame/parse symmetry asCloneDispatchFrame has on the input side
│   │       ├── asCloneDispatchAck.ts                      [+] transformer (i012-r11-1; phase widened i045-r11-3/r12-3) — the client-side inverse: `bytes → { phase: 'queued'|'delivered'|'rejected', reason?: string }`. sayClone parses each server response frame with this to learn which phase fired — `queued` (accepted, hold for the delivery frame), `delivered` (done, succeed), `rejected` (fail-loud with the reason); pure + unit-testable
│   │       ├── genCloneSocketServer.ts                   [+] communicator — unix socket server via infra/socket/createUnixSocketServer [←]; on EACH connection: FIRST isCallerSameUser [+] (i005-blocker-1 CORRECTED threat model — see below), THEN — per raw `data` event — accumulate the byte stream into COMPLETE frames via asCloneDispatchFrameSplit [+] (i039-r07-1, the SOCK_STREAM reassembly seam), and per COMPLETE frame run the isSafeCloneDispatchInput content gate → the write-queue (below) → pty.write. the socket bounds BOTH who may connect (caller-auth) AND what they may send (content gate) — the vision's "small blast radius" was only the latter. FRAME-REASSEMBLY (i039-r07-1 fix — the review's byte-stream hazard): a unix domain socket is a BYTE STREAM with NO message boundaries, so the server NEVER hands a raw `data` chunk straight to the content gate — it holds a per-connection `buffered` tail and passes ONLY a whole bracketed-paste-sentinel-terminated frame to the gate. so a large payload SPLIT across multiple `data` events is REASSEMBLED into one whole frame (never a partial mis-read as torn/corruption, never a partial handed to pty.write), and a COALESCED pair that arrives in one event is SPLIT into two whole frames (never one garbled write) — the intermittent, load-dependent SOCK_STREAM race the review named is closed at the source, with a real-stream chunked-delivery test at the server boundary (below). DISPATCH ACK RESPONSE FRAMES — TWO-PHASE (i012-r11-1 blocker fix, widened i045-r11-3/r12-3 — the rejection's TRANSPORT + the false-positive-wedged decoupling): after the content gate passes, the server enqueues the dispatch and IMMEDIATELY writes back `asCloneDispatchAckFrame({ phase: 'queued' })` (the message is accepted onto the write-queue, the server is alive — this frame does NOT wait for pty.write), then, once the dispatch reaches the queue HEAD and pty.write lands, writes `asCloneDispatchAckFrame({ phase: 'delivered' })` and closes; a gate/overflow/caller-auth rejection writes `asCloneDispatchAckFrame({ phase: 'rejected', reason })` and closes. the SECOND phase is what lets sayClone's WEDGED timeout apply ONLY to the in-flight `queued → delivered` window, never to the queue-wait behind other dispatches (i045-r11-3/r12-3): a busy clone streams `queued` at once, so a caller behind a deep queue is never falsely reported wedged, and only a genuinely stuck head-of-queue pty.write leaves `delivered` unsent. this makes the socket a genuine REQUEST/RESPONSE protocol (the extant `connectToKeyrackDaemon` precedent, described "returns socket instance for request/response"), not fire-and-forget: without a response frame a server-side rejection could NEVER reach invokeCloneSay's exit code/stderr, so the matrix + uc10 "unsafe/rejected content → fail loud back to the CLI caller" contract had a stated outcome with NO wire to carry it. the ack frames ARE that wire. BUFFER CAP — NACK + CLOSE ON OVERFLOW (i043-r07-1 fix — the unbounded-reassembly-buffer hazard): the per-connection `buffered` tail feeds asCloneDispatchFrameSplit with a `maxFrameBytes` cap (ONE constant set well above any legitimate comms-relay payload); on an `overflow: true` return (a no-sentinel stream that pushed the buffer past the cap) the server writes a NACK `asCloneDispatchAckFrame({ phase: 'rejected', reason })` that names the cap, THEN CLOSES the connection — so the reassembly buffer is BOUNDED, never an unbounded grow under an uncooperative/adversarial client (an OOM whose symptom is hard to trace, the exact behavior-hazard rule.forbid.behavior-hazards forbids). this bounds the INPUT buffer where genCloneWriteQueue's DEPTH is deliberately unbounded — two DISTINCT resources, one capped (the reassembly tail, a hard limit) and one trust-bounded (the queue depth, a same-user-runaway ledger note), each governed by the right discipline.
│   │       │   ── ⚠️ THREAT-MODEL CORRECTION (i005-blocker-1): the i003 fix wrongly reused the keyrack daemon's verifyCallerLoginSession (same-LOGIN-SESSION). that gate is right for SECRETS on a shared host, but it REJECTS a cross-session caller — which is EXACTLY the cron / comms-webhook usecase this behavior exists to serve (a cron's PAM session ≠ the interactive session that ran `rhx enroll`). so it would fail-loud on "a cron nudges an idle clone" (uc6/uc7, the vision's day-in-life). the CORRECT threat model for a clone socket is same-USER-any-session: block OTHER users on the host, ALLOW this user's cron/bot/other-session. so the gate is isCallerSameUser (compare the peer's UID from SO_PEERCRED to process.getuid()), NOT verifyCallerLoginSession
│   │       ├── isCallerSameUser.ts                   [+] communicator (i005-blocker-1) — read the peer's UID from the socket via getSocketPeerCred (SO_PEERCRED → { pid, uid, gid }), compare to the server process's own getuid(); a DIFFERENT-user peer → fail-loud ConstraintError. same-user (any login session) → pass. this is the threat model uc6's "any process, via the clone's socket" + matrix "two crons dispatch to one live clone" actually need
│   │       ├── genCloneWriteQueue.ts                     [+] communicator — the NAMED single-writer primitive (nitpick-6 fix): a per-clone serial write-queue that genCloneSocketServer funnels EVERY inbound dispatch through, so two concurrent `say` connections whose bytes chunk across socket packets are drained one-whole-message-at-a-time to pty.write — never interleaved. this makes the "single-writer, no interleave" guarantee an OWNED, unit-testable mechanism, not an emergent property of node's per-connection handlers (which do NOT serialize chunked concurrent writers for free). TWO-PHASE ACK AT THE QUEUE BOUNDARY (i045-r11-3/r12-3): the queue emits `phase:'queued'` the instant a dispatch enqueues (before its turn at the head) and `phase:'delivered'` once pty.write lands at the head — so a caller learns acceptance immediately + delivery on its turn, which is what lets sayClone's WEDGED timeout skip the queue-wait and arm only on the in-flight window (never a false-positive wedged for a busy-but-healthy clone). BOUNDED DEPTH via a GENEROUS CAP + NACK (i054-r07-1 fix — the buffer-capped-but-queue-uncapped inconsistency the review named): the reassembly buffer already caps at `maxFrameBytes` (asCloneDispatchFrameSplit, i043-r07-1), yet the queue DEPTH was left uncapped — two adjacent memory resources under two different rules, the exact asymmetry the review flags. so the queue now caps at ONE `CLONE_WRITE_QUEUE_MAX_DEPTH` const (a GENEROUS limit set well above any legitimate burst — no real caller holds thousands of not-yet-delivered dispatches to ONE clone): a dispatch that arrives when the queued-not-delivered set is already at the cap gets an IMMEDIATE `asCloneDispatchAckFrame({ phase: 'rejected', reason })` that names the cap + the fix (slow the dispatch rate, or await `delivered` before the next `say`), reused off the EXACT two-phase `rejected` wire the gate-reject + drain-on-exit paths already carry — so sayClone fails-loud on the NACK with no new client path. the cap bites the runaway, NOT the burst: a legitimate two-cron burst sits far below it and is never touched, while a genuine same-user runaway is bounded rather than an unbounded memory grow — so the queue now behaves predictably under repeated same-user invocation, on par with the buffer (rule.forbid.behavior-hazards, the review's exact rule). the same-user trust boundary (isCallerSameUser) still bounds WHO may enqueue; the cap now ALSO bounds HOW MANY may sit queued, so the two distinct resources (reassembly buffer, queue depth) are governed by the SAME bounded discipline — no longer one capped + one open. the `queued` ack still makes an accepted-but-not-yet-delivered dispatch OBSERVABLE to the caller; the cap adds a hard stop past the generous limit. a depth-cap NACK is the say-side analog of the enroll-side accrual-WARN — both bound a same-user runaway, but ASYMMETRICALLY-BY-NATURE: a single clone's input queue has a real finite limit (no caller legitimately queues thousands of undelivered messages to ONE brain), so the say-side is a HARD cap; a valid parallel-clone burst has NO natural limit (the wish's own parallel-clone motive), so the enroll-side stays a SOFT warn. same rule, two resources, two correct disciplines
│   │       ├── sayClone.ts                               [+] communicator — connect via connectToClone [←] (the shared adapter, nitpick-4) + write framed message to socket, THEN read the server's TWO-PHASE response frames + parse each via asCloneDispatchAck (i012-r11-1; two-phase i045-r11-3/r12-3): a `rejected` frame → FAIL-LOUD (ConstraintError naming the server's reason); a `queued` frame → the message is accepted onto the write-queue, hold for the `delivered` frame; a `delivered` frame → succeed. so sayClone is request/RESPONSE, NOT fire-and-forget — a server-side content-gate rejection now propagates to invokeCloneSay's non-zero exit + stderr, which is the ONLY way the matrix "unsafe input → rejected + fail loud back to the CLI caller" contract can actually hold (a write-then-return sayClone would report success on a rejected dispatch — the exact fail-hide this blocker names). the DELIVERY-vs-PROCESSING boundary (i007-r12-3) is UNCHANGED: the `delivered` ack confirms the message was ACCEPTED onto the input (gate passed + pty.write landed), NOT that the brain finished the work — reply correlation stays the `get`-poll the round-trip proves. TWO-PHASE TIMEOUTS — the false-positive-wedged fix (i017-r12-1 fail-fast gap, corrected i045-r11-3/r12-3 — the review's queue-vs-timeout interaction): the AWAIT is bounded by TWO distinct timeouts, not one fixed 5s over the whole exchange. (1) a SHORT connect-liveness bound on the `queued` frame (default 5s, an IsoDuration const) — if the server accepts the connection but never even emits `queued` (a crash mid-accept, a server that cannot enqueue), sayClone aborts + FAILS-LOUD MalfunctionError ("clone @:<x> accepted the connection but sent no queued-ack within 5s — the server may be wedged; check `rhx clone get @:<x>`, or re-enroll"). (2) a WEDGED bound on the `queued → delivered` transition — armed ONLY once `queued` is received (i.e. measured from acceptance onto the queue, NOT from connect), and RESET/absorbed by queue-wait: because the server sends `queued` immediately, a dispatch behind a deep queue has already cleared timeout (1) and simply waits for its turn; the wedged bound covers only the in-flight window once the dispatch is at the HEAD. so a HEALTHY-but-BUSY clone (two crons that hammer it, or a large paste ahead in line) is NEVER falsely reported wedged — it gets `queued` at once and `delivered` when its turn comes; a `delivered` that never arrives AFTER a bounded in-flight window → FAIL-LOUD MalfunctionError ("clone @:<x> accepted the message onto its queue but did not deliver it within <bound> once in-flight — the brain may be wedged; check `rhx clone get @:<x>`, or re-enroll"). the connect step is bounded too (a refused/absent socket already fails fast via connectToClone; these bounds cover the connected-but-silent cases the connect step cannot). MalfunctionError (exit 1) because a silent server is a state/infra fault, not a caller-input error. this closes BOTH the original fail-fast gap (a cron never hangs forever on a stuck clone) AND the false-positive the review found (a busy-but-healthy clone is never mis-reported wedged just because a legitimate queue formed) — the two decisions (unbounded queue, ack-timeout) that were reasoned about separately now compose correctly
│   │       ├── asCloneOutputText.ts                      [+] transformer (i005-r12-C) — a transcript LINE/BLOCK (`history/<exid>.jsonl`, where each line is a full structured claude-code event — content arrays, tool-use, thinking blocks) → the plain-text, human/bot-LEGIBLE assistant reply. this is the seam the comms usecase needs: the vision's "a slack reply becomes a message… `rhx clone get`" implies a legible read, but a raw JSONL line is NOT legible; asCloneOutputText extracts the text content, so `get` returns a message a human/bot can act on, not a structured-event dump. a non-assistant / tool-only line renders to empty (a SEMANTIC filter — a tool-result/thinking block carries no assistant text, so an empty render is the correct projection, NOT a swallowed error), so the tail is assistant text. NO PARSE-ERROR SWALLOW (i018-r02-1): asCloneOutputText does NOT catch-and-skip a JSON.parse failure — getCloneOutput upstream feeds it only COMPLETE (newline-terminated) records (the completeness check), so a parse fault here is genuine corruption of a complete record → it FAILS LOUD (MalfunctionError), never maps to empty. the empty render is reserved for a well-formed line that simply holds no assistant text; a malformed complete line throws (rule.forbid.failhide). PER-BRAIN OBSERVE ADAPTER (i009-r11-2): this parses claude-code's SPECIFIC jsonl schema — it is a claude-specific adapter TODAY, the observe-side twin of the dispatch-side per-brain gate. it is REACHED only for a brain whose `.socket` capability is true (claude), so a non-claude clone never hits this parser (the `.socket` gate now covers BOTH say + get, above). when a second socket-capable brain lands, its transcript schema gets its OWN observe adapter selected off the same getSupportedBrainCommand map — the SAME reactive-lift seam the pty-spawn primitives use (flagged, not built now: claude is the sole socket-capable brain shipping, so a second observe adapter would be a speculative build). so the observe side is per-brain by the SAME mechanism as the input side, no longer a silent claude-only hole
│   │       └── getCloneOutput.ts                         [+] communicator — tail the clone's history. MULTI-EPISODE MERGE ORDER (i007-r11-B4: a long-lived clone's `series` accretes MANY `history/<exid>.jsonl` episodes bridged by compaction — this is the MAINLINE, not an edge — and exid is a session UUID, NOT chronologically sortable): the symlinks are ordered by each linked file's MTIME (chronological across episode boundaries), with the `<exid>` filename as a LEXICOGRAPHIC SECONDARY key that breaks a same-mtime tie DETERMINISTICALLY (i052-r11-3/r12-2 fix — mtime granularity is coarse (a whole second on many filesystems), so two episodes written in the SAME tick, a plausible compaction-boundary race, would otherwise sort non-deterministically and FLAKE the mandated multi-episode snapshot + the cron's `get` output; the exid filename is a stable, deterministic string, so the secondary sort is reproducible run-to-run — the two same-second episodes are functionally adjacent, so the exact interleave between them does not matter, only that it is STABLE), then their lines are concatenated in that order, THEN each line is MAPPED through asCloneOutputText + the empties FILTERED OUT, and ONLY THEN is the tail applied — the ONE explicit order (i011-r11-1): map+filter to logical messages FIRST (across all mtime-ordered episodes), tail N of the survivors LAST. this order is load-bearing, not incidental: a tail applied to RAW lines BEFORE the map would very often return an empty `--tail 1` (the true last raw line is usually a tool-result/thought-block line that maps to empty), breaking the mandated `say` → `get --tail 1` round-trip (uc8/the clamp). so `get` never returns messages out of order or from the wrong episode when a session compacts, AND `--tail N` counts LOGICAL MESSAGES (assistant replies via asCloneOutputText), NOT raw JSONL lines — so `--tail 1` returns the last reply whole, never a fragment of a structured event nor an empty tool-result line; `--tail N` > available → all available (i006-r12-6). TORN-LINE POLICY via a RECORD-COMPLETENESS CHECK, NOT a swallow (i009-r12-2, sharpened i018-r02-1 — the failhide reviewer is RIGHT that a silent catch-and-skip of a JSON.parse failure is a failhide): `history/<exid>.jsonl` is the brain's OWN LIVE transcript, appended incrementally while the clone runs — so a `get` mid-write (a cron polls an in-progress session, the mainline usecase) can read a final line not yet newline-terminated. the fix is NOT to parse-then-catch-then-skip (that WOULD be failhide); it is a COMPLETENESS CHECK done BEFORE any parse: a JSONL record is COMPLETE iff it is newline-terminated. getCloneOutput reads only COMPLETE (newline-terminated) records; an un-terminated TRAILING fragment is NOT a record yet — it is never handed to JSON.parse at all, so there is no error to catch and no error to hide (the standard tail-a-log discipline: an incomplete final record is not-yet-data, not a failure). CONSEQUENTLY a JSON.parse failure on a COMPLETE (newline-terminated) record is a GENUINE corruption fault → FAIL-LOUD MalfunctionError that names the bad file + line (rule.forbid.failhide honored — the only lines "skipped" are non-records by construction; every real parse fault on a complete record throws). so the torn-line case is no longer a swallowed exception — it is a record-boundary rule, and true corruption is never hidden. DANGLING-SYMLINK POLICY via a SURFACED WARN, NOT a silent skip (i017-r12-2, sharpened i018-r02-2 — the reviewer is RIGHT that a silent ENOENT skip hides a real condition): `history/<exid>.jsonl` symlinks point OUTSIDE rhachet's control, into `~/.claude/projects/**`, so a target can VANISH (claude-code retention/cleanup, or a user who clears that dir), and `readFile` on it throws ENOENT. getCloneOutput allowlists ONLY ENOENT-on-a-symlink-target (any OTHER read error re-throws — rule.forbid.failhide's allowlist discipline), and it does NOT skip silently. CRITICALLY it does NOT write to stderr ITSELF (i023-r07-nitpick1 — a `get*` op must carry NO hidden IO side effect per rule.forbid.hidden-side-effects + the get-set-gen-verbs "get = pure read" convention; a stderr write buried in a retrieval would surprise a cron that redirects only stdout): instead getCloneOutput RETURNS the unreadable episodes IN-BAND as part of its result — `{ lines, exidsUnreadable: string[] }` — so the RETRIEVAL stays a pure read that REPORTS the condition in its return value, and the INVOKER (invokeCloneGet, the contract/cli layer where IO belongs) renders the one-line stderr WARN from `exidsUnreadable` (`⚠ get: N history episode(s) unreadable — transcript(s) reclaimed by the brain-cli: <exid>…`). getCloneOutput reads the episodes that remain + returns the readable lines. the condition is thus VISIBLE (the invoker surfaces it to stderr — the reviewer's own offered resolution) yet the side effect lives at the PRESENTATION boundary, not hidden inside a `get`. `get` still returns the readable output rather than crash a cron on a benign reclaimed-transcript. the invoker's WARN goes to STDERR so the `--output json` stdout stays clean for a machine consumer (which reads stdout); an all-vanished history → empty stdout + the WARN on stderr, never a raw ENOENT crash. so both former "silent skips" are gone: the torn line is a completeness check (no error exists to hide, real corruption fails loud), the vanished episode is a SURFACED warn (the condition is announced, not swallowed) — rule.forbid.failhide satisfied on both, with the fail-fast-vs-usability balance the mainline cron/comms path needs

