│   ├── host/getOneRepoPath.ts                            [+] create — communicator (i040-r11-B1 fix — the CANONICALIZATION single-owner op the review named): `{ cwd } → repoPath` (the canonical, symlink-followed, trailing-slash-stripped absolute path) via `fs.realpathSync(cwd)`. a COMMUNICATOR, not a transformer, because `realpathSync` touches the filesystem to follow symlinks to the real path (impure i/o, so `(input, context)` grain per define.domain-operation-grains). THE ROOT-CAUSE FIX for the identity-FRAGMENTATION hazard the i037/i039 composite-key fix itself introduced: `repoPath` joined ActorEnrolled's primary key (i037-r12-1) + Clone.actor's composite ref (i039-r11-B1) to make actor identity repo-truthful, BUT no op canonicalized the string — so two callers pointing at the SAME actual repo via DIFFERENT equivalent paths (a symlinked checkout vs the real path, a `/tmp`→`/private/tmp` macOS alias, a trailing slash) would derive DIFFERENT `repoPath` strings → a DIFFERENT ActorEnrolled/Clone.actor identity for what is genuinely ONE actor — a human who `cd`s in via an equivalent path suddenly cannot find their own actor/clone (the exact "one decision, two places" trap re-introduced one layer up: the identity FIX had no canonicalization POLICY, so it swapped a narrow collision risk for a broad FRAGMENTATION risk). so `getOneRepoPath` is the ONE op that derives `repoPath`, and EVERY repoPath-consuming op receives ITS output, NEVER a raw `process.cwd()`: getActorEnrolledDir, getOneActorEnrolledByRef, getOneCloneHydrated, findsertActorEnrolled, getAllActorsEnrolled, and the FIVE invokers (invokeEnroll, invokeActorList, invokeCloneList, invokeCloneSay, invokeCloneGet) all take a canonicalized `repoPath` threaded from ONE getOneRepoPath({ cwd: process.cwd() }) call at the cli boundary — so two equivalent cwds converge to ONE identity by construction (rule.require.solve-at-cause), and the composite key stays repo-truthful WITHOUT the fragmentation. THE REACHABLE SCENARIO that justifies keeping the composite key at all (answers the review's nitpick — "is repoPath solving a reachable hazard?"): (1) the HERMETIC TEMP-REPO ACCEPTANCE HARNESS runs under `os.tmpdir()`, and on macOS `os.tmpdir()` returns `/var/folders/…` (a symlink to `/private/var/folders/…`) OR `/tmp`→`/private/tmp` — so the SAME temp repo is reachable by two path strings WITHIN one test run, the fragmentation the canonicalization closes; (2) SYMLINKED CHECKOUTS + GIT WORKTREES — this VERY repo runs its behavior under `_worktrees/`, and a worktree's path vs its main-checkout equivalent are distinct strings for repos that share `.agent/.actors/` on a synced/shared fs. so repoPath IS solving a reachable hazard (not a phantom the directory-nesting already covers), and getOneRepoPath is what makes the composite key SAFE rather than fragment — the two decisions (keep the key, canonicalize the input) are one coherent identity policy. Clone.ts + ActorEnrolled.ts prose note that `repoPath` is ALWAYS the getOneRepoPath-canonical realpath, never a raw cwd, so an executor threads the canonical value, never re-derives from process.cwd() at a consuming op. FAIL-LOUD WRAP (i048-r11-1/r12 fix — the sole communicator without one, the review's last consistency holdout): `fs.realpathSync(cwd)` THROWS a raw ENOENT/EACCES on a stale/deleted/inaccessible cwd — a REALISTIC hazard in THIS very repo, which runs behavior work under `_worktrees/` a `git worktree prune` can remove mid-session. every OTHER communicator wraps its failure per rule.require.failloud; this op was the exception, so a human in a pruned worktree would hit an unhelpful raw stack trace instead of a named fix. so getOneRepoPath now wraps the realpathSync in a MalfunctionError (exit 1, a state/environment fault, NOT a caller-input error — the cwd went away underneath a running process) that NAMES the cause + the fix: `"the working directory <cwd> no longer resolves — it may be a pruned git worktree or a deleted dir; cd into a live checkout and retry"`. an ENOENT/EACCES is the wrapped case; any other realpathSync throw re-raises through the wrap with its own context (rule.forbid.failhide — the allowlist is the resolvable-cwd faults, none swallowed). getOneRepoPath.integration.test gains a DOGFOODED stale-cwd row: run the op against a cwd that was `rmdir`'d out from under it → assert the MalfunctionError names the dir + the fix, NOT a raw ENOENT (the exact failure-path coverage the review found absent)
