├── contract/cli/
│   ├── renderCliOutput.ts                                [+] create (i010-r11-B) — the ONE shared `--output tree|json` seam: `{ result, output: 'tree'|'json', asTree: (result) => string } → string`. `json` → `JSON.stringify(result, null, 2)` of the SAME domain result the tree branch renders; `tree` → `asTree(result)`; an unknown `--output` value → fail-loud naming `tree|json` — and THAT fail-loud renders through the TREE/plain channel (stderr prose + exit 2), NEVER json (i040-r11-minor: by construction the error CANNOT be json — the value that would select json is exactly the invalid one — so an invalid `--output` reports on the human/plain channel, the safe fallback; stated here so the invalid-output error's own channel is specified, not left implicit). all FOUR talk invokers (invokeActorList, invokeCloneList, invokeCloneSay, invokeCloneGet) COMPOSE this instead of each re-implementing the identical branch (i010-r11-B: 4 known call sites at design time is PAST the rule-of-three, and the dream's `make`/`fork`/`wake` will each want the same flag — one seam keeps tree/json from drifting as the talk surface grows, rule.prefer.wet-over-dry's rule-of-three met). pure transform, unit-testable; the `asTree` renderer stays per-command (each result shape has its own treestruct), only the tree-vs-json SELECTION + the unknown-value fail-loud are shared. STRUCTURED-ERROR ARM under `--output json` (i024-r12-1 blocker fix — the machine-consumption gap the review named: the SUCCESS shape was covered, the FAILURE shape was not, so a cron that requested `--output json` to AVOID prose still had to regex stderr to tell DEAD from NOT-REACHABLE from cross-host). the seam gains an error arm: when a talk verb THROWS a ConstraintError/MalfunctionError AND `--output json` was requested, the invoker's catch renders the failure through the paired `asCliErrorJson` transformer (below) to STDOUT as `{ error: { class, message, hint, reachState? } }`, and STILL exits with the error's OWN code (ConstraintError → 2, MalfunctionError → 1, per rule.require.exit-code-semantics) — so a machine BRANCHES on `error.reachState` (`'DEAD'|'NOT-REACHABLE'|null`) + `error.class`, never on prose, exactly the friction `--output json` exists to remove (usecase.11: "a machine consumer reads fields, not box-glyph characters"). under `--output tree` (the human default) the failure path is UNCHANGED — the human stderr prose + exit code the adherence checklist already specifies. so the tree human sees prose on stderr, the json machine sees a structured error on stdout, and BOTH carry the same non-zero exit
│   ├── asCliErrorJson.ts                                 [+] create (i024-r12-1) — transformer (PURE): `{ error: ConstraintError | MalfunctionError } → { class: string, message: string, hint: string | null, reachState: CloneReachState | null }`. the STRUCTURED-ERROR projection the `--output json` failure arm renders: pulls `class` (the error constructor name → caller-vs-server granularity a bare exit code cannot carry), `message`, the `hint` (the named fix the error already carries per rule.require.errors-name-the-fix), and — when the thrown error is a reach failure whose cause is a tri-state (a computeCloneUnreachableHint reject) — the `reachState` (the shared `CloneReachState` imported from computeCloneReachState.ts, i012-r11-3, NEVER re-declared) so a machine branches DEAD-vs-NOT-REACHABLE without a regex. a non-reach error (unknown-ref, ambiguous-prefix, malformed-`--as`) carries `reachState: null` (the field is present-but-null, never absent, so a consumer's `error.reachState` read is always defined). pure + unit-testable (data-driven rows over the error classes); the SIX invokers' catch — via the shared `withCliOutputErrors` wrapper (below, i046-r11-1/r12-3) — composes it for the json arm, so the structured-error shape has ONE owner across ALL SIX `--output json` commands (invokeEnroll + the four talk verbs + invokeCloneWhoami) and cannot drift command-to-command
│   ├── withCliOutputErrors.ts                            [+] create (i046-r11-1/r12-3 fix — the SIX-copy `--output json` error-catch skeleton, past rule-of-three, un-extracted — the highest-confidence duplicate item, raised INDEPENDENTLY by both l3 lenses) — a higher-order wrapper `withCliOutputErrors(operate, { output }) → wrapped` that OWNS the `--output`-aware failure control-flow ONE time: it runs `operate`, and on a thrown ConstraintError/MalfunctionError it branches ONCE — `output === 'json'` → the failure through asCliErrorJson to STDOUT + exit with the error's OWN code (ConstraintError → 2, MalfunctionError → 1, per rule.require.exit-code-semantics); else (tree/human) → the stderr prose + the same code. WHY: `invokeEnroll` + `invokeActorList` + `invokeCloneList` + `invokeCloneSay` + `invokeCloneGet` + `invokeCloneWhoami` each hand-rolled the IDENTICAL try/catch → `output==='json'` branch → asCliErrorJson → set-exit-code sequence — SIX copies, well past the rule-of-three the blueprint's OWN renderCliOutput extraction cites at 4 sites (i010-r11-B), so this is the STRONGER case for the SAME extraction discipline. the CONTROL-FLOW wrapper is now extracted the way the TRANSFORMER (asCliErrorJson) already was: one owner, so a future copy-paste omission in one invoker's catch (a forgotten `output==='json'` branch, or a drifted exit-code) is IMPOSSIBLE — there is one catch, not six. mirrors the extant `withHook`/`withLogTrail`/`withRetry` HOF-wrapper convention (rule.require.hook-wrapper-pattern) the repo already sanctions: `export const invokeCloneSay = withCliOutputErrors(_invokeCloneSay)`, so a hook add/remove is a one-line diff and each of the six invokers reads as pure narrative with NO catch boilerplate. the `asTree`/success render stays each invoker's own (via renderCliOutput); only the FAILURE control-flow is the shared wrapper — the exact decompose-for-recompose split the blueprint applies everywhere (renderCliOutput owns the success tree-vs-json SELECTION, asCliErrorJson owns the error PROJECTION, withCliOutputErrors owns the error CONTROL-FLOW). this also closes the cross-invoker `--output json` error-shape consistency gap both l3 lenses named at i046: the error-format.consistency acceptance test's own documented limitation covers only the human stderr "what+why+fix" shape, NOT the cross-invoker consistency of the json structured-error shape — the shared wrapper makes that consistency STRUCTURAL (one catch, one shape), not a per-invoker copy a future edit could drift
│   ├── invokeEnroll.ts                                   [~] update — optional positional, --brain, --as, --no-socket, --output, --reason; findsert actor + gen clone. AUDIT REASON (i046-r12-5 fix — the vision's audit "why" had no CLI input): `--reason <text>` (optional) captures WHY this enroll happened, threaded to findsertActorEnrolled → setActorEnrolledRolesLog so the `roles/enrollment.jsonl` "why" column carries a real, caller-supplied motive (the vision's stated purpose), never a constant. `--reason @stdin` reads the motive from stdin (the same `@stdin` convention as `say --what`). omitted → a null reason (present-but-empty, never fabricated); a clone.acceptance case asserts a CLI-supplied reason reaches the log line and a bare enroll records a null reason. VALIDATES the `--as @:<slug>` body (i005-r12-D): fail-loud if it lacks the `@:` marker OR if the slug body is UUID-shaped (a uuid-shaped slug would route to asCloneRef's SERIAL branch and never be looked up as a slug — reject at enroll, name the fix) OR if the slug body is NOT filesystem-path-safe (i008-r11-B2: the slug builds a real path `.agent/.actors/.slugs/<slug>` in setCloneSlugIndex, so an unvalidated `--as @:../../evil` is a PATH-TRAVERSAL vector — reject via isSafeCloneSlug at the SAME enroll-time boundary as the marker + uuid checks, name the fix "a --as slug must match [a-z0-9][a-z0-9-_]*"). DID-YOU-MEAN ON A SIGIL TYPO (i044-r12-2 fix — the did-you-mean symmetry asCloneRef/asActorRef already carry, absent at the enroll boundary the review named): the marker fail-loud PROPOSES the corrected form, never a bare reject — a no-sigil bare `--as driver` → "a --as name needs the clone sigil — did you mean --as @:driver?"; a WRONG-grain `--as @driver` (the actor sigil where a clone name is wanted) → "@driver is an actor address — did you mean --as @:driver?" — the SAME one-character correction the address parsers give, folded in HERE because enroll's `--as` is the exact point a first-time user is most likely to typo the sigil (rule.require.errors-name-the-fix); a clone.acceptance snapshot asserts both suggestion texts. SUPERSEDED-FLAG HINT (i044-r12-4 fix, minor): the wish's ORIGINAL `--alias` flag (superseded by `--as` at the 2026-08-07 amendment) is caught as an unknown option and answered with a did-you-mean — "--alias was renamed to --as — use --as @:<slug>" — so an early adopter who followed the pre-amendment wish text is redirected, never left with a bare "unknown option" (rule.require.errors-name-the-fix). MACHINE-SERIAL HANDOFF via `--output json` (i026-r12-2 blocker — best-guess delivery, the channel-design fork flagged for council: the wish's LEAD motive is a supervisor/cron that spawns `rhx enroll` non-interactively to stand up a REACHABLE clone, but F7's stderr hint fires ONLY for an interactive TTY, so a detached supervisor got NO serial — the missing link for the wish's own headline usecase, and it had no test). the delivery: `rhx enroll <brain> --output json` (a) OPTS the clone INTO socket eligibility even WITHOUT an interactive TTY — a machine that passes `--output json` is EXPLICITLY asking for a reachable clone, so isCloneSocketEligible is satisfied by `--output json` in lieu of isInteractiveTty (still gated on isBrainSocketCapable + getPtyModuleOrNull, so a non-capable brain / absent addon still falls back), and (b) EMITS a ONE-LINE machine handoff `{ serial, socketEligible, actorHash, socketPath }` to STDOUT as the FIRST line, so the supervisor reads the serial from stdout (not a race on `clone list`, not a reach into `identity.json` off-disk behind the CLI contract), THEN `say`/`get`s the clone by that serial. this is the SAME `renderCliOutput` json seam the talk verbs use. CHANNEL-DESIGN FORK (flagged, ledger row 10): the interactive enroll's stdout is the invisible human mirror (uc1 byte-fidelity), so the machine json + the mirror cannot share stdout for an INTERACTIVE run — but a `--output json` enroll is the MACHINE path (a supervisor is not watching a terminal), so under `--output json` the human mirror is SUPPRESSED (a detached supervisor has no human to mirror to) and stdout carries ONLY the handoff line; the interactive path (no `--output json`) keeps stdout as the pure mirror + the F7 stderr hint, unchanged. INTERACTIVE `--output json` NOTICE (i044-r12-1 fix — the footgun the review named): the suppression is keyed on the FLAG alone, so a HUMAN who runs `rhx enroll claude --output json` at an interactive TTY (to test the flag, or from a copy-pasted doc line) would lose the invisible mirror + see only the one handoff line then apparent silence, with NO cue it is expected. so when `--output json` AND isInteractiveTty both hold, enroll emits a one-line STDERR notice — "⌨ --output json is the machine-handoff mode — the interactive mirror is suppressed; drop --output json for a normal session" — so a human is never left at a silent terminal (rule.require.status-feedback); a NON-tty spawn (the detached supervisor, the intended caller) SKIPS the notice, so the machine-handoff stdout stays a clean single line. a clone.acceptance case asserts the interactive-tty notice fires (exit 0, the enroll still succeeds) and the detached spawn does NOT. the alternative shape — mirror-plus-a-dedicated-fd handoff — is the clean-rework the council may prefer; either way the interactive uc1 byte-fidelity is untouched (json handoff is opt-in, machine-only). STRUCTURED FAILURE ARM (i039-r07-nit1 fix — the enroll-vs-talk asymmetry the review named): under `--output json`, a FAILURE (brain conflict, global slug collision, no-`.agent/`, malformed/uuid/path-unsafe `--as`) renders the SAME `{ error: { class, message, hint, reachState: null } }` via asCliErrorJson to STDOUT + the error's OWN exit code (ConstraintError → 2, MalfunctionError → 1), SYMMETRIC with the four talk verbs. so a supervisor that spawns `rhx enroll <brain> --output json` and hits, say, a global slug collision reads a STRUCTURED, field-addressable error — NOT the stderr prose the earlier spec left it, which was the exact machine-parseability friction `--output json` exists to remove everywhere else. the human path (no `--output json`) keeps its stderr prose + exit code, unchanged; only the machine path gains the structured failure
│   ├── invokeActor.ts                                    [+] create — dispatcher: registers the `actor` subcommands (mirrors invokeRoles.ts [←])
│   ├── invokeActorList.ts                                [+] create — `rhx actor list [--output tree|json]` (one subcommand per file, per invokeRolesLink/Boot/… [←]). EMPTY-STATE HINT (i052-r12-5, discoverability): when NO actor is enrolled, the `tree` render is NOT a bare empty tree — it carries a one-line get-started breadcrumb `no actors yet — run 'rhx enroll <brain>' to enroll one` (rule.require.discoverability: a first-time human hitting an empty tree is prompted the next move, consistent with F7's breadcrumbs + the did-you-mean sigils elsewhere). the `--output json` empty case stays a clean empty array (a machine reads emptiness as `[]`, never a prose hint on the stdout it parses)
│   ├── invokeClone.ts                                    [+] create — dispatcher: registers the `clone` subcommands — list, say, get, AND whoami (i040-r12-1) — (mirrors invokeRoles.ts [←])
│   ├── invokeCloneList.ts                                [+] create — `rhx clone list [@<actor>] [--output tree|json]`. EMPTY-STATE HINT (i052-r12-5, discoverability): the UNSCOPED `clone list` with NO clones anywhere renders the one-line breadcrumb `no clones yet — run 'rhx enroll <brain>' to spawn one` (the same get-started nudge as `actor list`, so an empty tree is never a dead end). the `--output json` empty case stays `[]`. NOTE the SCOPED `clone list @<actor>` to a VALID actor with zero clones keeps its OWN distinct empty render (ledger row: an existing actor with no clones, DISTINCT from "no clones anywhere" and from "unknown actor → fail-loud") — the get-started breadcrumb is the NO-CLONES-ANYWHERE case, not the scoped-empty case
│   ├── invokeCloneSay.ts                                 [+] create — `rhx clone say @:<slug|serial> --what <m> [--output tree|json]`; `--what @stdin` reads the message from stdin (i004-r12 finding-3 — matches the repo convention `git.commit.set -m @stdin`, `sedreplace --old @stdin`), so multi-line / quote-heavy comms payloads (the vision's "a slack reply becomes a message") have a clean CLI path. INTERACTIVE-STDIN HINT (i040-r12-3 nitpick — the forgot-to-pipe hang): when `--what @stdin` is given AND `process.stdin.isTTY` is true (an interactive terminal, no pipe) → print a one-line stderr hint `⌨ reading message from stdin — type it, then Ctrl-D to end (or Ctrl-C to cancel)` BEFORE the read blocks, so a human who ran `--what @stdin` without a pipe sees WHY the command waits, never a silent hang (rule.require.status-feedback). a piped/non-TTY stdin (the cron/comms path) skips the hint (no human to inform)
│   ├── invokeCloneGet.ts                                 [+] create — `rhx clone get @:<slug|serial> [--tail N] [--output tree|json]`. BARE-TEXT TREE RENDER (i013-r12-A fix — the comms-relay usecase): `get`'s `asTree` is the ONE talk render that emits RAW assistant text (the mapped `asCloneOutputText` reply lines joined by newline), with NO `├─`/`└─` box-glyphs — UNLIKE `list`/`actor list` whose tree is a real treestruct. this is REQUIRED by the vision's own lead usecase ("a slack reply becomes a message… `rhx clone get`"): a human/cron pipes `get` straight to Slack/email, so a box-glyph wrapper would ship garbage. the `--output json` branch stays structured (the reply lines as an array); only the human default is bare text. DEFAULT TAIL BOUND (i013-r12-B fix — the criteria's "recent output"): a bare `get` with NO `--tail` defaults to `--tail 20` (the last 20 LOGICAL messages), NOT the whole transcript — because uc7 says "recent output" (a bound is implied) and a long-lived multi-episode clone's full history would flood a human or a cron on every plain `get`. an explicit large `--tail N` is the opt-in for more (i006-r12-6: N > available → all available); `--tail 0`/negative stays INVALID (unchanged from i008-r12-4). the default is a sane bound, per rule.prefer.defaults-match-common-case. `--tail all` SENTINEL (i015-r12-5 fix): a comms/cron consumer that wants the WHOLE transcript passes `--tail all` — an explicit, discoverable "give me everything" rather than "pass a large N and guess" (the friction the reviewer named). `all` is the ONE non-numeric `--tail` value accepted (documented in `get --help`); every other non-numeric value stays the fail-loud "must be a positive integer or 'all'". UNREADABLE-EPISODE WARN (i023-r07-nitpick1 — the stderr side effect lives HERE, at the contract/cli presentation boundary, NOT inside getCloneOutput): getCloneOutput RETURNS `{ lines, exidsUnreadable }`; when `exidsUnreadable` is non-empty (a reclaimed/vanished transcript), invokeCloneGet renders the one-line stderr WARN (`⚠ get: N history episode(s) unreadable — transcript(s) reclaimed by the brain-cli: <exid>…`) from that field, THEN prints the readable `lines` to stdout. so the retrieval stays a pure `get` (no hidden stderr write) and the IO side effect is the invoker's, where presentation belongs (rule.forbid.hidden-side-effects). the WARN is on STDERR so `--output json` stdout stays clean for a machine consumer. TRUNCATION SIGNAL under `--output json` (i040-r12-2 fix — the silent-partial-output machine gap the review named): a bare `get` bounds to `--tail 20`, but the `--output json` shape carried ONLY the reply-line array — so a cron could NOT tell "this IS everything" from "this is the last 20 of many". the json shape now carries `{ lines, truncated: boolean, total: number }` — `truncated=true` + the full `total` message count when the tail bound clipped, `truncated=false` when `lines` IS the whole transcript (a `--tail all`, or a transcript shorter than the bound). so a machine reads `total`/`truncated` as FIELDS and knows whether to request more (`--tail all`), never guesses — the SAME machine-consumption discipline the orphan + reachState fields already carry. the BARE-TEXT TREE render is UNCHANGED (it stays pipe-clean for the Slack-relay usecase — the truncation signal is a json-only field, never a line injected into the human/relay text)
│   ├── invokeCloneWhoami.ts                              [+] create (i040-r12-1 fix — the self-identification verb the review named) — `rhx clone whoami [--output tree|json]`: reads `RHACHET_CLONE_SERIAL` (+ `RHACHET_CLONE_SOCKET`) from `process.env` (the vars genBrainCliPtyClone / enrollBrainCli injected at spawn), then RESOLVES that serial to its full Clone via getOneCloneByRef and RENDERS the clone's own address (serial, slug if any, reach-state) — so a clone that self-manages (or a human inside one) learns ITS OWN address in one read, never `clone list` + a fragile pid/cwd match (the wish's "self-management" motive; self-dispatch is DECIDED-allowed, so a clone MUST be able to name itself). NOT-INSIDE-A-CLONE fail-loud: when `RHACHET_CLONE_SERIAL` is absent (the command was run OUTSIDE any enrolled clone — a plain shell) → a ConstraintError that NAMES the cause + fix ("not inside an enrolled clone — RHACHET_CLONE_SERIAL is unset; run this from within a `rhx enroll`-spawned brain, or use `rhx clone list` to see all clones"). the `--output json` shape carries `{ serial, slug, reachState, socketPath, actorHash }` for a clone that self-manages to branch on; composes renderCliOutput like the other verbs. SIBLING-DISCOVERY FIELD — `actorHash` (i046-r12-4 fix — whoami omitted the clone's OWN actor ref, so a clone that self-manages could NOT find its peers): the wish's LEAD motive is "a clone can enumerate and message its peer clones… instead of full-terminal control", and a clone finds its siblings by `rhx clone list @<its-actor>` — but the OLD whoami shape `{ serial, slug, reachState, socketPath }` NEVER exposed the clone's own actor, so a clone that self-manages had NO way to name its actor scope: it would fall back to an UNSCOPED `clone list` and parse/find its own group, the exact decode-friction this codebase forbids elsewhere. `Clone.actor` (the `RefByPrimary<ActorEnrolled>` the hydrator already attaches) carries the actor hash, so whoami now surfaces `actorHash` — a clone runs `rhx clone list @<actorHash>` (the git-style hash-scope reach, asActorRef) to enumerate its siblings directly. a new invokeCloneWhoami.integration + a clone.acceptance sibling-discovery case prove the loop end-to-end: enroll clone A `--as @:driver` and clone B `--as @:builder` of the SAME actor (both buildable in-scope — the CONFIG-SHARED JOINT CLAMP already spawns two named clones of one actor), then from INSIDE clone A run whoami → read `actorHash` → `clone list @<actorHash>` → assert BOTH @:driver and @:builder appear (A discovers B). this closes the untested-sibling-discovery gap the review named: only self-say (a clone that messages ITSELF, [t3-whoami]) was covered; A-discovers-B was not. JSON-ERROR SYMMETRY (i042-r11-1 fix — the just-established cross-command invariant this newest verb must honor too): its NOT-INSIDE-A-CLONE `ConstraintError` failure path composes `asCliErrorJson` under `--output json` — SAME as invokeEnroll (i039) + the four talk verbs (i024) — so `rhx clone whoami --output json` run outside a clone emits the STRUCTURED `{ error: { class, message, hint, reachState: null } }` to STDOUT + exit 2, NOT stderr prose. without this, whoami (born at i040, after the i039 symmetry fix) would be the ONE `--output json` command whose failure is prose — a re-open of the exact machine-consumption inconsistency i024/i039 closed. THIN by design (reads its own env + one getOneCloneByRef) — the self-discovery counterpart to F7's one-time human stderr hint, now a durable, repeatable read
│   └── invoke.ts                                         [~] update — the ROOT CLI registrar: import + call `invokeActor({ program })` and `invokeClone({ program })` beside the extant `invokeEnroll`/`invokeRoles`/`invokeKeyrack` registrations, so `rhx actor`/`rhx clone` are actually REACHABLE (absent this registration the dispatchers exist but the commands 404)
│
