│   │                                                                     · a LIVE clone of THIS SAME actor owns it → REUSE (i052-r11-B1 fix — the hidden-side-effect the fresh holistic pass named): genClone NEVER prints and NEVER calls exit ITSELF — it RETURNS a structured `{ outcome: 'reused' | 'baked' | 'rebound', clone }`, and invokeEnroll renders that outcome via the shared renderCliOutput seam + picks the exit code, EXACTLY as every other enroll outcome does. this honors rule.forbid.hidden-side-effects — the SAME rule getCloneOutput's i023 fix enforced when it stopped a `get*` op from a hidden stderr write, and the SAME reason the six invokers unified under withCliOutputErrors: a domain op that chose its own render + called process.exit is a presentation decision buried below the contract layer, decode-friction inverted. it ALSO closes a real machine-caller regression the earlier print-and-exit carried: that path dropped the `--output json` handoff on every reuse tick, so a cron that runs `rhx enroll <brain> --as @:driver --output json` on a timer (the wish's headline usecase, the exact scenario the C4 accrual-warn polices) read the `{serial, socketEligible, actorHash, socketPath}` handoff ONLY on the FIRST call, then a hardcoded human stderr notice on every reuse tick. the handoff is about REACHABILITY (unchanged on reuse), NOT growth — which is why the audit-log append IS correctly skipped on reuse (i050-r11-3), an asymmetry the review rightly distinguishes: a reuse records no NEW event (skip the append) yet the clone stays reachable (emit the handoff). so the reuse OUTCOME flows the SAME render path as bake/rebind: `--output json` emits the SAME handoff shape REGARDLESS of outcome (a machine reads reachability every tick, never only the first), `--output tree` (default) emits the outcome prose — for reuse the one-line `⛵ @:driver is already live (serial <s>, since <ago>) — reach it with \`rhx clone say @:driver\` / \`rhx clone get @:driver\`; to open a fresh