│   ├── setCloneIdentity({ cloneDir, serial, slug, socketEligible })  [+] communicator — identity.json + per-actor slug symlink; then setCloneSlugIndex for the global slug (fail-loud only on a DIFFERENT-actor collision, per the findsert step above). ORDER (i011-r11-5, the STARTUP-RACE fix): for an ELIGIBLE clone this runs AFTER genCloneSocketServer is LISTENING — so `socketEligible=true` is persisted only once the socket can actually accept a connect. this removes the false-DEAD window the earlier identity-before-bind order carried (a concurrent `clone list` that saw socketEligible=true + a not-yet-bound socket → refused connect → computeCloneReachState reported DEAD, indistinguishable from a crash, contradicting the tri-state legend "DEAD = crashed/exited"). the ordering keeps the tri-state model at THREE states (no STARTING state needed): identity-present ⟹ socket-was-bound ⟹ a refused connect is a GENUINE crash. a fallback clone (socketEligible=false) has no socket + no race, so its identity write order is unconstrained. SLUG-INDEX ORDER (i017-r11-3, INVERTED by i030-r07-1 — the transient-ghost fix): the `.slugs/<slug>` atomic claim (setCloneSlugIndex) now runs BEFORE the final serial-dir rename (i018-r07-1) — the reorder that makes a concurrent-bake loser reap a still-temp-named dir the `serial=*` enumerator never matches, so no transient LIVE ghost row is ever enumerable (rule.forbid.behavior-hazards, deterministic `clone list`). so the write sequence for an eligible named clone is now: socket LISTENING → identity.json written INTO the temp dir (socketEligible=true) → `.slugs/<slug>` atomic claim → rename temp dir → the published `serial=<uuid>/`. this INVERTS the earlier one-directional invariant: it WAS **slug-resolvable ⟹ enumerable-by-serial** (slug written LAST); it is NOW **enumerable-by-serial ⟹ the slug was claimable** (the published serial dir is the most-derived, last-published fact). the new benign lag is the truthful reverse of the old one: a `.slug