│   └── [bare enroll, no --as] emit serial to STDERR once             [+] F7 (i007-r12-6, best-guess + clean rework): a bare `rhx enroll <brain>` gives the human no in-band way to learn their own clone's `@:<serial>` (the mirror is invisible by design). so on a socket-enabled bare enroll, print a COMPACT hint to STDERR, NOT stdout. it names ALL THREE reach verbs, not `say` alone (i017-r12-3 fix — the review's incomplete-breadcrumb gap): `clone @:<serial> — say into it: rhx clone say @:<serial> --what … · read it: rhx clone get @:<serial> · re-find it: rhx clone list`. the `rhx clone list` breadcrumb is the RECOVERY path the review asked for — a human who loses the stderr line runs `clone list` to re-discover every live serial, so the handle is never a dead end. stderr is not the mirror stream, so stdout stays byte-for-byte invisible (item-5 fidelity holds); the human still learns their handle + the full reach vocabulary (pit of success, rule.require.discoverability). NAMED-ENROLL BREADCRUMB TOO (i027-r12-4 fix — the discoverability asymmetry the review named): a `--as @:<slug>` enroll is NOT silent either — it prints a COMPACT breadcrumb that names the reach SYNTAX with the chosen slug: `clone @:<slug> — say into it: rhx clone say @:<slug> --what … · read it: rhx clone get @:<slug>`. the earlier "skipped when `--as` is given (the human already named it)" was the gap: a human who chose the HANDLE still may not know the `clone say`/`clone get` SYNTAX, so a first-time named enroll got a worse discoverability story than a bare one — the exact inconsistency with rule.require.discoverability the review flags. so BOTH forms teach the reach syntax; the difference is only the `rhx clone list` RECOVERY breadcrumb, which the BARE line carries (an auto-serial is easy to lose) and the NAMED line omits (the slug is stable + chosen, so re-discovery is not owed). clean rework: drop the hint for pure silence + `rhx clone list` if the council prefers — one invoker block, no downstream teardown. "INVISIBLE" IS STDOUT-INVISIBLE, NOT TERMINAL-INVISIBLE (i014-r12-c clarification): the fidelity guarantee is precisely that the STDOUT MIRROR is byte-for-byte identical to a plain spawn — the brain's own output stream is unpolluted, so any tool that pipes/captures stdout (a machine, a `| tee`) sees exactly what the brain emitted. F7's one line is DELIBERATELY on stderr BECAUSE the human SHOULD see it (it is the discoverability handle) while a stdout consumer does NOT — the stderr line is intentionally human-visible, not a fidelity leak. so the two claims are consistent, not in tension: stdout is invisible (unpolluted), the stderr serial line is intentionally visible to the human at the terminal. the fidelity test asserts stdout-equality (the real guarantee); it does NOT claim the combined terminal stream is byte-identical, because F7's whole purpose is one intentional human-visible stderr line
├── [any enroll, after the clone is LIVE] emit a soft ACCRUAL WARN when the actor's live-clone count crosses a threshold  [+] i049-r12-1 (best-guess BUILT in v1 + clean rework): the review's last blocker — a bare `rhx enroll` is CREATE-ALWAYS by design (every call spawns a fresh pty+socket+billed brain), so a misconfigured cron that retries (the wish's HEADLINE caller class) silently accrues real-cost brain processes with no cap/warn until a human runs `clone list`. the ledger best-guessed a soft WARN but left it UNBUILT with zero test coverage; it is now BUILT as a v1 deliverable. AFTER genClone publishes the live clone, invokeEnroll composes getOneCloneLiveCountForActor [+] (a NAMED leaf that counts the actor's LIVE clones — getAllClonesForActor over the just-resolved actor → getCloneReachState per clone → tally LIVE — so the invoker never inlines the count pipeline, i057-r03-1) and passes that count to computeCloneAccrualWarn [+] (a PURE transformer, below); when the count EXCEEDS the soft threshold (CLONE_ACCRUAL_WARN_THRESHOLD, a const in utils/cloneAccrualThreshold — a generous default well above a normal parallel-clone burst), invokeEnroll prints a one-line STDERR advisory — `⚠ this actor now has <N> live clones (roles=<roles>); a cron that retries can accrue billed brains unbounded — inspect with 'rhx clone list @<actorHash>', halt stale ones by exit/kill`. it is a SOFT WARN, NOT a hard block: the enroll STILL SUCCEEDS (a genuine burst of parallel clones is a valid use — the wish's own parallel-clone motive — so a hard cap would fight it), the advisory only makes the accrual VISIBLE (rule.require.status-feedback), never prevents it. STDERR (not stdout), so the invisible mirror + the `--output json` machine handoff stay unpolluted; SUPPRESSED under `--output json` unless the json handoff carries it as a structured `accrualWarn: { liveCount } | null` field (a machine reads the count, never scrapes the prose). CLEAN-REWORK FULCRUM (flagged for the council, ledger row 5): the THRESHOLD value + the soft-WARN-vs-hard-cap choice are the wisher's — this ships the soft WARN as the non-disruptive best-guess; the council may (a) tune the threshold, (b) strip the WARN for pure create-always silence, or (c) promote it to the `rhx clone` dream's HARD cap + auto-reap — each a one-block change, no teardown. a clone.acceptance case asserts the WARN fires past the threshold (exit 0 — enroll still succeeds) and is ABSENT below it, and that `--output json` carries the structured `accrualWarn` field
