├── isCloneOrphanProcess({ clone }) per DEAD clone                   [+] communicator (i026-r12-4, the SIGKILL-orphan cost mitigation; PID-REUSE guard added i027-r12-3) — for a socket-DEAD clone ONLY, is its recorded `hostPid` STILL the SAME live brain? returns false unless ALL THREE hold: (a) `clone.hostHash === getHomeHash()` (a foreign pid is not ours to probe); (b) `process.kill(hostPid, 0)` succeeds (signal-0 = an existence check, no signal delivered; ESRCH → dead, EPERM → alive-but-not-ours which on the same host still counts as alive); (c) the LIVE pid's start-time equals the recorded `hostPidStartedAt` (i027-r12-3, THE PID-REUSE GUARD — OS pids recycle, so a pid recorded at bake could later name an UNRELATED process; the child's start-time, read at bake from the process table and re-read at list time, disambiguates the SAME process from a recycled pid, so a long-dead brain whose pid was reused is NOT flagged). a match on all three → an ADVISORY annotation `DEAD (orphan? pid <n> may still be this brain — verify with \`ps <n>\`, then kill to halt charges)` — DELIBERATELY advisory + VERIFY-FIRST, NEVER an imperative "kill it" (i027-r12-3): a false positive must never lead a human to kill an unrelated process, so the hint says VERIFY before kill, not kill-on-sight. a start-time MISMATCH (recycled pid), a dead pid, a foreign host, or a null hostPid (fallback clone) → the plain DEAD line, no annotation. NOT a liveness source (F4 unchanged — reach-state stays socket-derived); this is a render-time ADVISORY on an already-DEAD clone. START-TIME READ is a POSIX best-effort (Linux `/proc/<pid>/stat` field 22, or the BSD/macOS `ps -o lstart`); a host where the start-time cannot be read → the guard treats it as UNVERIFIABLE and SUPPRESSES the annotation (fail-safe: a hint that could misfire is withheld, not shown), so the advisory appears ONLY when the same-process identity is positively confirmed. PURE/IMPURE SPLIT (i042-r11-2 fix — the one multi-condition decision that bundled fetch + classify, breaking this blueprint's own pattern): the CLASSIFICATION — the three-way AND with the pid-reuse guard — is extracted to a PURE `computeCloneOrphanVerdict({ hostMatch, pidAlive, startTimeMatch, startTimeReadable }) → boolean` (the SAME pure-classifier + impure-fetcher shape as computeCloneReachState/getCloneReachState, computeCloneSlugDecision, computeCloneUnreachableHint); isCloneOrphanProcess stays the IMPURE fetcher that reads the host state (getHomeHash, `process.kill(pid,0)`, the start-time lookup) and feeds those booleans to the pure verdict. WHY it matters HERE especially: this is the SAFETY-CRITICAL guard (the one that must never point a human at the wrong process), so its logic earns a FAST, data-driven UNIT test over every branch combination — instead of depending only on integration tests that spawn+kill real processes per branch (slow, and awkward to cover the recycled-pid + unreadable-start-time corners). the fetcher's host reads stay integration-tested; the verdict's LOGIC is unit-tested exhaustively
└── → treestruct stdout, grouped by actor; brain+roles on the ACTOR header (blocker-6), serial+slug+reach-state+since=<ago> per clone line (i005-r12-B: the `since=` column derives from Clone.spawnedAt via a getDuration({ range: { since: spawnedAt, until: now() } }) render — the vision's "since=2m ago"; masked `__AGO__` in snapshots). SPAWN-TIME json-vs-tree SPLIT (i012-r12-4 fix — the machine-consumption shape, previously unspecified): the RELATIVE `since=<ago>` string is a TREE-ONLY render (a human reads "2m ago"; it decays the instant it is read, so it is useless to a machine). the `--output json` shape carries the RAW `spawnedAt` IsoTimeStamp (`2026-08-08T__:__:__Z`, masked here per the no-raw-timestamps rule) — NOT the rendered relative string — because the json result IS the domain Clone (which stores spawnedAt raw), and the relative render is a display transform applied ONLY by asTree. so a cron parses a stable absolute instant and computes its OWN idleness, never re-parses prose that decays. this is the same tree-renders / json-emits-raw discipline renderCliOutput already draws, made explicit for the one time-valued field. ORPHAN FIELD IN JSON (i039-r11-5/r12-1 fix — the machine-consumption gap BOTH l3 lenses named): the SIGKILL-orphan advisory is NOT tree-only — the `--output json` shape ALSO carries a structured `orphan` field per clone: `orphan: { suspectedPid: number } | null`, non-null ONLY when isCloneOrphanProcess positively confirms the recorded hostPid is alive AND same-process on this host (null for a live clone, a cleanly-dead clone, a cross-host DEAD clone, or a recycled-pid). so a CRON — the wish's HEADLINE machine consumer — detects the live-orphan cost hazard (a brain that still draws credits yet reads DEAD) by a FIELD read (`clone.orphan?.suspectedPid`), NEVER by a regex over the tree advisory suffix — the exact machine-consumption purpose `--output json` exists to serve, and the precise hole both lenses flagged (the json shape carried spawnedAt/tri-state/serial/slug but NOT the orphan signal). the field is computed by the SAME isCloneOrphanProcess op the tree advisory uses (ONE owner, both renders off one computation), so tree + json can NEVER disagree about which clone is a suspected orphan. TRI-STATE LEGEND (i010-r12-E, discoverability; DEAD text reconciled i025-r11-1): when ANY clone reads NOT-REACHABLE or DEAD, the output appends a one-line footer legend — `LIVE = socket connectable · DEAD = crashed, exited, or unreachable via a dead socket · NOT-REACHABLE = no socket (--no-socket / non-socket-capable brain)` — so the new vocabulary is self-explaining in-band (rule.require.discoverability: recognition over recall), never a term the human must already know; omitted when all clones are LIVE (no unfamiliar state to explain). the DEAD text ADMITS the "unreachable via a dead socket" case (i025-r11-1): a brain ORPHANED by a `SIGKILL`-ed wrapper (its socket died with the wrapper, though the brain itself neither crashed nor exited — ledger row 9) reads DEAD-by-unreachability, so the reach-state stays HONEST rather than the earlier narrow "crashed/exited" text a still-alive orphan would contradict. ORPHAN-SUFFIX LEGEND (i027-r12-7 fix — the discoverability gap for the new advisory): when ANY DEAD line carries the `(orphan? pid <n> …)` advisory, the footer legend gains a SECOND clause — `orphan? = the wrapper was killed (-9) but this brain pid may still run + bill — verify with \`ps\` before you kill it` — so a human who meets the suffix for the first time gets an in-band gloss of what "orphan" means here, the SAME recognition-over-recall discipline that justifies the base legend (rule.require.discoverability); the clause is present ONLY when a suffix is (no unexplained term, no noise when absent)  [←] reuse the getSkillListTreestruct render pattern (consistent list output)

