├── asCloneRef({ address })                                           [+] transformer — PARSE `@:<x>` → typed ref; fail-loud on wrong-grain `@<actor>` / no-marker
├── getOneCloneByRef({ repoPath, ref })                               [+] communicator — slug|serial → cloneDir
├── getCloneReachState → computeCloneUnreachableHint → fail-loud with a CAUSE-SPECIFIC fix (i006-r12-3/r12-4; i010-r11-A extracts the selection)  [+] the error names the fix per the tri-state, NOT one generic message. the 3-way SELECTION is a NAMED PURE transformer computeCloneUnreachableHint({ reachState, hostHashClone, hostHashCurrent }) → { message, hint } (i010-r11-A: this multi-way branch was inline decode-friction in the invoker — the EXACT shape computeCloneSlugDecision was extracted for, and the exact violation rule.forbid.decode-friction-in-orchestrators / the never-run mech-decode-friction lens guard against; it mirrors computeCloneReachState's split — that op stops at the tri-state, THIS is the absent downstream hint step): NOT-REACHABLE (fallback, socketEligible=false) → "this clone has no socket (--no-socket or a non-socket-capable brain); re-enroll without --no-socket / use a socket-capable brain"; DEAD same-host (hostHashClone === hostHashCurrent) → "re-enroll to spawn a fresh clone" (`wake`, once the dream ships); DEAD cross-host (hostHashClone ≠ hostHashCurrent) → "this clone runs on host <hosthash>, unreachable here; reach it on that host, or enroll a fresh clone here" (r12-4: the cross-host case gets a TRUE hint, not the wrong "re-enroll" advice that would spawn an unrelated duplicate). the invoker COMPOSES the transformer, NOT an inline branch, so the invoker reads as narrative (rule.require.orchestrators-as-narrative) and the hint logic is pure + unit-testable data-driven over the 3 states. unknown ref → fail-loud [2.2 matrix 4]
├── asCloneDispatchFrame({ message })                                      [+] transformer — bracketed-paste + submit [3.1.1 topic-3]
└── sayClone({ cloneDir, bytes })                                     [+] communicator — connectToClone (shared adapter, nitpick-4) + write, THEN await + asCloneDispatchAck the server response (i012-r11-1) → succeed on ACK / fail-loud on NACK
    ├── (server) isSafeCloneDispatchInput gate → genCloneWriteQueue → pty.write  [+] SERVER-side safety, not client-trusted [2.1 uc10]; the write-queue (nitpick-6) serializes concurrent senders so no interleave
    └── (server) asCloneDispatchAckFrame({ ok, reason }) written back    [+] i012-r11-1 — the response half: ACK once pty.write lands, NACK+reason on a gate rejection; the wire that carries the rejection to the caller's exit code
    (DELIVERY CONTRACT — i007-r12-3, scope boundary: `say` is DISPATCH-then-return — it confirms the
     message was DELIVERED to the clone's input (the write landed, or fail-loud if it did not), NOT that
     the brain has finished PROCESSING it. correlation of a dispatch to its matching reply (a dispatch-id
     threaded to a specific answer) and a blocking WAIT-for-idle are a richer request/response protocol
     that belongs to the `rhx clone` dream's lifecycle surface — the SAME scope boundary as the "idle 20m"
     activity signal, deferred there by design. THIS behavior's caller-facing contract is the POLL pattern
     the vision's cron/comms flows already imply: `say` to dispatch, then `get`/`get --tail N` to observe
     the reply — the round-trip uc8 proves end-to-end. flagged so a reviewer reads the absent
     ack-correlation as a scoped deferral, not an omission)

rhx clone get @:<slug|serial> [--tail N]                              [+] invokeCloneGet
├── asCloneRef({ address })                                           [+] transformer — same parse+validate seam (wrong-grain / no-marker fail-loud)
├── getOneCloneByRef({ repoPath, ref })                               [+] communicator
└── getCloneOutput({ cloneDir, tail })                                [+] communicator — MAP+FILTER FIRST, THEN tail (i011-r11-1 fix, the ONE explicit order): concatenate all mtime-ordered episode lines → map each through asCloneOutputText → FILTER OUT the empties (tool-result/thought-block/torn lines) → THEN tail N of the messages that survived. this order is MANDATORY, not incidental: if tail were applied to RAW lines first, `--tail 1` would very often return an empty string (the true last raw line is usually a tool-result/thought-block line asCloneOutputText maps to empty), which breaks the mandated round-trip critipath (`say` → `get --tail 1`, uc8/the clamp) exactly when it must hold. so `--tail N` counts LOGICAL messages (the assistant replies that survived the filter), never raw JSONL lines (i005-r12-C: legible reply text). DEFAULT `tail=20` when the caller omits `--tail` (i013-r12-B): a bare `get` bounds to the last 20 messages, not the whole multi-episode transcript; a large `--tail N` (> available) returns all available (i006-r12-6); `--tail 0`/negative stays invalid (i008-r12-4). RETURNS `{ lines, exidsUnreadable, exidsAmbiguous, total, truncated }` (i040-r12-2: `total` = the FULL logical-message count BEFORE the tail bound, `truncated` = whether the tail clipped it — computed here where the pre-tail count is known, so the invoker's `--output json` shape can carry the truncation signal a cron needs; the tree path ignores both) — NO stderr write here (i023-r07-nitpick1): a reclaimed/vanished episode is REPORTED in-band via `exidsUnreadable`, and the INVOKER (invokeCloneGet) emits the stderr WARN, so this `get*` op stays a pure read with no hidden IO side effect (rule.forbid.hidden-side-effects)
    └── asCloneOutputText({ line })                                   [+] transformer — structured JSONL event → plain-text assistant reply
    (NB: get does NOT gate on isCloneLive — it reads the PERSISTED history, so it succeeds on a DEAD
     clone that has output [matrix 4]; the say/get asymmetry: say needs the live socket, get needs only
     the history. get fails-loud only on an UNKNOWN ref, never on a dead-but-recorded clone.
     ACCESS-CONTROL asymmetry (i007-r11-N3, INTENTIONAL): say's access control is EXPLICIT + tested
     (isCallerSameUser on the socket, i005-blocker-1), because say opens the live socket; get's is
     IMPLICIT — filesystem permissions ALONE — because get never opens the socket, it only reads the
     `history/<exid>.jsonl` symlinks + their targets under the caller's own home. so the same-user bound
     get inherits is the OS file-mode bound on `.agent/.actors/**` + `~/.claude/projects/**`, not a
     caller-cred check. this asymmetry is DELIBERATE, not a gap in the caller-auth story: a read of
     already-persisted, home-scoped transcript files carries no dispatch risk, so it needs no socket-cred
     gate; flagged here so a future reader does not misread the absent isCallerSameUser on get as an
     omission)

rhx init / roles link                                                 [~] syncHooksForLinkedRoles
├── writeClaudeCodeSettings({ to: repoRoot })                         [←] reuse [3.1.3.prod:16]
└── for each actorDir: writeClaudeCodeSettings({ to: actorDir/brain })[~] extend — per-actor application
    (live clones inherit on RESPAWN — claude reads --settings ONCE at launch; no mid-session hot-reload assumed [1.vision edge-case].
     NO write-vs-live-read RACE (still-open i004): the live clone read its settings at spawn and does NOT re-read the file, so a
     re-link's write to that same file cannot corrupt a live session — the write lands for the NEXT spawn, the running process is
     unaffected. writeClaudeCodeSettings writes atomically (temp + rename, the extant dao behavior), so even a concurrent reader at
     spawn sees a whole file, never a half-written one)

  DISAMBIGUATION — usecase.9 "the actor's roles change" (blocker-2 i004): under the anonymous hash-actor model, `roles` ARE the
  identity (hash = genEnrollmentHash({brain,roles})), so there are TWO readings and this behavior delivers exactly one:
  · (b, DELIVERED) the config CONTENT behind an already-fixed roleset refreshes — a role's hooks/briefs change on disk, and a
    re-link re-writes each actor's settings.json so the NEXT clone spawn picks it up. this is what syncHooksForLinkedRoles does.
  · (a, OUT OF SCOPE) the SET of role names on an actor changes in place — impossible under anonymity: a different roleset is a
    different hash, i.e. a DIFFERENT ActorEnrolled by construction; extant clones point at the old hash and never migrate. mutable,
    declared actors (whose roleset can be edited) are the `rhx clone` / `actors.yml` dream's domain. so uc9 reading (a) is scoped
    OUT here and named as the dream's; reading (b) is the "clones stay in sync" guarantee this behavior keeps.
```

### decomposition notes (architect patterns)

- **grains honored** ([define.domain-operation-grains]): transformers pure (`computeBrainCliInput`,
  `genCloneSerial`, `asCloneDispatchFrame`, `getActorEnrolledDir`, `getCloneDir`, `isBrainSocketCapable`);
  communicators own i/o (`sayClone`, `getCloneOutput`, `isCloneLive`, `setCloneIdentity`, `getCloneSocketPath`
  (i020-r04-1: reads host env `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` + `getHomeHash`), the socket
  server, `genBrainCliPtyClone`); orchestrators compose (`findsertActorEnrolled`, `genClone`, the cli
  invokers). orchestrators read as narrative (`rule.require.orchestrators-as-narrative`).
- **directional deps** ([rule.require.directional-deps]): `contract/cli/* → domain.operations/* →
  (fs/pty)`; the cli never reaches the filesystem directly.
- **cross-cluster composition within `domain.operations/` — resolved by placement + directional layer-dependency**
  (i018-r05 articulated, i019-r05 acted): the scopeleaks lens flagged two clone→sibling edges. BOTH are
  now addressed:
  - **the pty-spawn edge (blocker.1) — FIXED by RELOCATION:** the pty primitives moved from `enroll/` to
    `clone/pty/` (i019-r05-1), because their SOLE consumer `genClone` lives in `clone/` — so by
    [rule.prefer.most-common-denominator] they belong WITH their consumer, and `genClone` composes them
    INTRA-cluster now. the cross-folder edge the lens named is GONE (not merely argued away).
  - **the actor/enrolled edge (blocker.2) — a SOUND DOWNSTREAM dependency, articulated:** `clone/`'s
    dependence on `actor/enrolled/` (getOneCloneByRef reads getAllActorsEnrolled) is DIRECTIONAL
    layer-dependency, not a peer reach-in: a `Clone` is spawned FROM, and REFERENCES, an `ActorEnrolled`
    (`Clone.actor` is a `RefByPrimary<ActorEnrolled>`) — clone is built ATOP the enrolled-actor layer,
    the SAME shape as `domain → access` ([rule.require.directional-deps]). the dependency flows ONE way
    (clone → actor/enrolled); actor/enrolled NEVER imports clone, so there is no cycle and no leak. within
    one `domain.operations/` layer, an op's typed signature IS its contract ([rule.forbid.io-as-domain-objects]:
    inline i/o contracts) — so `getOneCloneByRef` reads `getAllActorsEnrolled` THROUGH its contract (the
    signature), exactly as [philosophy.domain-as-a-garden] intends ("orchestrators compose leaf
    operations"). to instead route a downstream layer-dependency through a separate `contract/` file
    would VIOLATE directional-deps (domain.operations must not import from `contract/`) AND add the
    speculative indirection the repo forbids ([rule.forbid.barrel-exports], [rule.prefer.wet-over-dry]) —
    a boundary with zero invariant to protect. `getAllActorsEnrolled` is read by BOTH the actor-list
    invoker AND the clone reads, so it is correctly placed at the actor/enrolled cluster (its own home),
    and its consumers layer atop it — the extant, sanctioned pattern (e.g. `init/` reads `keyrack/`
    in this same repo).
- **idempotency** ([rule.require.idempotent-operations]): `findsertActorEnrolled` (find-or-create the
  actor dir), `genClone` findserts the clone dir, `setCloneIdentity` upserts — a re-enroll of the same
  `{brain,roles}` converges on the same actor; `--as @:<slug>` findserts by slug.
- **fewer paths via idempotency**: `isCloneLive` is the single liveness reader (F4) — no LIVE→DEAD state
  machine, no reap branch; a dead socket speaks for itself.
- **inline i/o contracts** ([rule.forbid.io-as-domain-objects]): every op declares `(input: {...},
  context)` inline; the two new domain objects are `ActorEnrolled` + `Clone` (both genuine persisted
  entities, not i/o bags).
- **two Actor grains, made concrete** ([1.vision "two grains of actor"], blocker-2): the repo now
  carries `Actor` (the extant in-memory SDK composition — live `act`/`run`/`ask`, `brains:
  ActorBrain[]`, keyed `role.slug`; NOT serializable) AND `ActorEnrolled` (the NEW on-disk enrolled
  identity — hash-keyed, persists `{hash,brain,roles}` (i009-r11-1: NO `dir` field — the dir is a PURE
  derivation via getActorEnrolledDir, per i006-r11-B2; the earlier `{brain,roles,dir}` wording here was
  the exact "one decision, two places, one updated" drift and is corrected), the persisted wrapper of the
  `BrainCliEnrollmentManifest` + `genEnrollmentHash` F5 names). `Clone.actor` is a
  `RefByPrimary<ActorEnrolled>`, so the reference has a real referent — the earlier "add only a Clone
  object" was one object short. the two are "one concept, two layers": runtime vs on-disk, never fused.
- **serial vs series** ([1.vision edge-case] — "document how they relate"): a clone's `serial` is its
  IDENTITY (the `RefByPrimary`, one uuid per clone, minted by `genCloneSerial`); its `series` is its
  CONTINUITY — the extant `BrainSeries` of many `BrainEpisode`s bridged by compaction. one `serial`
  owns one `series` that accretes over time, and `history/<exid>.jsonl` holds one symlink per episode
  of that series (the `exid` = the series' latest-episode session id, the resume target). so `serial`
  answers "which clone?" and `series` answers "what has this clone lived through?" — never conflated:
  `getCloneDir` keys on `serial`, `genCloneHistoryLink` records the `series` episodes.
- **on-disk-tree fidelity — reconciled deviations across BOTH cited authorities** (blocker-3: the
  literal labels are superseded by technically-forced decisions; reconciled here against the vision's
  day-in-life tree AND `define.actor-clone-hierarchy.md` — the sibling brief the blueprint cites as
  authority elsewhere — so the two canonical sources no longer disagree silently. the brief itself
  gains a superseding pointer at its diagram; the blueprint edit is the record of why):
  - **roles log = `enrollment.jsonl`, not the `.yml` BOTH the vision tree and the hierarchy brief draw.**
    the vision's day-in-life tree AND `define.actor-clone-hierarchy.md` both draw `roles/enrollment.yml`,
    but the vision's OWN timeline draws `roles/enrollment.jsonl += mechanic`, and the wish's original
    tree said `.jsonl`. an **append-only** log is idiomatic as `.jsonl` — one JSON object per line,
    appended without a doc rewrite — whereas a `.yml` doc must be re-serialized on every append. so
    `.jsonl` is the faithful choice; both `.yml` labels are stale, superseded here (`setActorEnrolledRolesLog`
    writes jsonl). the hierarchy brief's diagram is updated to match, with a superseding note.
  - **socket lives in `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, not inside the clone dir BOTH sources draw.** the vision tree
    AND the hierarchy brief draw `serial=…/socket` (the socket as a file in the clone dir). a unix domain
    socket path is capped at ~104 chars (`sun_path`), and a repo-nested
    `.agent/.actors/actor.via.hash=…/clones/serial=…/socket` blows that cap. so F2 puts the socket at
    `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/clone.$serial.$homeHash.sock` — mirroring the extant `getKeyrackDaemonSocketPath` —
    and the clone dir keeps identity + history only. the vision's intent (a per-clone socket whose
    liveness IS the clone's) is honored exactly; only its drawn LOCATION moves, for a hard OS constraint
    (`Clone.socketEligible` records WHETHER a socket was stood up; the runtime path is derived from
    serial at read time, never stored — i005-nitpick-2). the hierarchy brief's
    diagram is updated to match, with a superseding note.
- **brain/roles render at the ACTOR-group header, not the clone line** (blocker-6: the mandated worked
  example vs the data model, resolved): `clone list` groups clones under their actor (getAllClonesGroupedByActor),
  and brain+roles ARE the actor's identity (the hash IS genEnrollmentHash({brain,roles})), stored on
  `ActorEnrolled`, never on `Clone` — so the GROUPED rendering shows `brain=… roles=…` ONCE on the actor
  header, and each clone line shows serial+slug+reach-state. the vision day-in-life tree AND
  `inventory.case=4` draw `brain=claude roles=mechanic` on the flat clone line — that is the
  pre-grouping illustrative form, superseded by the grouped output (DRY: brain/roles are not repeated
  per clone). the mandated clamp (clone.acceptance) + the `clone list` snapshot both assert the GROUPED
  rendering, so the winner is picked and tested, not left to drift.
- **the derived actor is accommodated, not built** ([1.vision max-boundary edge]): a role-delta clone
  (`actor.via.slug=builder._.delta=$hash`) is the `rhx clone` dream's command, out of scope here — but
  the frame REPRESENTS it without change: `Clone.actor` is a `RefByPrimary<ActorEnrolled>` that points at
  an actor by its hash key, whether that actor is a plain hash actor (enroll) or a derived one whose hash
  is `genEnrollmentHash(delta)` (the dream). the frame carries the identity shape; the dream adds the
  command.

---

## test coverage

> **the paramount priority** (vision): exhaustive critipath coverage — every happy AND edge path of
> [2.1]/[2.2], proven by real tests, no gaps. an assertion (and, for user-faced stdout, a snapshot) on
> every critipath. the socket is proven against a **real stub brain** (blackbox acceptance), never a
> mock ([rule.forbid.acceptance.mocks]).

### coverage by layer

| layer | codepaths | test type |
|-------|-----------|-----------|
| transformers | `computeBrainCliInput`, `getBrainCliPassthroughArgs`, `genCloneSerial`, `isSafeCloneSlug` (i008-r11-B2), `computeCloneSlugDecision` (i008-r11-N1), `computeCloneUnreachableHint` (i010-r11-A), `asCloneRef`, `asActorRef`, `getActorEnrolledDir`, `getCloneDir`, `computeCloneReachState` (i007-r11-B1), `isCloneSocketEligible`, `asCloneDispatchFrame`, `isSafeCloneDispatchInput`, `asCloneDispatchAckFrame` (i012-r11-1), `asCloneDispatchAck` (i012-r11-1), `asCloneOutputText`, `isBrainSocketCapable` (i008-r11-B1, a thin projection of getSupportedBrainCommand's `.socket` field) | **unit** (`.test.ts`, pure, no mocks) |
| communicators | `getOneRepoPath` (i040-r11-B1, the canonicalization single-owner — reads the fs via realpathSync), `findsertActorEnrolled`*, `setActorEnrolledRolesLog`, `getAllActorsEnrolled`, `getOneActorEnrolledByRef`, `getCloneSocketPath` (i020-r04-1: reads host env, so a communicator not a transformer), `setCloneIdentity`, `getCloneIdentity` (i015-r12-4), `getOneCloneHydrated` (i017-r11-2), `setCloneSlugIndex`, `getOneCloneSlugReconciled` (i012-r11-2), `isInteractiveTty`, `genCloneHistoryLink`, `getOneCloneByRef`, `getAllClonesForActor`, `getOneCloneLiveCountForActor` (i057-r03-1, the impure live-count leaf extracted out of invokeEnroll's accrual step), `connectToClone`, `isCloneLive`, `getCloneReachState` (i007-r11-B1, the impure caller), `isCloneOrphanProcess` (i026-r12-4 + i027-r12-3, the SIGKILL-orphan detection with the PID-reuse guard), `genCloneSocketServer`, `genCloneWriteQueue`, `sayClone`, `getCloneOutput`, `genBrainCliPtyClone` | **integration** (`.integration.test.ts`, real fs/socket/pty, real stub brain) |
| orchestrators | `findsertActorEnrolled`, `getAllClonesGroupedByActor`, `genClone` | **integration** |
| contracts | `invokeEnroll`, `invokeActor`(+`invokeActorList`), `invokeClone`(+`invokeCloneList`/`Say`/`Get`) | **integration** + **acceptance** (blackbox) |

### coverage by case (per the matrices)

| codepath | positive | negative | edge |
|----------|----------|----------|------|
| `computeBrainCliInput` | absent→default, `--brain`, positional, flag+positional agree (matrix 1) | flag+positional differ → fail-loud; no brain + empty allowlist → fail-loud (**the owed gap**, matrix 1) | delta-token collision with `--as` |
| `getBrainCliPassthroughArgs` | strips resolved brain (positional + `--brain` pair), `--as` pair, `--no-socket` (uc3 passthrough) | — | absent brain → no brain token stripped; a real brain flag (`--model opus`) survives; enroll flags never leak into child argv |
| `asCloneRef` | `@:<uuid>` → serial ref; `@:<name>` → slug ref | bare `@<actor>` (wrong-grain) → fail-loud; no-`@:` string → fail-loud | empty body after `@:` → fail-loud |
| `asActorRef` (PURE parse) | `@<actor>` → hash-or-prefix literal ref (the `<actor>` body is the anonymous-actor hash literal, blocker-4) | `@:<clone>` (wrong-grain) → fail-loud; no-`@` string → fail-loud | — |
| `getOneActorEnrolledByRef` (DISK, i006-r11-B1) | full hash → exact dir; a UNIQUE git-style prefix (`9c1e…`) → the one actor it prefixes | an AMBIGUOUS prefix (2+ actors) → fail-loud, NAMES the candidate hashes + fix (lengthen); a no-match → fail-loud | — |
| `computeCloneReachState` (PURE, i007-r11-B1) | `{socketEligible:true, live:true}` → LIVE | `{socketEligible:true, live:false}` → DEAD | `{socketEligible:false}` → NOT-REACHABLE (distinct from DEAD) |
| `getCloneReachState` (impure caller, i007-r11-B1) | live socket → LIVE | refused socket → DEAD | socketEligible **false** → NOT-REACHABLE (no socket call) |
| `setCloneSlugIndex` (blocker-1) | `--as @:driver` writes `.slugs/driver` → owning clone | a SECOND actor's `--as @:driver` → fail-loud ConstraintError, names the owning actor + fix, NO overwrite | null slug → no index entry (unreachable by slug, only serial) |
| enroll → actor+clone | roles-linked → actor dir + live clone (matrix 2) | no `.agent/` → fail-loud "roles link first" | same roleset twice → same actor; roleset A vs B → distinct actors (matrix 3) |
| `--as` name | `@:<slug>` → clone reachable by slug+serial (matrix 2) | `--as <value>` without `@:` → fail-loud; a slug already owned by ANOTHER actor's live clone → fail-loud (blocker-1 global collision); **a UUID-shaped slug body → fail-loud at enroll, names the fix** (i005-r12-D: asCloneRef routes a uuid-shaped `@:<x>` to the SERIAL branch, so a uuid-shaped custom slug could never be looked up as a slug again — reject it up front, "a --as slug may not be UUID-shaped; pick a non-uuid name"); **a path-unsafe slug body (`@:../../evil`, `@:a/b`, `@:.hidden`) → fail-loud at enroll via isSafeCloneSlug** (i008-r11-B2: the slug builds a real path in setCloneSlugIndex, so a traversal body is rejected before it reaches path construction, names the fix `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-_]*`); **the role-tweak-same-handle DEAD-END (i010-r12-A): `--as @:driver` with roleset A, then `--as @:driver` with a CHANGED roleset B → a DIFFERENT actor hash → cross-actor collision → fail-loud, fix = "pick a different --as name (e.g. @:driver-2)"** (a cross-actor slug REASSIGNMENT — keep the handle, move it to the new-roles actor — is the `rhx clone` dream's lifecycle, NOT available here; the in-scope action is a new name). named as a known dead-end, not left implicit | absent `--as` → serial-only, no slug index entry |
| dead-slug re-bind (i005-nitpick-5) | re-`enroll --as @:driver` on a LIVE same-actor owner → REUSE the extant clone (i004 findsert, continuity kept) | — | re-`enroll --as @:driver` on a DEAD same-actor owner → a FRESH serial with an EMPTY `history/` (a NEW clone, NOT a resume — there is no continuity with the dead serial; the dead clone's dir becomes an orphan reachable only by its OLD serial). enroll emits a one-line notice so this is never mistaken for `wake` (the dream's resume verb, which alone reopens the old serial's series). documented here so the absent-continuity is an EXPECTED, announced outcome, not a silent history loss |
| `clone list` | live clone shown; unscoped GROUPS by actor across many actors, brain+roles on the ACTOR header (blocker-6), serial+slug+reach-state per clone line; scoped `@<actor>` resolves a HASH-actor ref (matrix 4) | unknown `@<actor>` scope → fail-loud (names the fix) | tri-state (blocker-5): live→LIVE, crashed (orphan/absent socket)→DEAD, fallback (socketEligible=false)→NOT-REACHABLE — three DISTINCT states; concurrent clones distinct (matrix 7) |
| `clone say` | live: message lands + brain acts; by slug ≡ serial (matrix 4); **SELF-DISPATCH positive path (i010-r12-D): a clone `say`s its OWN serial → the message LANDS on its own input** (self-management is a wish motive, decided allowed+unguarded — this asserts the positive path WORKS, not only that the same-user gate does not block it) | fail-loud, never drops, with a CAUSE-SPECIFIC fix (i006-r12-3/r12-4): DEAD same-host → "re-enroll"; NOT-REACHABLE (fallback) → "no socket, re-enroll without --no-socket / use a capable brain" (DISTINCT message, not the crash one); DEAD cross-host (foreign hostHash) → "runs on host X, unreachable here" (not the wrong same-host advice); wrong-grain (`@` actor, not `@:` clone) → fail-loud; unsafe input (raw-escape / paste-escape `\e[201~`) → rejected + fail-loud (uc10); an empty `--what ""` (literal) → fail-loud, a message is required (i008-r12-4); a peer of a DIFFERENT user → rejected by isCallerSameUser BEFORE any byte (i005-blocker-1 caller-auth) | two concurrent `say` → single-writer, no interleave (matrix 7) |
| `clone get` | recent output; `--tail N`; by slug ≡ serial; **get on a DEAD clone with output → returns output** (get reads persisted history, does NOT gate on liveness — matrix 4 line 79); **get on a FALLBACK clone → returns its linked history** (i008-r12-1b, both-branch history link); **`--tail N` > available history → returns ALL available** (i006-r12-6: never errors, never pads — the declared contract) | unknown serial → fail-loud; wrong-grain (`@` actor) → fail-loud; **invalid `--tail` (negative / zero / non-numeric) → fail-loud, names the fix** (i008-r12-4) | empty history → empty, not a false read; `--tail N` on empty → empty (distinct from unknown-ref fail-loud) |
| reach round-trip | enroll `--as` → say → get by slug AND serial; actor stays anonymous (matrix 5) | — | the mandated clamp critipath ([rule.require.clamp-edge-cases]) |
| init re-link | config to root AND each actor dir (matrix 6) | — | no actors → root only |
| fallback | no-pty/non-capable/headless/`--no-socket` → plain spawn + notice + `Clone.socketEligible=false` (matrix 7, [1.vision open-q #6]) | a fallback (socketEligible=false) clone → getCloneReachState → NOT-REACHABLE in `clone list`; `say` to it → fail-loud, names the fix (uc10) | `get` on a fallback clone with recorded history still returns output — genCloneHistoryLink runs on the FALLBACK branch too (i007-r12-1), so the plain-spawn clone's own transcript is linked and get reads it (get reads history, not the socket) |
| `--output json` (i007-r12-2) | `clone list`/`clone get`/`clone say`/`actor list` with `--output json` → machine-parseable JSON of the same domain result the `tree` branch renders; asserted programmatically parseable (JSON.parse round-trips to the expected shape) | `--output <unknown>` → fail-loud, names `tree\|json`; **a talk verb that FAILS under `--output json` → a STRUCTURED error `{ error: { class, message, hint, reachState } }` to STDOUT with the error's own non-zero exit (i024-r12-1): `say @:<dead>` → `reachState:'DEAD'`; `say @:<fallback>` → `reachState:'NOT-REACHABLE'`; cross-host → `reachState:'DEAD'` + a cross-host hint; unknown-ref / ambiguous-prefix / malformed-`--as` → `reachState:null` — so a cron branches on `error.reachState` + `error.class`, never on stderr prose** | default (no `--output`) → `tree`; the `json` SUCCESS shape carries the tri-state, serial, slug, brain, roles, and the RAW `spawnedAt` IsoTimeStamp (i012-r12-4: the absolute instant, NOT the tree-only relative `since=<ago>`) — so a cron parses stable fields, not glyphs and not prose that decays on read; the `tree` failure path is UNCHANGED (human stderr prose + exit code) — only the `json` failure path emits the structured error object |
| bare-enroll serial line (i007-r12-6, F7) | a bare `rhx enroll <brain>` (no `--as`, socket-enabled) → ONE `clone @:<serial> — reach it with …` line on STDERR; STDOUT stays byte-for-byte invisible (finding-5 fidelity co-asserted) | — | `--as` given → the line is skipped (human already named it); a fallback (non-socket) bare enroll → the line still names the serial for `get`, since get works on a fallback clone |
| headless + `--as` (i004-r12 finding-8) | `-p` one-shot (non-interactive) + `--as @:driver` → the slug is accepted + recorded, but the clone is NOT socket-reachable, so enroll emits a WARN ("--as slug set but this clone is not socket-reachable; it will list NOT-REACHABLE and `say` will fail") | `say @:driver` → fail-loud (NOT-REACHABLE) | `get @:driver` still reads its history (the slug is not useless — get works, say does not) |
| `clone say --what @stdin` (i004-r12 finding-3) | `--what @stdin` reads a multi-line / quote-heavy payload from stdin, dispatched whole | empty stdin → fail-loud (a message is required) | a multi-line body is framed as ONE bracketed-paste dispatch, not line-by-line |
| socket 0700 mode (i004-r12 finding-4) | the bound socket is created mode 0700 (owner-only), asserted | — | a group/other-readable socket would be a leak — the assertion is the enforcement proof behind the "small blast radius" pitch |
| hot-path fidelity (i004-r12 finding-9) | enroll THROUGH the socket produces the SAME visible stdout as a plain spawn — the "zero added friction / invisible mirror" headline, proven as a first-class acceptance assertion (not only a manual playtest) | — | asserted byte-for-byte with NO extra line against a plain-spawn baseline (i007-r12-5: the SUCCESS socket path is TRULY invisible — uc1 "exactly as today, zero added friction" — so it prints NO notice; the one-line notice belongs ONLY to the FALLBACK path, where it announces the degradation. the earlier "modulo the one-line socket notice" wording was the internal contradiction the reviewer named — corrected: success = zero extra bytes, fallback = one notice line) |
| cross-host (uc10) | — | machine-B `say`/`get` on a machine-A clone → fail-loud (isCloneLive → DEAD) | the mechanism is the **host-local socket** (getCloneSocketPath carries a per-host hash under `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, off-repo tmpfs), so machine B never resolves machine A's socket — this REPLACES the vision's `status.json` host-field guard, dropped by F4 (socket-derived liveness); tested in isCloneLive. TWO SUB-CASES, BOTH FAIL-LOUD (i014-r12-e clarification — uc10's given says "second machine OR worktree"): (1) SHARED clone-tree, cross-MACHINE (the `.agent/.actors/` tree is on a shared/synced filesystem so machine B CAN read machine A's identity.json) → getOneCloneByRef FINDS the clone, isCloneLive can't reach the host-local socket → DEAD, and the recorded `hostHash` ≠ B's getHomeHash → the CROSS-HOST hint ("runs on host <x>"); (2) SEPARATE trees — a second git WORKTREE on the SAME host, OR a cross-machine checkout that does NOT share `.agent/.actors/` (the default, since the whole tree is gitignored so it is NEVER synced by git) → worktree/checkout B has its OWN empty `.agent/.actors/`, so getOneCloneByRef finds no such clone → UNKNOWN-REF fail-loud. so the `hostHash` hint covers the shared-tree case; the separate-tree case (incl. the literal same-host worktree) is the unknown-ref path — BOTH fail loud + safe (never a silent wrong-target), which is exactly what uc10 requires. the two are DIFFERENT error texts by design (a cross-host hint needs a shared record to read the hostHash from; without a shared record there is simply no clone to hint about) |
| resize (uc10) | terminal resized mid-run → SIGWINCH → pty.resize re-flows the child, no visible degradation | — | tested in genBrainCliPtyClone |

\* `findsertActorEnrolled` is both an orchestrator and a communicator boundary — integration-tested.

**test form — transformers use data-driven caselists** ([rule.prefer.data-driven]): the pure
transformers (`computeBrainCliInput` with matrix-1's 6 rows, `asCloneDispatchFrame` frame+edge cases,
`isSafeCloneDispatchInput` accept/reject cases, `isBrainSocketCapable` per-brain rows) are declared as
`TEST_CASES`-driven tables, so each case is one row and a new case is one line — the exhaustive-case
mandate stays cheap to extend.

### snapshots (mandatory, exhaustive)

each acceptance error case also ASSERTS the exit code alongside the snapshot (ConstraintError → exit 2,
MalfunctionError → exit 1, success → exit 0), per [rule.require.exit-code-semantics] — so a wrong exit
code is caught even when the stderr text is masked. THE NOTICE/WARN PATHS ASSERT EXIT 0 TOO (i017-r12-4
fix — the review's gap: a stderr NOTICE is not an error, so a regression that turns it into a hard
failure would slip past a snapshot alone): the fallback-notice, the addon-load-failure notice, the
dead-slug-REBIND notice, the headless-`-p`+`--as` WARN, and the F7 bare-enroll serial hint each pair
their stderr snapshot with an EXPLICIT `exit 0` assertion — because all five are ADVISORY lines on a
SUCCESSFUL enroll (the clone spawned; the line only informs), so exit 0 is the contract. without the
paired assertion, a notice→exit-1 regression (a fallback that starts hard-failing) would pass the text
snapshot while breaking the "enroll never hard-fails on a degraded path" guarantee (uc10). so the exit
code is asserted on BOTH sides of the success/error line: error paths exit 1/2, advisory-on-success
paths exit 0.

**every snapshot is PAIRED with a functional assertion** ([rule.require.snapshots] "use both";
[rule.forbid.failhide] forbids snapshot-only): a `toMatchSnapshot()` never stands alone — each is joined
by an explicit assertion on the behavior (e.g. clone.acceptance asserts the `ack:<nonce>` value AND
snapshots the tree; `clone list` asserts the LIVE/DEAD state AND snapshots). the snapshot is for visual
PR review; the assertion is the functional gate.

**expensive real-clone spawns are SHARED, never re-spawned per assertion**
([rule.forbid.redundant-expensive-operations], [rule.require.useThen-useWhen-for-shared-results]): a real
enroll+spawn is costly, so each acceptance/integration test spawns ONCE via `useBeforeAll` and shares the
live clone across its many `then` assertions (say-then-get, list, by-slug, by-serial) — the round-trip
uses `useThen`/`useWhen` to thread one spawn's result through the peer assertions, never a fresh enroll
per `then` (no `let` mutation, no redundant spawn).

acceptance tests snapshot every contract stdout/stderr via `asSnapshotSafe` (extended with masks for
`serial` uuid → `__SERIAL__`, socket path → `__SOCKET__`, relative time → `__AGO__`; the actor **hash is
deterministic**, so it is NOT masked — [3.1.3.test:2]):

- **enroll:** conflict error, no-`.agent/` error, malformed `--as` error, **UUID-shaped `--as` error** (i005-r12-D), **path-unsafe `--as` error** (i008-r11-B2, the traversal-body reject), **empty-allowlist / no-default-brain error** (the owed-gap negative, matrix 1), **global slug-collision error** (a second actor's `--as @:driver`, blocker-1), **success = NO notice on stdout** (i008-r12-1a corrected: r12-5 settled the success socket path prints ZERO extra bytes — the stale "success notice" bullet is dropped; the snapshot asserts the mirror stdout carries no added line), **fallback notice** (the ONE notice, on the fallback path only), **bare-enroll serial STDERR line** (i008-r12-1c: the F7 `clone @:<serial> — reach it with …` line — genuinely new user-facing stderr, so it earns a mandatory snapshot, not only an integration assertion), **dead-slug-REBIND notice** (i006-r12-1: the "fresh serial, history-less, not a wake" one-line notice — snapshotted so its proof IS its regression guard, per rule.require.clamp-edge-cases; an integration assertion on internal state alone could not catch a dropped print), **headless-`-p` + `--as` WARN** (i006-r12-1: the "slug set but not socket-reachable" warn line, snapshotted for the same reason)
- **actor list:** populated tree (with a COPYABLE hash column — the tree shows the git-style ABBREVIATED 12-char prefix, i013-r12-C; the full hash appears only in `--output json`), empty tree WITH the get-started breadcrumb (i052-r12-5: the empty tree carries `run 'rhx enroll <brain>' to enroll one`, asserted present, so an empty state is never a dead end), **`--output json`** (i008-r12-2: the raw SUBPROCESS stdout JSON.parse-es clean — no ANSI/log/stray-byte contamination — AND carries the FULL 64-char hash, i013-r12-C; the EMPTY json case is `[]`, no breadcrumb prose, i052-r12-5)
- **clone list:** live+dead+not-reachable mixed (the tri-state, blocker-5) WITH the footer legend present (i010-r12-E), grouped-by-actor with brain+roles on the actor header (blocker-6) + `since=<ago>` column (i005-r12-B, masked `__AGO__`), **all-LIVE with the legend OMITTED** (i013-r12-E: the legend's ABSENCE case — a list where every clone is LIVE must NOT print the footer legend; the mixed case proves it appears, this proves it is correctly withheld, so a regression that always-prints or never-prints the legend goes red), **DEAD-with-ORPHAN-ADVISORY** (i027-r12-1/3/7, blocker-1 — the orphan mitigation had NO snapshot: a socket-DEAD clone whose `hostPid` is confirmed alive-AND-same-process → the line carries the advisory `DEAD (orphan? pid <n> may still be this brain — verify with ps, then kill to halt charges)` AND the footer legend gains the `orphan? = …` clause; pid masked `__PID__`. a COMPANION snapshot where the DEAD clone's pid is DEAD or RECYCLED (start-time mismatch) → the PLAIN DEAD line, NO advisory, NO orphan legend clause — so the annotation + its legend clause appear ONLY on positive same-process confirmation, and a false-positive regression goes red), scoped, **empty (no clones anywhere) WITH the get-started breadcrumb** (i052-r12-5: the unscoped no-clones list carries `run 'rhx enroll <brain>' to spawn one`, asserted present; the `--output json` empty case is `[]`, no prose), **scoped `@<actor>` to a VALID actor with ZERO clones → an EMPTY clone list, NOT an error** (i025-r12-minor: distinct from "unknown actor → fail-loud" and from "no actors at all → empty" — a real actor that simply has no live-or-dead clones yet returns a clean empty list under its header, its own snapshot, so a caller never confuses "actor exists, no clones" with "no such actor"), **unknown-`@<actor>`-scope error**, **ambiguous-hash-prefix error** (names both candidates, i006-r11-B1), **`--output json`** (i008-r12-2: subprocess stdout parses to the expected array), **`--output json` STRUCTURED error** (i024-r12-1: an ambiguous-prefix scope under `--output json` → the `{ error: { class:'ConstraintError', message, hint, reachState:null } }` object on STDOUT + exit 2, JSON.parses clean — the machine gets the candidate hint as a field, not stderr prose), **unknown `--output` value error**
- **clone say:** DEAD-same-host error, **NOT-REACHABLE (fallback) error** (distinct message, i006-r12-3), **cross-host DEAD error** (foreign hostHash, distinct hint, i006-r12-4), unknown-serial error, **wrong-grain (`@` actor) error**, **unsafe-input rejected error** (raw-escape / paste-escape, uc10), **different-user-peer rejected error** (i005-blocker-1 caller-auth), **empty `--what ""` (literal) error** (i008-r12-4), **empty `--what @stdin` (empty pipe) error** (i011-r12-C6: the empty-stdin negative was TESTED but absent from this enumeration — added, distinct from the literal empty-string case), **`--output json` STRUCTURED error** (i024-r12-1: a `say @:<dead>` under `--output json` → the `{ error: { class, message, hint, reachState:'DEAD' } }` object on STDOUT + exit 2; the raw SUBPROCESS stdout JSON.parses clean and `error.reachState` is asserted DEAD, so the machine-error shape is snapshotted, not only the success ack), success ack
- **clone get:** tail output, `--tail N` > history → all-available (i006-r12-6), **invalid `--tail` (negative / zero / non-numeric) error** (i008-r12-4), unknown-serial error, **wrong-grain error**, empty-history output, **get-on-a-fallback-clone → returns its linked history** (i008-r12-1b: the r12-1 blocker-grade both-branch-history fix earns its own mandatory snapshot), **get-on-a-DEAD-clone → returns its recorded output** (the say/get asymmetry, matrix 4 l79), **`--output json`** (i008-r12-2: subprocess stdout parses to the reply lines), **`--output json` STRUCTURED error** (i024-r12-1: an `get @:<unknown>` under `--output json` → the `{ error: { class, message, hint, reachState:null } }` object on STDOUT + exit 2, JSON.parses clean)
- **`--help` (i011-r12-C3 — the promised-but-absent snapshots, now enumerated):** `rhx enroll --help`, `rhx actor --help`, `rhx actor list --help`, `rhx clone --help`, `rhx clone list --help`, `rhx clone say --help`, `rhx clone get --help` — EACH snapshotted, EACH asserted to document its new flags (`--brain`/`--as`/`--no-socket`/`--output` on enroll; `--output` on the list/get/say verbs; `--what`/`--what @stdin` on say; `--tail` on get; the `@<actor>` / `@:<slug|serial>` address grammar) with a `.what` line + a worked example — the first-class proof behind the help-on-demand adherence item (rule.require.help-on-demand), so "documented" is asserted, not only "functional"

### test tree

```
src/domain.objects/
├── ActorEnrolled.test.ts                                  [+] unit — on-disk actor entity shape; primary=['repoPath','hash'], unique=['repoPath','hash'] (i037-r12-1: the repo-scoped identity); persists `{repoPath,hash,brain,roles}` — NO `dir` field (i009-r11-1: dir is derived via getActorEnrolledDir, never persisted — the corrected shape); the hash IS genEnrollmentHash({brain,roles}) (blocker-2); + CROSS-REPO IDENTITY GUARD (i037-r12-1, the review's blocker #1): TWO records with the SAME {brain,roles} (so the SAME hash) but DIFFERENT `repoPath` → their `getUniqueIdentifier` / RefByPrimary keys DIFFER, asserted at the domain-objects library boundary, so a bare `Ref<ActorEnrolled>` can NEVER conflate the two-repo pair (dogfooded: revert primary to ['hash'] alone → the two refs collide + the assertion goes red); + HASH-CONTENT INVARIANT GUARD (i009-r11-3, the F6 pit-of-failure): asserts the hash === genEnrollmentHash({brain,roles}) for the record's OWN fields — so a record whose hash does not match its content (the exact bug a stray `.clone({brain:…})` on the DomainEntity would mint) is caught. this VERIFIES the "immutable by construction / content-addressed" invariant rather than only declaring it (F6 resolution (b): keep DomainEntity but test the invariant; if the council picks DomainLiteral, the guard still holds)
└── Clone.test.ts                                          [+] unit — entity shape; primary=['serial'], unique=['slug'] (blocker-1); slug null-ok AND two slug=null clones NEVER collide as a unique match — VERIFIED at the domain-objects LIBRARY BOUNDARY (i007-r11-N2): assert `getUniqueIdentifier` / the DomainEntity equality machinery treats a null slug as "NO key", NOT a literal shared key value (so two null-slug clones are distinct), pinned directly at the library, not only at getOneCloneByRef; socketEligible: boolean (false for a fallback clone — NOT a stored path, i005-nitpick-2 / blocker-5); spawnedAt is an IsoTimeStamp (i005-r12-B, the `since=` referent); hostHash: string (i007-r11-B2, persisted); actor + historyDir are hydration-derived, NOT persisted (i007-r11-B2); Clone.actor is a RefByPrimary<ActorEnrolled> over the COMPOSITE {repoPath, hash} key (i039-r11-B1): asserted the ref carries BOTH repoPath AND hash, so it matches ActorEnrolled's composite primary — a hash-only ref would fail to equal the two-repo-distinct ActorEnrolled and re-open the cross-repo collision (dogfooded: a hash-only Clone.actor ref against two same-hash different-repo ActorEnrolleds resolves ambiguously → the assertion goes red)

src/infra/host/
└── getOneRepoPath.integration.test.ts                     [+] integration (i040-r11-B1) — the CANONICALIZATION guard: a cwd that is a SYMLINK to the real repo → getOneRepoPath returns the REAL (symlink-followed) path, NOT the symlink string; a cwd WITH a trailing slash → the stripped path; the SAME repo reached by TWO equivalent paths (a symlink + the real path) → getOneRepoPath returns the IDENTICAL string for both (the fragmentation-closing property). DOGFOODED the composite-key convergence: build two ActorEnrolled records from the SAME {brain,roles} where one caller passed a symlinked cwd + the other the real cwd, BOTH threaded through getOneRepoPath → the two records share ONE identity key (a hash-only or a raw-cwd repoPath would fragment into two → the assertion goes red), so the i037/i039 composite key is proven SAFE-by-canonicalization, not fragment. an integration test (not unit) because realpathSync crosses the filesystem boundary (rule.forbid.unit.remote-boundaries); + STALE-CWD FAIL-LOUD (i048-r11-1/r12): a cwd `rmdir`'d out from under a running process → getOneRepoPath throws a MalfunctionError that NAMES the dir + the fix ("pruned git worktree or deleted dir; cd into a live checkout"), NOT a raw ENOENT — dogfooded by a temp dir removed BEFORE the op runs, so the failure path is proven, the exact coverage the review found absent

src/domain.operations/enroll/                            (the PURE enroll transformers — paths now MATCH the filediff, i044-r11-1)
├── computeBrainCliInput.test.ts                              [+] unit — 3-form derive + conflict fail-loud (matrix 1)
└── getBrainCliPassthroughArgs.test.ts                        [+] unit — data-driven: strips the positional brain by value; strips the `--brain <b>` PAIR; strips `--as @:<slug>` PAIR; strips lone `--no-socket`; absent brain → no brain token stripped; a genuine brain passthrough flag (e.g. `--model opus`) SURVIVES untouched — proves enroll's flags never leak into the child argv and the brain's own flags are never eaten

src/infra/enroll/                                        (the IMPURE enroll ops — colocated tests match the infra/enroll/ filediff paths, i044-r11-1: the earlier placement under domain.operations/enroll/ was the path-drift the review named; these three are boundary ops, so their tests live beside them in infra/)
