src/domain.operations/clone/pty/                          (the pty-spawn primitive tests — RELOCATED here from enroll/ with their ops, i019-r05-1)
├── isBrainSocketCapable.test.ts                           [+] unit — per-brain socket gate (boolean): capable brain (claude) → true; non-capable / unknown brain → false (reads the shared getSupportedBrainCommand registry)
├── isCloneSocketEligible.test.ts                          [+] unit (nitpick-4 i004; +disjunction i042-r11-5) — data-driven: capable+interactive+socket-on → true; non-capable → false; non-interactive (`-p`) → false; `--no-socket` → false; + THE DISJUNCTION: capable + NON-interactive + `outputIsJson:true` → TRUE (a headless `--output json` machine spawn opts in); capable + NON-interactive + `outputIsJson:false` → FALSE; `outputIsJson:true` + `--no-socket` → FALSE (no-socket still wins) — the `interactive OR outputIsJson` rule proven in the one pure owner
├── isInteractiveTty.integration.test.ts                   [+] integration (nitpick-4 i004) — isTTY true under a pty, false under a pipe (the `interactive` source)
├── getPtyModuleOrNull.integration.test.ts                 [+] integration (i011-r12-C1) — with node-pty installed → returns the module (non-null); with the require forced to throw a MODULE_NOT_FOUND / native-load error (injected via a mock resolver that simulates the absent addon) → returns null, NOT a throw; a NON-load error (e.g. a syntax fault) → RE-THROWS (rule.forbid.failhide — only the addon-load error is allowlisted)
└── genBrainCliPtyClone.integration.test.ts                [+] integration — real stub brain: spawn, socket, exid capture; BOTH directions of the invisible stream (uc1 "inherits the terminal exactly as today"): pty.onData → process.stdout MIRROR, AND process.stdin → pty.write PASSTHROUGH (a human keystroke reaches the brain); + RESIZE: a SIGWINCH → pty.resize propagates the new cols/rows to the child (uc10 "terminal resizes mid-run"); + HOT-PATH FIDELITY (i004-r12 finding-9, the headline "zero added friction"): the mirrored stdout is asserted byte-for-byte equal to a plain-spawn baseline with NO extra line (i007-r12-5: the SUCCESS socket path prints NO notice — truly invisible per uc1; only the FALLBACK path prints its one degradation-notice line) — a first-class automated proof, not only a manual playtest; + PASSTHROUGH-ARG DELIVERY (i010-r12-B, a NEW critipath: only the RETAINED fallback path's passthrough was already known-good; the pty path's was untested end-to-end): a real brain flag (`--model opus`) supplied to enroll is asserted to REACH the child through genBrainCliPtyClone's argv (the stub brain echoes its received argv to its transcript; get asserts `--model opus` is present), proving getBrainCliPassthroughArgs's args actually ARRIVE at the pty-spawned child, not only that they survive the strip in the unit test; + PTY-CHILD LIFECYCLE (i025-r11-1 / i025-r12-1, the blocker both l3 lenses converged on — the mandatory happy AND edge critipaths the vision's "no gaps" priority demands): (a) EXIT-CODE PARITY — the stub brain exits 0 → the wrapper exits 0; the stub exits a NONZERO code → the wrapper exits that SAME code (asserted the child's code propagates, matching the plain-spawn fallback); (b) RETURN-TO-SHELL — after the brain exits, the wrapper process TERMINATES promptly (asserted it does NOT hang on the still-listening socket — the socket server is closed on pty.onExit; a test that the process resolves within a bound, so the uc1 return-to-shell is proven, not only the stdout bytes); (c) SOCKET CLEANUP — after exit, the socket file is UNLINKED from `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` (no orphan); (d) SIGNAL FORWARDING — a SIGINT and a SIGTERM sent to the wrapper are each FORWARDED to the pty child (asserted the stub brain receives the signal — it records receipt to its transcript before it dies — so Ctrl-C reaches the brain, uc1); (e) TTY RESTORATION — process.stdin is put in raw mode at spawn and asserted RESTORED to its prior (cooked) mode after the wrapper exits by ANY path (normal exit, SIGINT, SIGTERM), so the human's shell is never left broken (the node-pty footgun). the SIGKILL residual (a -9'd wrapper cannot restore tty / cannot forward, orphaning the child) is NOT asserted here — it is the uncatchable-signal OS limit named in ledger row 9, not a testable wrapper behavior; + SELF-ID ENV INJECTION (i042-r12-C fix — the injection itself was untested, only its consumption was): the stub brain reads `process.env[CLONE_ENV.serial]` from ITS OWN spawned environment and reports it back (to its transcript) → the test asserts the reported value EQUALS the clone's serial, proving genBrainCliPtyClone actually INJECTS `RHACHET_CLONE_SERIAL` into the real child's env (not that a test merely set it in-process). the enrollBrainCli fallback path carries the SAME assertion in its own test (enrollBrainCli.test.ts) — so BOTH spawn paths are proven to inject, the wish's "a clone learns its own address" mechanism verified end-to-end, not only in prose; + SPAWN-TIME ALLOCATION FALLBACK (i050-r12-new — the sandbox `/dev/ptmx` hazard): with node-pty LOADED but its `pty.spawn()` forced to throw a pty-device error (a mocked spawn that raises EACCES/ENOENT/ENXIO, simulating a container without a pty mount) → genBrainCliPtyClone returns the null-spawn signal, genClone FALLS BACK to plain-spawn + the loud notice, and the clone records socketEligible=false (asserted the brain still OPENS, `clone list` reads NOT-REACHABLE, and the notice fires) — the SAME shape as getPtyModuleOrNull's load-failure test but at the spawn boundary; + a NON-device spawn error (e.g. an unexpected internal throw) RE-THROWS, NOT swallowed (rule.forbid.failhide — only the pty-device-allocation faults are allowlisted)

src/domain.operations/actor/enrolled/
