    ├── asCloneDispatchFrame.test.ts                            [+] unit — bracketed-paste + submit frames (matrix, [3.1.1 topic-3]); edges: empty body, multi-line body, and a body that itself carries the paste-terminator `\e[201~` (the paste-escape case)
    ├── isSafeCloneDispatchInput.test.ts                        [+] unit — data-driven, CLASS-SCOPED boundary (i042-r12-A): accepts plain input; ACCEPTS an SGR-COLORED relay payload (`\e[31mred\e[0m`, a CI-log/diff capture — the POSITIVE case the earlier spec lacked, proving the comms-relay usecase is NOT broken); REJECTS a cursor-movement/screen CSI (`\e[2J`, `\e[H`), an OSC sequence (`\e]0;title\a`, `\e]52;…` clipboard), AND an EMBEDDED paste-terminator `\e[201~` mid-body (not only at the start) — so the gate proves "color passes, terminal-state-control + frame-forgery reject", not the conflated "any escape fails" (uc10 safety + the wish's relay usecase both held)
    ├── asCloneDispatchFrameSplit.test.ts                       [+] unit (i039-r07-1) — data-driven byte-stream reassembly: a single whole frame in ONE chunk → [frame] + rest=''; a frame SPLIT across 2 chunks → the whole frame emitted ONLY on the second chunk (first chunk → 0 frames + a partial rest); a frame split across N byte-by-byte chunks → the whole frame on the final byte; TWO frames COALESCED in one chunk → [frame1, frame2]; a partial with NO sentinel → 0 frames + the whole chunk as rest; an oversized multi-KB frame fed byte-by-byte → exactly ONE whole frame reassembled; a NO-SENTINEL stream that pushes `buffered + chunk` past `maxFrameBytes` → `{ frames: [], rest: '', overflow: true }` (i043-r07-1, the bounded-buffer row — asserts the cap trips ONLY when no sentinel is seen, and a legitimate at-cap frame that DOES carry the sentinel still splits clean, so the bound never clips a real payload). asserts the sentinel-terminated boundary is the ONLY split point, so a complete-but-split frame is NEVER mis-read as torn (the review's core hazard) — pure, no socket, no mocks
    ├── asCloneDispatchAckFrame.test.ts                         [+] unit (i012-r11-1; two-phase i045-r11-3/r12-3) — data-driven over the THREE phases: `{phase:'queued'}` → a queued frame; `{phase:'delivered'}` → a delivered frame; `{phase:'rejected', reason}` → a rejected frame carrying the reason; PAIRED round-trips with asCloneDispatchAck below (frame then parse yields the input phase)
    ├── asCloneDispatchAck.test.ts                              [+] unit (i012-r11-1; two-phase i045-r11-3/r12-3) — data-driven: a queued frame → `{phase:'queued'}`; a delivered frame → `{phase:'delivered'}`; a rejected frame → `{phase:'rejected', reason}`; a torn/partial frame → fail-loud (the client must never read a half-frame as a phase — the fail-hide this blocker guards)
    ├── genCloneSocketServer.integration.test.ts           [+] integration — the bound socket is mode 0700, asserted (i004-r12 finding-4, the blast-radius enforcement); on connect: a SAME-user peer (any login session) passes isCallerSameUser, a DIFFERENT-user peer is REJECTED before any byte (i005-blocker-1 caller-auth — proves a cross-SESSION same-user cron/comms caller is ALLOWED, only a cross-USER peer blocked); then inbound gated by isSafeCloneDispatchInput → genCloneWriteQueue → pty.write; a raw-escape client AND a paste-escape (`\e[201~`-in-body) client are both rejected; + TWO-PHASE ACK RESPONSE (i012-r11-1; two-phase i045-r11-3/r12-3): a PLAIN dispatch → the server writes back `queued` IMMEDIATELY on enqueue, then `delivered` after pty.write lands at the head — asserted BOTH phases arrive in order, and the `queued` frame precedes the pty.write (so the acceptance signal is decoupled from delivery); a REJECTED dispatch (raw-escape / paste-escape) → the server writes back a `rejected` frame carrying the reason, THEN closes — asserted the client receives the DISTINCT phase frames, so the rejection has a real wire home; + FRAME-REASSEMBLY AT THE REAL STREAM BOUNDARY (i039-r07-1, the review's blocker — the coverage gap at the real process boundary): a LARGE bracketed-paste dispatch WRITTEN to the socket in MULTIPLE small `write()` calls (forced to chunk across separate `data` events) → the server REASSEMBLES it into ONE whole frame, gates it ONCE, and pty.write receives the ENTIRE payload intact (asserted the brain's transcript shows the whole large body, NOT a truncated or torn fragment, NOT a false NACK); AND two dispatches written back-to-back in ONE socket write (coalesced) → the server SPLITS them into TWO gated frames, both delivered (asserted both acks + both bodies). dogfooded: remove the asCloneDispatchFrameSplit seam → a split frame reaches the gate as a partial → a false torn/NACK → the assertion goes red, so the reassembly is proven essential at the real-stream boundary the review said had NO test; + BUFFER-CAP OVERFLOW (i043-r07-1): a client that streams bytes past `maxFrameBytes` with NO sentinel ever sent → the server writes a NACK frame that names the cap, CLOSES the connection, and the per-connection buffer never grows past the cap (asserted the connection is closed + the buffer is bounded), while a legitimate at-cap payload that DOES carry the sentinel still delivers whole — so the bound stops an unbounded-buffer client and a real large payload stays untouched
    ├── isCallerSameUser.integration.test.ts           [+] integration (i005-blocker-1) — a same-user peer (SO_PEERCRED uid === getuid()) passes; a foreign-uid peer → fail-loud ConstraintError; the cross-SESSION same-user case (a distinct login session, same uid) PASSES — the cron/comms path the same-login-session gate would have wrongly rejected
    ├── genCloneWriteQueue.integration.test.ts             [+] integration (nitpick-6) — the named single-writer: two dispatches whose bytes chunk across packets are drained one-whole-message-at-a-time, asserted NEVER interleaved (the owned mechanism, not an emergent property); + TWO-PHASE ACK ORDER (i045-r11-3/r12-3): a dispatch enqueued behind a still-in-flight one receives its `queued` frame IMMEDIATELY (before the head dispatch's pty.write drains) and its `delivered` frame only AFTER its turn at the head — asserted the `queued` ack for the SECOND dispatch fires while the FIRST is still in-flight, so the queue-wait is observable + decoupled from delivery (the referent behind sayClone's timeout split); + DRAIN-ON-EXIT (i050-r11-2/r12-2): with N dispatches sitting `queued`-but-not-`delivered` (each already ack'd `queued`), the queue's drain method is invoked (simulating pty.onExit) → asserts ALL N pending connections receive an IMMEDIATE `rejected` ack naming the exit cause, and NONE waits out a WEDGED timeout — dogfooded the thundering-timeout hazard: a drain that instead just closed the server (no reject fan-out) leaves the N callers hanging until their own timers expire, so the assertion that every caller gets a prompt `rejected` proves the drain is the fix, not the bare close; + DEPTH CAP (i054-r07-1): with the queue held so its head never drains (a wedged-head stub), a FLOOD of dispatches past `CLONE_WRITE_QUEUE_MAX_DEPTH` → the over-cap dispatch gets an IMMEDIATE `rejected` NACK that names the cap + the fix (asserted the NACK arrives + the queue depth never exceeds the cap), while a BELOW-cap burst of dispatches ALL deliver (asserted no false NACK on a legitimate burst) — dogfooded the buffer-vs-queue symmetry: a variant that leaves the depth uncapped grows the queued set without bound past the cap + the depth-invariant assertion goes red, so the hard cap is proven load-bearing, on par with the reassembly-buffer cap
    ├── sayClone.integration.test.ts                       [+] integration — connect via connectToClone + write; two concurrent `say` → serialized by the queue, no interleave; SELF-DISPATCH (i010-r12-D): a clone `say`s its OWN serial → the message lands on its own input (asserted via the stub brain's transcript) — the self-management positive path works, not merely that isCallerSameUser passes (same uid); + ACK/NACK PROPAGATION (i012-r11-1; two-phase i045-r11-3/r12-3): a plain dispatch → sayClone reads `queued` then `delivered` and succeeds; a dispatch the server REJECTS (unsafe input) → sayClone parses the `rejected` phase and FAILS-LOUD (ConstraintError naming the server's reason), NEVER reports a false success — dogfooded: force the server to reject, assert sayClone throws (a write-then-return sayClone would stay green, so this proves the ack carries weight); + TWO TIMEOUTS (i017-r12-1 corrected i045-r11-3/r12-3 — the false-positive-wedged clamp): (a) a stub server that ACCEPTS the connection but NEVER emits `queued` → sayClone aborts on the SHORT connect-liveness bound and FAILS-LOUD MalfunctionError (the genuinely-dead-server case); (b) a stub server that emits `queued` then NEVER emits `delivered` → sayClone aborts on the in-flight WEDGED bound and FAILS-LOUD (the genuinely-wedged-in-flight case); (c) THE FALSE-POSITIVE CLAMP: a stub server that emits `queued` immediately but delays `delivered` PAST what a single fixed timeout would allow (simulating a deep legitimate queue), then finally delivers → sayClone SUCCEEDS, NEVER a false wedged — dogfooded: a reorder that arms the wedged bound from CONNECT (the old single-fixed-timeout) instead of from `queued` → the busy-but-healthy case reads wedged + the assertion goes red, proving the two-phase split closes the queue-vs-timeout interaction the review named; all bounds asserted to REJECT/RESOLVE within their window, never a forever-hang
    ├── asCloneOutputText.test.ts                          [+] unit (i005-r12-C) — data-driven against a REALISTIC MULTI-BLOCK transcript fixture: an assistant line with a content-ARRAY (text + tool-use blocks) → just the text; a thinking-only line → empty (a semantic filter — the block holds no assistant text); a tool-result line → empty; a plain-text assistant line → itself; **a COMPLETE but CORRUPT (invalid-JSON) record → THROWS a MalfunctionError, NEVER maps to empty** (i018-r02-1, the sharpened failhide guard — asCloneOutputText does NOT swallow a parse fault; the empty render is reserved for a well-formed-but-textless block, a malformed complete record fails loud). NOTE the TORN mid-write fragment is NOT tested here — getCloneOutput's completeness check upstream never feeds an un-terminated fragment to this transformer, so a torn line never reaches it (the reliability guard moved to getCloneOutput.integration's completeness-check case). proves the raw structured JSONL becomes legible reply text (the comms read), NOT a synthetic-stub-only assertion
    └── getCloneOutput.integration.test.ts                 [+] integration — tail transcript mapped through asCloneOutputText → legible text; `--tail N` counts LOGICAL messages (assistant replies), not raw JSONL lines; `--tail N` > available → all available (i006-r12-6); empty history; get on a DEAD clone with output → RETURNS output (reads persisted history, no liveness gate — matrix 4 line 79); **MULTI-EPISODE (i007-r11-B4, the mainline case): a history/ with TWO `<exid>.jsonl` episodes (mtime-ordered, session-uuid names NOT chronological) → get returns them in MTIME order across the compaction boundary, never filename-order, never wrong-episode**; **SAME-MTIME TIE-BREAK (i052-r11-3/r12-2): two episodes forced to the SAME mtime (a same-tick compaction-boundary race) → get orders them by the `<exid>` filename LEXICOGRAPHICALLY, DETERMINISTICALLY — asserted the merged order is STABLE across repeated runs (dogfooded: run the merge N times over two same-mtime episodes → the concatenated order is identical every run; a mtime-only sort with no secondary key → the order flakes run-to-run + the stability assertion goes red)**; **TORN LAST LINE via COMPLETENESS CHECK (i009-r12-2, sharpened i018-r02-1): a transcript whose final line is a partial/un-terminated JSON write (the LIVE-append common case) → get reads only the newline-TERMINATED records, so the in-flight fragment is not-yet-a-record and is never parsed (NO swallowed exception) — get returns the complete records + OMITS the un-terminated tail, NEVER throws (dogfooded: append a half line with no trailing newline, assert get succeeds + the half is absent); AND a COMPLETE (newline-terminated) but CORRUPT record → get FAILS LOUD MalfunctionError that names the bad file+line, NEVER silently skips (dogfooded: write a terminated line of invalid JSON, assert get throws — proving the completeness check is not a blanket parse-swallow, real corruption surfaces)**; **MAP-THEN-TAIL ORDER (i011-r11-1, the correctness clamp): a transcript whose LAST raw line is a tool-result/thought-block (maps to empty), preceded by a real assistant reply → `get --tail 1` returns the REAL REPLY WHOLE, NEVER an empty string — proves the map+filter runs BEFORE the tail (a tail-first order would return the empty last raw line, breaking the say → get --tail 1 clamp); dogfooded by a reorder to tail-first → the assertion goes red**; **ORPHANED SYMLINK via IN-BAND exidsUnreadable, NO stderr WRITE (i017-r12-2, sharpened i018-r02-2, side-effect moved i023-r07-nitpick1): a history/ with TWO episodes where ONE target file is deleted (a `history/<exid>.jsonl` symlink orphaned — the claude-cleanup / user-cleared-dir case) → getCloneOutput reads the episode that remains AND RETURNS `{ lines, exidsUnreadable: [<vanished exid>] }`, NEVER a raw ENOENT crash AND never a SILENT skip AND — critically — it does NOT write to stderr ITSELF (a `get*` op carries no hidden IO side effect; the stderr WARN is the invoker's job, asserted in invokeCloneGet's test). dogfooded: unlink a linked target, assert getCloneOutput returns the survivor in `lines` + names the vanished exid in `exidsUnreadable` + does NOT touch stderr; a history/ where ALL targets are deleted → `{ lines: [], exidsUnreadable: [all] }`, not a throw; AND a non-ENOENT read error (e.g. EACCES) → RE-THROWS (the allowlist is ENOENT-only, rule.forbid.failhide — a permission fault is not swallowed)**

src/contract/cli/
├── renderCliOutput.test.ts                                [+] unit (i010-r11-B / i011-r12-C4) — data-driven: `output:'json'` → JSON.stringify of the result; `output:'tree'` → asTree(result); an unknown `--output` value → fail-loud that names `tree|json`; + SAME-DATA CROSS-CHECK (i011-r12-C4, the observable-but-unasserted criterion): for one fixture result, JSON.parse the `json` render AND assert every field it carries (serial, slug, reach-state, brain, roles) ALSO appears in the `tree` render — a proof that the two outputs describe the SAME data, not just that each parses (so a future divergence, where tree gains/drops a field json does not, goes red); + SPAWN-TIME SPLIT (i012-r12-4): the cross-check ALSO pins the one time-valued field's json-vs-tree shape — the `json` render carries the RAW `spawnedAt` IsoTimeStamp (an absolute instant a machine can compute idleness from, asserted a valid iso-time stamp, NOT a relative string), while the `tree` render carries the DERIVED relative `since=<ago>` (asserted the tree does NOT emit the raw stamp, the json does NOT emit the decaying "ago" prose) — so the machine gets the stable instant and the human gets the friendly relative, and a future swap of either goes red
├── asCliErrorJson.test.ts                                 [+] unit (i024-r12-1) — data-driven over the error classes: a ConstraintError with a hint → `{ class:'ConstraintError', message, hint, reachState:null }`; a MalfunctionError → `{ class:'MalfunctionError', … }`; a REACH-failure error carrying a tri-state → `reachState` is the DEAD/NOT-REACHABLE value (asserted the machine can branch on it); a non-reach error → `reachState:null` (asserted PRESENT-but-null, never absent, so `error.reachState` is always a defined read); asserts the `class` distinguishes caller-vs-server where a bare exit code cannot
