└── invokeCloneGet.integration.test.ts                     [+] integration — `clone get` + `--tail`; `--tail N` LARGER than available history → returns ALL available, no error, no pad (i006-r12-6); an INVALID `--tail` (negative / zero / non-numeric-and-not-`all`) → fail-loud, names the fix "--tail must be a positive integer or 'all'" (i008-r12-4 + i015-r12-5); DEFAULT TAIL (i013-r12-B): a bare `get` (no `--tail`) on a clone with >20 messages returns exactly the last 20, NOT the whole history; a large `--tail N` (> available) returns all available (i006-r12-6); `--tail all` (i015-r12-5) returns the WHOLE transcript (the explicit everything-sentinel, distinct from the guess-a-big-N path); LARGE-TRANSCRIPT SIZE DISCIPLINE (i027-r12-5 fix — the review's zero-coverage-of-a-large-case gap on the machine-consumption pipe): a clone with a MANY-episode, thousands-of-message transcript → a bare `get` returns exactly the last 20 (the default bound already protects a Slack/email relay of a bare `get` — bounded by construction), a `--tail 5` returns exactly 5, and `--tail all` delivers the WHOLE transcript WHOLE as the caller's EXPLICIT opt-in (asserted not truncated — the wish's 'deliver whole' contract holds). so the SIZE KNOB is `--tail N` (default 20) and `--tail all` is a deliberate 'I accept unbounded' choice, NOT a silent default — the pit-of-success posture: the common case is bounded, the unbounded case is opt-in + named in `get --help`. no HARD cap is imposed (a cap would silently truncate a caller who genuinely wants a full-session archive), but the DEFAULT bound + the named `--tail all` opt-in make the downstream-size footgun a conscious choice, never an accident; BARE-TEXT TREE (i013-r12-A): the default (tree) render of a multi-line reply is RAW text with NO `├─`/`└─` box-glyph — asserted the output contains the reply text and NO tree-glyph chars, so a pipe-to-Slack ships clean; `--output json` → machine-parseable reply lines (i007-r12-2); UNREADABLE-EPISODE WARN AT THE INVOKER (i023-r07-nitpick1 — the stderr side effect lives HERE, not in getCloneOutput): a `get` on a clone whose getCloneOutput returns a non-empty `exidsUnreadable` (a reclaimed episode) → the invoker prints the readable `lines` to STDOUT and the one-line WARN (which names the count + vanished exids) to STDERR (asserted: the survivor output on stdout, the WARN on stderr, the two streams captured separately); an all-vanished history → empty stdout + the WARN on stderr; `--output json` → the stdout JSON stays clean (the WARN is stderr-only) — a proof that the IO side effect is the contract-layer invoker's, so `getCloneOutput` stays a pure `get` (rule.forbid.hidden-side-effects); + TRUNCATION FIELD (i040-r12-2): a `--output json` bare `get` on a clone with >20 messages → the parsed shape carries `truncated:true` + `total:<N>` (asserted a cron reads `total` to know more exists), and a `--tail all` (or a short transcript) → `truncated:false` with `total === lines.length` (asserted the machine can tell "this is everything" from "this is the last 20"); the TREE render is unchanged by either (the truncation signal is json-only, never a line in the pipe-clean relay text)
