nroll — the pure live-slug reuse records NO enrollment event — while a companion enroll that BAKES or REBINDS a clone DOES append one line; dogfooded the wish's headline cron: N repeated `--as @:driver` reuse-enrolls leave the log at its original line-count, not N lines longer, so a 5-minute cron does not grow the audit log without bound**
├── enroll-lifecycle.acceptance.test.ts                    [+] acceptance (i026-r12-1 blocker — the pty-lifecycle guarantees are PROCESS-BOUNDARY properties, so they MUST be proven at the real `rhx enroll` wrapper boundary, NOT only in-process): the i025 genBrainCliPtyClone.integration.test asserts signal-forward/exit-parity/tty-restore by a DIRECT leaf call inside one jest worker — but signal handlers, raw-mode state, and process.exit semantics behave DIFFERENTLY across a real process boundary than inside a worker (the exact class the vision reserves for blackbox: "never a mock… proven against a real enrolled clone"). so this test SPAWNS the real `bin/run` binary as an ACTUAL child process (via the self-linked invokeRhachetCloneDetached harness), against genStubBrainLive, and: (a) SIGINT DELIVERY — sends a GENUINE `SIGINT` (via `child.kill('SIGINT')`) from OUTSIDE and asserts the stub brain RECEIVED it (the stub records signal receipt to its transcript, read back via `get` before teardown) — a proof that Ctrl-C reaches the brain across the real boundary (uc1); (b) SIGTERM DELIVERY — same with `SIGTERM`; (c) EXIT-CODE PARITY — the stub brain exits a KNOWN nonzero code → the real wrapper's own exit code EQUALS it (asserted on the child's observed exit code, the property a jest-worker call cannot faithfully exercise); (d) RETURN-TO-SHELL — the wrapper process TERMINATES (does not hang) after the brain exits, asserted within a bound; (e) SOCKET CLEANUP — the `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` socket file is ABSENT after the child exits; (f) TTY RESTORATION AT THE REAL BOUNDARY (i027-r12-6 fix — the review's exact gap: tty-restore was the ONE lifecycle facet left at the in-process layer, on the SAME reasoning the reviewers rejected for the other three facets at i025/i026): this test spawns the real `bin/run` UNDER a real pseudo-terminal whose MASTER owns the child's stdio (a node-pty master), so the real wrapper puts the OUTER pty into raw mode exactly as it does a human's terminal; after the child exits by EACH path (normal exit, SIGINT, SIGTERM) the test reads the OUTER pty master's termios and asserts it is back in COOKED mode — so a broken-shell-after-enroll regression (the worst-case ergonomic failure a human hits) goes red at the REAL process boundary, not merely in a worker. so ALL FOUR lifecycle facets — signal-forward, exit-parity, socket-cleanup, AND tty-restore — are now proven at the SAME real-subprocess boundary the vision reserves for blackbox. this is the blackbox-level proof BOTH l3 lenses called the largest recent fix's absent coverage; + (g) BRAIN-EXITS-MID-DISPATCH DRAIN (i050-r11-2/r12-2, the real-boundary proof of the write-queue drain): a DETACHED, different-session process issues a `rhx clone say @:<serial>` at the real clone AND, while that dispatch is still `queued` (the stub brain held in a busy state so delivery is deferred), the stub brain's wrapper is sent a real exit signal → the say-caller receives an IMMEDIATE fail-loud `rejected` naming the exit cause AND returns within a short bound (asserted it does NOT burn the full WEDGED timeout) — so the pty.onExit drain is proven to fan out a prompt rejection across the real process boundary, the exact "no acceptance test exercises brain-exits-mid-dispatch" gap r12 named, closed
