├── invokeCloneSayDetached.ts                            [+] infra (i005-r12-A) — issue a `clone say`/`get` from a GENUINELY separate, different-login-session process — a `setsid`-detached child (NOT a same-runner test block) — so the DETACHED-session reach test proves isCallerSameUser ALLOWS a same-user cross-session caller (the cron/comms premise). the caller runs under the SAME uid but a distinct session, exactly the cron/webhook shape
└── genStubBrainLive.ts                                    [~] update (i005-r12-C) — alive + READ-then-TRANSFORM stdin (input `poke X` → output `ack:X`, NOT a raw echo — so a `get` assertion proves the BRAIN read+processed the input, not that the pty terminal-echoed the bytes); AND writes a REALISTIC MULTI-BLOCK transcript (a claude-code-shaped `.jsonl`: assistant lines with content-ARRAYS mixing text + tool-use + thinking blocks, plus tool-result lines) so asCloneOutputText/getCloneOutput are proven against real structured JSONL, not a text-only synthetic stub [3.1.3.test:5]; AND a BUSY-STATE mode (i022-r09-1): on `poke A` it emits a long output stream (or a bounded sleep) BEFORE it acks, so a second `say poke B` can arrive mid-stream — the clone.mid-response.acceptance.test uses this to prove genCloneWriteQueue serializes B behind A with no interleave (the mid-response dispatch mechanism, automatable against the stub even though the stub cannot replicate claude's real Ink-TUI render — that fidelity is the real-claude test's job)
```

**legend:** `[+]` create · `[~]` update · `[○]` retain

### experience-inventory traceability — every catalogued experience → its test

> applies the experience-inventory-coverage audit (dispatched to `ehmpathy/rhachet-roles-bhuild` #326,
> to become an l3 lens) to THIS blueprint: each `inventory.of=experience.case=*` (+ the
> `howto.use.clones` reach flow) traced to the acceptance test that EXERCISES + CLAMPS its IN-SCOPE
> critipath, or flagged to the `rhx clone` dream for its out-of-scope (bake / derived-actor) portion. per
> the vision's mandate — "each experience ⇒ owed an acceptance critipath + clamp in whichever behavior
> owns its primitives." NOTE a clone reach is addressable by EITHER body of the `@:` sigil — `@:<serial>`
> (primary) OR `@:<slug>` (`--as` unique) — so each reach test below exercises BOTH forms
> (`define.address-sigils.md`).

| # | experience | primitives | IN-SCOPE here → test (happy + edge) | out-of-scope → `rhx clone` dream |
|---|-----------|-----------|--------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|
| 1 | surf-school-crew (actor composition + base-sync tether) | actors.yml slugs, `clone @slug`, `+/-role`, tether, `--say` | none — the FRAME only REPRESENTS a derived actor (`Clone.actor` RefByPrimary, proven by `Clone.test.ts` ref-shape) | the whole composition + base-sync tether = dream (its steel-test beside case 2) |
| 2 | redteam-fan-out (clone forks + inter-clone observe) | `fork @:<serial\|slug>`, `+/-role`, `--say`, peer observe | the inter-clone PEER-OBSERVE half → `clone.acceptance` peer-discovery (A finds B via whoami `actorHash` → `clone list @<actorHash>`) + `invokeCloneWhoami.integration`; edge: whoami OUTSIDE a clone → fail-loud | `fork` + `+/-role` fan-out = the dream's vision-named STEEL-TEST critipath |
| 3 | worktree-crew (@:driver drives, @:foreman reviews) | `clone @mechanic`, `@mechanic -driver` (derived), `say @:foreman --what`, `get @:driver --tail` | the REACH half (`say @:<serial\|slug> --what`, `get @:<serial\|slug> --tail`) → shares case-4's clamp + `enroll-to-reach.journey` [t2][t3]; edge: say-to-dead / get-unknown → `clone.acceptance` | the default two-clone worktree spawn + the `-driver` DERIVED actor = dream |
| 4 | named-clone-on-enroll (anonymous actor, named clone) | enroll `--as @:slug`, `say`, `get`, `actor.via.hash` | **FULLY in-scope — THE mandated clamp** → `clone.acceptance` (enroll `--as` → say/get by BOTH `@:<slug>` AND `@:<serial>`; assert on-disk actor is `actor.via.hash`, never a slug actor); edge: dead / unknown / wrong-grain / uuid-shaped-`--as` / path-unsafe-`--as` / dropped-sigil → `clone.acceptance` + `enroll.acceptance` | none — fully here |
| 5 | supervisor-observes-prod (ad-hoc actor extension) | `clone @actor +role --say` (cross-supplier delta-hash), `get`/observe | the OBSERVE half (`get @:<serial\|slug>`, cross-session detached) → `clone.acceptance` get + DETACHED-session reach (`invokeCloneSayDetached`, i005-r12-A) + `enroll-to-reach.journey` [t0a] | the `@actor +role` cross-supplier DERIVED-actor construction = dream |
| — | howto.use.clones worked reach (name-on-bake → say/get/wake) | enroll `--as @:driver`, `say`, `get`, `wake` | the `enroll --as → say → get` reach (by both `@:<slug>` + `@:<serial>`) → `clone.acceptance` + journey (the vision's mandated reach flow) | `wake` (reopen a serial) = dream |

**the result:** every IN-SCOPE experience subset is covered + clamped — case 4 fully (the mandated
clamp), cases 2/3/5 by their reach-subset clamps (case 2's peer-observe has its OWN clamp; cases 3/5
share case-4's reach clamp + the detached-session proof), case 1 frame-only. every out-of-scope (bake /
derived-actor) portion is flagged to the `rhx clone` dream, never a silent omission. no in-scope
critipath — happy or edge — is left uncovered. (this table is the traceability the dispatched l3 lens
will enforce for future behaviors; here it is satisfied at author time.)

### the clamp (mandated)

`blackbox/cli/clone.acceptance.test.ts` **clamps** the reach round-trip ([rule.require.clamp-edge-cases]):
enroll `--as @:driver` against `genStubBrainLive`, `say` a **command marker** (`poke <nonce>`), `get` and
assert the **transformed response** (`ack:<nonce>`) reads back — **by both `@:driver` and `@:<serial>`**
— and assert the on-disk actor is `actor.via.hash=…` (never a slug actor), which proves the two-grain
split (matrix 5). the assertion is the TRANSFORMED `ack:` output, **not** a raw echo of the input —
because a pty terminal-echoes stdin independent of the brain, so an echo assertion would pass even if
`say` never reached the brain (a clamp with no teeth). the `ack:` transform appears ONLY if the brain
read+processed the input, so the clamp proves the dispatch's real effect (uc6). the test must go **red**
if `say` fails to reach the brain, if the brain does not process it, or if slug/serial diverge — and
this is dogfooded (revert the say-path → the clamp goes red).

