## adherence checklist (what execution must honor)

- verbs: get/set/gen only; `findsert*` for the idempotent actor/clone bake; `is*`/`as*` transformers;
  `del*` (delClone/kill) is the clone LIFECYCLE — deferred to the `rhx clone` dream, so the gen↔del
  mirror is intentionally half-open here (genClone bakes; the stale-orphan-socket unlink inside genClone
  uses the `delFileSync` infra primitive, not a domain `del*`) — flagged so a reviewer reads the absent
  `delClone` as a scoped deferral, not an omission (per `rule.require.get-set-gen-verbs` gen↔del mirror).
  the one non-get/set/gen name — `connectToClone` — follows the extant repo `connectTo*` socket
  convention (`connectToKeyrackDaemon`, `connectToUnixSocket`), the sanctioned imperative-action form
  for opening an i/o connection; it is not a data mutation, so get/set/gen does not apply
- **the lifecycle-cleanup deferrals — one named family** (i004 nitpicks 3, 5): the absent `del*` above is
  one of a SET of clone-lifecycle cleanups all deferred to the `rhx clone` dream, each flagged so a
  reviewer reads it as scoped, not omitted: (a) `delClone`/kill; (b) **slug-release-on-death** — a dead
  slug is re-bindable by its own actor, but cross-actor takeover + explicit release is the dream's
  (nitpick-3); (c) **clone-dir prune** — every bare enroll bakes a fresh `serial` dir that is never
  removed, so `.agent/.actors/**/clones/` grows unbounded over a repo's life (nitpick-5). growth is
  host-local + gitignored (not committed bloat), and `clone list` shows every clone WITH its state per
  uc5 (so a DEAD clone is never hidden — a default-hide filter would fight uc5); a prune command + an
  optional `--state` filter are the dream's lifecycle surface. (d) **orphan-socket-file prune** (i009-r11-5):
  every bare enroll mints a fresh serial + a fresh socket in `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, and genClone unlinks only a
  STALE orphan of the SAME serial on a findsert-reuse — a serial never reused (the common case) leaves its
  socket file behind. on a long-lived box that is not rebooted (a devcontainer, an always-on VM, where tmpfs
  does not clear), these accumulate. this is the socket-file twin of (c) the clone-dir growth: host-local,
  off-repo, harmless per-file, unbounded over time. the same prune command that sweeps dead clone dirs sweeps
  their orphan sockets — the dream's lifecycle surface. (e) **enrolled-actor-dir prune** (i010-r11-C): every
  distinct `{brain,roles}` tried locally — including one-off `-r <delta>` experiments — mints a permanent
  `actor.via.hash=…/` dir (brain/.claude/settings.json + roles/enrollment.jsonl) that is never removed, parallel
  to (c) the clone-dir growth one level up. host-local + gitignored (the whole `.agent/.actors/` tree is ignored,
  blocker-3), so not committed bloat — but a long-lived checkout accretes a hash-actor dir per roleset permutation.
  the same prune command sweeps stale actor dirs whose clones are all dead. (f) **orphan-exid-claim prune**
  (i021-r07-1): the atomic `.exids/<exid>` history-link claims (the same-cwd race fix) accumulate one entry per
  linked episode, parallel to the `.slugs/` entries — host-local runtime under the gitignored `.agent/.actors/`,
  swept by the same prune when their owner clone is reaped. (g) **enrollment.jsonl rotation/truncation**
  (i050-r11-3/r12-3): a genuinely LONG-LIVED actor accretes one enrollment.jsonl line per REAL enrollment event
  (bake/rebind) over a repo's life — the pure-reuse cron-tick appends are already closed at source (setActorEnrolledRolesLog
  skips the live-slug-reuse path, above), so this residual is bounded by real events, not cron frequency, but an
  append-only audit log still has no size cap. getAllActorsEnrolled reads the LATEST line, so read cost also scales with
  the log's length. a rotation/truncation policy (cap the line count, or roll to a dated `.1.jsonl`) is the dream's
  lifecycle surface, the SAME shape as the other cleanups — named here as the 7th so an otherwise-exhaustive ledger has no
  silent gap. all SEVEN share the `del*` deferral shape.
  COMPOUNDING CROSS-REFERENCE (i011-r11-6): (e) actor-dir growth and the flagged unindexed serial-scan in
  getOneCloneByRef (a full walk across every actor dir per `say`/`get`-by-serial, "flagged not built") share ONE
  timeline — every serial lookup rescans every actor, and the actor count only grows — so they are ONE
  prioritization decision for the executor, not two unrelated footnotes: if the box is long-lived enough that
  actor-dir growth matters, the serial-scan cost has grown in lockstep, and the fix (a global `.clones/<serial>`
  index symmetric with `.slugs/`, plus the prune) addresses both at once. (i052-r11-5) THE SAME TIMELINE carries a
  THIRD cost: the i049 accrual-WARN enumerates the actor's clones + probes `getCloneReachState` (an `isCloneLive`
  socket connect) per clone on EVERY enroll, unconditionally — so as an actor's clone count grows, each enroll
  pays an O(N) socket-probe sweep in lockstep with (e)'s dir growth and the serial-scan. severity is LOW (a
  refused/absent unix-socket connect is near-instant, and the count is bounded by the accrual-WARN's own soft
  ceiling in practice), so it is NOT built-around now — but it is the SAME prune-and-index decision, not a
  separate one: the same global `.clones/<serial>` index that collapses the serial-scan also lets the accrual
  count read the index instead of a per-clone probe sweep. so all THREE (actor-dir growth, serial-scan,
  accrual-probe) are ONE lifecycle prioritization for the executor, folded here rather than left as an
  unnamed per-enroll cost
- **near-duplicate sigil parsers — wet, by design** (i004 nitpick-6; count corrected i048-r11-3): `asCloneRef`
  + `asActorRef` share the `@`/`@:` marker-split but validate different grains. a THIRD occurrence of the
  did-you-mean-for-sigil logic ALSO exists TODAY — `invokeEnroll`'s `--as` validation (i044-r12-2)
  independently re-derives the same dropped-sigil / wrong-grain detection + corrected-suggestion text. so
  the live count is THREE, not two. per `rule.prefer.wet-over-dry` these stay wet even so, because the
  enroll case is arguably a DIFFERENT concern — validate-on-ASSIGN (reject a bad `--as` at the write
  boundary) vs parse-on-LOOKUP (turn an address into a typed ref) — so a forced `asAddressRef` extraction
  would fold two genuinely-distinct concerns under one op (over-abstraction the rule forbids). the trigger
  to REVISIT is now a FOURTH parse-on-lookup consumer specifically (the dream's `make`/`fork`/`wake`), at
  which point the two lookup parsers (`asCloneRef` + `asActorRef` + the new ones) extract to a shared
  `asAddressRef` and the enroll validate-on-assign case stays its own — the count note no longer implies
  the trigger is pending at two when three live instances exist
- **two atomic-symlink-claim instances — wet, by design, tracked for rule-of-three** (i024-r11-2): the
  `.slugs/<slug>` winner-election in `setCloneSlugIndex` (i017-r11-1) and the `.exids/<exid>` claim in
  `genCloneHistoryLink` (i021-r07-1) are the SAME mechanism — attempt an exclusive `symlink(2)` at
  `<indexDir>/<key> -> <target>`, `EEXIST` means a peer won, then reconcile against the winner. UNLIKE
  the sigil parsers (which share a shape but validate different grains), this is IDENTICAL logic, and the
  blueprint's own prose already names the sameness ("the SAME atomic exclusive symlink primitive… i017-r11-1
  uses for slugs"). per `rule.prefer.wet-over-dry` two instances stay wet BY DESIGN — a shared seam
  `genAtomicSymlinkClaim({ indexDir, key, target }) → { claimed: boolean, owner: string }` (attempt the
  exclusive symlink; on EEXIST readlink the target as `owner`) is a one-file lift, but the rule-of-three
  says hold until a third caller. what THIS note adds — the gap the review named — is the FORCING FUNCTION
  the sigil parsers already carry but this pair lacked: a THIRD claim-index is the extraction trigger, and
  two are already foreseeable (the deferred slug-release-on-death rebind, and a future dispatch-id claim in
  the `rhx clone` dream's richer request/response protocol). so the third such index becomes the
  `genAtomicSymlinkClaim` extraction — both extant copies fold into it — never a silent third copy of a
  subtle concurrency primitive where a fix applied to one copy but not the other would be a real hazard.
  tracked in the open-decisions ledger so the third instance forces the decision
- **the invoker skeleton — wet, by design, tracked for rule-of-three** (i017-r11-5 — nitpick-5): the
  CONTAINING skeleton the sigil parsers sit inside — parse the address ref → look up the clone/actor →
  gate on reach-state (or scope-resolve) → render via renderCliOutput — is identically WET across
  `invokeCloneList` (the scoped `@<actor>` path), `invokeCloneSay`, and `invokeCloneGet`. per
  `rule.prefer.wet-over-dry` these three instances stay wet BY DESIGN: each verb's middle step differs
  (say gates on a LIVE socket + dispatches, get reads persisted history with NO liveness gate, list
  scope-resolves an actor + enumerates), so a premature `withResolvedCloneRef` wrapper would need a
  switch over the three middles — the exact over-abstraction the rule forbids. this is FLAGGED with the
  SAME forcing function the sigil parsers carry: the dream's `wake`/`fork` land the same parse→lookup→
  gate→render shape as a FOURTH+FIFTH instance, and THAT is the trigger to revisit the extraction (a
  shared `withResolvedCloneRef({ ref }, operate)` higher-order wrapper, if the middles have converged by
  then). tracked in the open-decisions ledger so the arrival of the third+ instance forces the decision,
  never a silent fifth copy
- **idempotency** ([rule.require.idempotent-operations]): findsertActorEnrolled / setCloneIdentity converge
  on re-run; **genClone is findsert-by-slug** (i004 blocker-1) — `--as @:<slug>` finds a live same-actor
  owner and REUSES it (converges), re-binds a dead owner, and fails-loud only on a cross-actor collision;
  a BARE enroll (no slug) is create-always by design (each anonymous run is a distinct clone, so a fresh
  serial is correct, not a convergence miss). the ONE sanctioned NON-idempotent op is **`setActorEnrolledRolesLog`**
  — an append-only **audit log** of enrollment events (each enroll IS a distinct dated event worth a
  record: the who/when/why history the vision demands). this is the brief's named exception ("an audit-log
  append… the exception, named so the non-idempotency is explicit"): a re-enroll findserts the SAME actor
  but appends a NEW dated line by design; the record is the audit trail, not convergent state
- args by grain ([rule.require.input-context-pattern] / [rule.require.input-options-pattern]): IMPURE ops
  (communicators + orchestrators — sayClone, isCloneLive, getCloneSocketPath (i020-r04-1: reads host env, an impure communicator), genClone, findsertActorEnrolled, the invokers)
  take `(input, context)` for dependency injection; PURE transformers (computeBrainCliInput,
  getBrainCliPassthroughArgs, genCloneSerial, getActorEnrolledDir/getCloneDir, asCloneDispatchFrame,
  isSafeCloneDispatchInput, isBrainSocketCapable) take `(input)` alone — NO context (they inject no deps), and
  a pure op with a config knob takes `(input, options)`, never a context. inline i/o; arrow-only; one op
  per file; filename === op name
- fail-loud on every error path — with the RIGHT error CLASS ([rule.require.failloud], [rule.require.exit-code-semantics]):
  caller-input faults (brain conflict, malformed `--as`, dead/unknown clone, unsafe say input,
  unknown `@<actor>` scope, no-`.agent/`, **a global slug already owned by another actor** (blocker-1),
  **a DIFFERENT-user peer on the socket** (i005-blocker-1, via isCallerSameUser's ConstraintError))
  throw a **ConstraintError**-family error (exit 2, caller must fix) each with a hint that names the fix; a
  genuine infra fault (e.g. the socket server cannot bind for a reason other than a stale orphan) throws
  **MalfunctionError** (exit 1). the absent-pty-addon path is NOT an error — it FALLS BACK (plain spawn +
  notice), per uc10
- **socket safety = caller-auth AND content-gate** (i005-blocker-1): genCloneSocketServer verifies the
  peer's USER — isCallerSameUser compares the SO_PEERCRED uid (via the shared getSocketPeerCred read)
  to the server's own getuid() — BEFORE it reads any byte, THEN content-gates each message
  (isSafeCloneDispatchInput). the threat model is same-USER-any-session, NOT same-login-session: it blocks
  OTHER users on a shared host while ALLOWING this user's cron / comms-webhook / other-session caller —
  the exact usecases (uc6/uc7) the behavior exists to serve. this is DELIBERATELY a different gate than the
  keyrack daemon's verifyCallerLoginSession (right for SECRETS, wrong for a cross-session clone dispatch);
  only the peer-cred READ is shared, never the session gate. the vision's "small blast radius" bounded
  only WHAT the socket does; the caller-auth now bounds WHO may connect
- treestruct stdout for `actor list` / `clone list`; snapshots on all contract stdout
- **blueprint citations are execution-time reasoning, NOT shipped comments** (i043-r11-2,
  [rule.require.timeless-comments]): the filediff/codepath rationale is dense with review-round
  citations — `(i042-r12-1 fix — the review's exact gap …)`, `(i005-blocker-1)`, `(the reviewer is
  right that …)`. these are the blueprint's OWN review-round history, not source text: a literal
  transcription into a code comment would ship `// i042-r12-1 fix …`, which fails the repo's own "make
  full sense to a reader with zero knowledge of the review session" test — no `i042`/`r12` exists in the
  codebase to look up. so the executor DISTILLS each annotation into a durable `.what`/`.why` before it
  reaches source: the WHY survives (the reason a choice holds), the review-round provenance drops.
  ONE EXCEPTION the i046 review named — the WET-BY-DESIGN TRIPWIRES (the "Nth instance triggers
  extraction" rules: the sigil parsers, the atomic-symlink-claim, the invoker skeleton, the
  CLI-error-catch skeleton, the dispatch-frame sentinel) are DURABLE RATIONALE, not review-session
  noise, so each MUST survive as a real `.why` comment AT ITS SITE (e.g. on `asCloneRef`: "peer:
  asActorRef; a 3rd address-parse consumer triggers a shared extraction"). only the `iNNN`/`rNN`
  provenance drops; the tripwire itself is the mechanism that prevents future duplication, so it has a
  durable home in the code a future executor reads, NOT only in this blueprint's prose
- **repoPath canonicalization — convention now, a branded type is the flagged type-guard** (i046-r11-nit):
  every repoPath-consumer op receives the getOneRepoPath-canonical realpath, never a raw `process.cwd()`
  — TODAY enforced by convention (the prose + the thread discipline). the i046 review rightly notes a
  convention is not a compiler backstop: a single missed thread silently re-introduces the
  cross-repo/symlink identity-fragmentation the i037/i039 composite key + getOneRepoPath close. the
  structural guard is a BRANDED `CanonicalRepoPath` type (distinct from `string`) as the input type on
  every repoPath-consumer op, so a raw cwd string FAILS to type-check rather than drift. FLAGGED as a
  clean-rework (one branded type + the op signatures), NOT mandated in v1: it ripples every repoPath
  op's signature at once (a wide, mechanical change), best done as its own typed-guard pass — the same
  deferral shape as the runtime-`actor/`-folder rename (ledger row 4). the convention + the
  getOneRepoPath single-owner + the canonicalization integration test are the v1 guard; the brand is the
  belt-and-suspenders upgrade
- **help-on-demand for every new surface** (i009-r12-5, [rule.require.help-on-demand]): each new flag +
  subcommand carries a `--help`/`-h` that states its `.what`, inputs, and a worked example — the new
  `enroll` flags (`--brain`, `--as @:<slug>`, `--no-socket`, `--output`), and the whole `actor`/`clone`
  subcommand family (`actor list`, `clone list`/`say`/`get`, each with `--output` and `say`'s `--what`/`get`'s
  `--tail`). commander (the extant CLI framework, per invokeRoles) auto-emits `--help` from the registered
  option descriptions + a `.addHelpText` example, so this is an ADHERENCE requirement on the registrations
  (every `.option(...)` carries a human description; every subcommand carries an example), not a new op. the
  discoverability the vision's pit-of-success demands is a first-class snapshot: `rhx clone --help` and
  `rhx enroll --help` snapshots assert the new flags are documented, not only functional
- `node-pty` pinned AND declared as an **`optionalDependency`** (not a plain `dependency`) — it is a
  native addon, so an install-time build failure on an unsupported platform must leave it absent (the
  runtime fallback then engages), never abort `pnpm install`; this keeps uc10's "never hard-fail" whole
  from INSTALL through runtime. loaded lazily on the node/tsx branch; `.agent/.actors/**` runtime gitignored
- no mocks in acceptance; the socket proven by TWO tiers (i022-r09-1/2): a HERMETIC stub-brain
  acceptance test (the fast, credential-free CI gate for the mechanism, incl. the mid-response
  busy-state dispatch) AND a REQUIRED real-claude external-contract acceptance test (the genuine
  external AI service, credential-gated + FAIL-LOUD when absent, never a stub-substitute and never a
  deferral) — per rule.require.external-contract-integration-tests + rule.forbid.acceptance.mocks; a
  credential gap fails loud (rule.require.failfast), never a silent skip
- the direct spawn is retained as the fallback — and the criteria-mandated enroll behaviors persist:
  the invisible mirror (uc1), the positional `<brain>` form (uc3), the `--roles` grammar, and arg
  passthrough. this is scoped compat, NOT a blanket "preserve every prior quirk" — only these named,
  requested behaviors are guaranteed; any unlisted incidental of the old direct spawn is free to change
- **concurrency + fidelity — the still-open cluster, each dispositioned** (i004 restated): none is a
  silent gap. (1) **concurrent identical enrolls racing `enrollment.jsonl`** — setActorEnrolledRolesLog appends
  with `O_APPEND` (POSIX guarantees an atomic append under `PIPE_BUF` for a single `write`), and the
  composed line is now BOUNDED under that ceiling BY ENFORCEMENT, not by assumption (i052-r11-2/r12-1): the
  one unbounded caller field (`--reason`) is truncated to fit with a marked `…[+<n>B elided]` elision, and
  a pathological over-ceiling roleset alone fails loud — so a >PIPE_BUF line can never reach the append,
  and two concurrent enrolls of the same actor produce two whole lines, never a
  torn one; tested with two concurrent appends asserted both-present-and-whole AND the bound asserted (the
  on-disk line is always ≤ ceiling). (2) **concurrent
  same-cwd history-link double-link — CLOSED at source (i021-r07-1), no longer a playtest item** — two
  clones spawned in the same cwd within one filesystem-timestamp tick could each grab the "newest"
  transcript by mtime + spawn-window and BOTH link it. the ROOT-CAUSE fix is the atomic EXCLUSIVE
  `.exids/<exid>` claim (symmetric with `.slugs/`, the proven i017-r11-1 election): genCloneHistoryLink
  atomically claims a candidate exid BEFORE it links, so a peer that lost the claim (EEXIST) skips to the
  next candidate — no two clones EVER link the same exid, the double-link is IMPOSSIBLE by construction
  (rule.require.solve-at-cause), not a mtime guess. the mtime + spawn-window filter still selects WHICH
  candidates are this-spawn-or-later; the atomic claim is what SERIALIZES the election among them. RESIDUAL
  (benign, named): a same-tick spawn of two SAME-actor clones in one cwd still yields distinct files, but
  which same-actor clone claims which is order-dependent — a swapped attribution between two interchangeable
  sessions of ONE identity, never a cross-actor mislink and never a double-link. the perfect per-clone
  attribution (a caller-assigned session id at spawn) awaits a per-brain `--session-id` capability research
  did not confirm (topic-2: the TUI emits no parseable session id), flagged as a future enhancement — the
  RACE itself is closed. NAMED-SEAM NOTE (i010-r11-D): the mtime + spawn-window filter is a one-line
  heuristic today, so it lives inline in genCloneHistoryLink; IF it grows past trivial during execution
  (e.g. a multi-signal match), extract it to a pure `isTranscriptWithinSpawnWindow({ transcriptMtime,
  spawnedAt }) → boolean` transformer — a reactive extraction (rule.prefer.wet-over-dry: one trivial
  instance does not yet warrant the seam), so the executor lifts it the moment the heuristic earns a second
  signal, not speculatively now. (3)
  **getCloneOutput vs a REAL multi-block claude JSONL** — NOW TESTED, not only playtest (i005-r12-C):
  asCloneOutputText is proven against a REALISTIC multi-block fixture (genStubBrainLive writes a
  claude-code-shaped `.jsonl` with content-arrays + tool-use + thinking blocks), so the structured-line →
  legible-text extraction is asserted, not synthetic-only. what REMAINS a post-execution **playtest** item
  (same class as the pty visual fidelity, F1 note) is fidelity against a LIVE real claude session's exact
  latest JSONL schema + compaction bridges — the parser targets the brain-cli's own on-disk `.jsonl`, so a
  schema drift is a playtest catch, but the extraction logic itself is now unit-covered. (4) **say-while-streaming / mid-response** — the
  genCloneWriteQueue serializes writes (no interleave); the exact ready-state protocol (does claude's TUI
  need a wait for idle before a paste lands mid-response?) is F3, flagged for the council + playtest.
  (5) **say-vs-a-human's-own-live-keystrokes** (i009-r12-3, a DISTINCT sub-case of F3, NOT the same hazard):
  F3 is about the BRAIN's readiness (a paste landing mid-RESPONSE); this is about the HUMAN's input channel —
  a cron's `say` bracketed-paste landing while the human is mid-typing an UNSUBMITTED line in the same pty.
  the two paths do NOT share the write-queue: the socket dispatch funnels through genCloneWriteQueue, but the
  human's own keyboard is a DIRECT `process.stdin → pty.write` passthrough (the invisible-mirror path), so it
  BYPASSES the queue. so a `say` and a human keystroke CAN interleave into one garbled pty input line — the
  queue serializes socket-vs-socket, never socket-vs-human-stdin. the honest scope call: this is ACCEPTED as
  out-of-scope-here, not silently missed — the human + a cron co-driving the SAME live pty at the same instant
  is an inherent property of a shared terminal (the human sees the injected paste appear in their own input, a
  visible collision, not a silent corruption of a detached process), and a per-line lock across the human's
  raw-mode stdin would fight the invisible-passthrough fidelity uc1 demands. bracketed-paste framing already
  makes the injected text a DISTINCT paste block the TUI can delimit from the human's own typing, which bounds
  the garble. a stricter interlock (hold the human's stdin while a paste drains) is flagged beside F3 for the
  council + playtest — same ready-state family, distinct trigger

## flagged for the end-of-road council (fulcrums, best-guessed above)

F1 pty lib (node-pty vs bun-pty), F2 socket file name, F3 dispatch frame exactness (does claude's TUI
need `\r` vs `\n`, or a ready-state wait — [3.1.1 topic-3 KHUE]), F4 socket-derived liveness (vs a
tracked field), F5 reuse `BrainCliEnrollmentManifest` as the actor identity, F6 ActorEnrolled as a
DomainEntity (primary≡unique=hash, for Clone.actor's typed RefByPrimary) vs a DomainLiteral (the honest
content-addressed shape — i005-nitpick-6), F7 the bare-enroll serial line on STDERR (i007-r12-6 — surface
the human's own `@:<serial>` on a no-`--as` enroll vs pure silence + `rhx clone list`). each is a **clean
rework** (one file / one adapter) if the wisher prefers otherwise.

**criteria backfill — CLOSED, not an IOU (i008-r11-N2 → i009-r11-4):** two surfaces were authored at THIS
blueprint layer without a backing line in `2.1.criteria.blackbox.yield.md` — `--output tree|json` on the four
talk verbs (i007-r12-2) and the bare-enroll STDERR serial line (F7, i007-r12-6). both are direct derivations
of the wish's OWN motives — `--output json` of its explicit machine-consumption "why" (crons/comms parse
fields, not glyphs), F7 of least-astonishment discoverability. rather than leave this as a scope leak / IOU
into execution (the i009-r11-4 concern), the criteria addendum is now ADDED: **`2.1.criteria.blackbox.yield.md`
usecase.11** ratifies both surfaces as blackbox criteria, so the contract and the blueprint AGREE going into
execution — no undecided surface ships.

a THIRD blueprint-originated surface is ratified by the SAME discipline (i011-r11-4): **git-style hash-PREFIX
matching** on `@<actor>` scopes (asActorRef / getOneActorEnrolledByRef), with its ambiguous-prefix fail-loud
contract, is a derivation of the vision's own "copyable hash" render — a full 64-char hash is unusable at a
human keyboard, so a prefix is the natural reach, exactly as `git` addresses a commit. it was authored here
with dedicated ops + tests + snapshots but had slipped the ratification the `--output json`/F7 pair got.
`2.1.criteria.blackbox.yield.md` **usecase.11** now also ratifies prefix-reach (a unique prefix resolves,
an ambiguous prefix fails loud naming the candidates), so all THREE blueprint-derived surfaces have a
criteria home — no un-ratified surface ships. (the i039/i046 rounds ratified two more by the same
discipline — the `enroll --output json` headless handoff and `rhx clone whoami` + self-id env-vars — as the
FOURTH and FIFTH usecase.11 addenda.)

a SIXTH blueprint-originated surface is ratified by the SAME discipline (i050-r11-1/r12-1): the
**`rhx enroll --reason <text>` audit-motive flag** (added i046-r12-5 alongside `whoami`) was the ONE peer
surface that never got its usecase.11 addendum — the exact scope-leak pattern the backfill discipline exists
to catch. it traces directly to the wish, which names `enrollment.jsonl` as the record of "the history of WHY
and which roles were enrolled." **`2.1.criteria.blackbox.yield.md` usecase.11** now ratifies `--reason` (a
supplied motive is recorded on a real enrollment event, a bare enroll records an absent reason, `--reason
@stdin` reads a piped motive), so six blueprint-derived surfaces have a criteria home — no un-ratified
surface ships.

a SEVENTH blueprint-originated surface is ratified by the SAME discipline (i059-r12-3): the **`rhx enroll`
soft accrual-WARN** (a stderr advisory + an `accrualWarn` json field, built i049-r12-1) was a real observable
CLI surface that slipped the usecase.11 backfill its six siblings each got — the i059 arch-defects lens named
it as the exact scope-leak pattern the backfill exists to catch, orthogonal to the still-open threshold-value
/ soft-vs-hard council fulcrum (the EXISTENCE of the surface needs a criteria line regardless of what that
value is decided to be). **`2.1.criteria.blackbox.yield.md` usecase.11** now ratifies the accrual-WARN (a
crossed live-clone threshold emits the advisory + the enroll still succeeds, a below-threshold enroll emits
none, `--output json` carries a structured accrual field), so all SEVEN blueprint-derived surfaces have a
criteria home — no un-ratified surface ships.

**genEnrollmentHash stakes escalation (i008-r11-N3, flagged):** `genEnrollmentHash` today only disambiguates
a per-session settings filename; this behavior PROMOTES it to the primary key of a durable, multi-clone actor
directory (live sockets + history). role-order instability is already handled (it `.sort()`s roles before the
hash — verified `genBrainCliConfigArtifact.ts:76`). the ONE unaddressed escalation: if the hash algorithm's
SHAPE ever changes, extant `actor.via.hash=…` dirs would key to new ids and become orphans reachable only by
their old-hash path. low probability; the mitigation is the standard content-address migration (a versioned
hash prefix if ever needed) — flagged as a one-line note for the execution, not built now.

**self-dispatch semantics — allowed, unguarded, by design (i009-r12-4):** a clone MAY `say` to its own
serial — the wish names "self-management" (a clone messaging its siblings) as a motivating usecase, and a
clone reaching its OWN serial is the degenerate case of reaching a sibling. so self-dispatch is DELIBERATELY
permitted, NOT blocked: getOneCloneByRef resolves a clone's own serial like any other, isCallerSameUser
passes (same uid), and the message lands on its own input. no self-say LOOP-guard is in scope — a runaway
self-dispatch loop is an application-logic concern for whatever drives the clone (the same way a shell script
that `echo`s into its own stdin is the script's bug, not the terminal's), not a socket-layer invariant; a
loop-breaker would need a dispatch-provenance model that belongs to the `rhx clone` dream's richer protocol.
flagged so a reviewer reads the absent self-say guard as a deliberate allow (self-management enabled), not an
overlooked hazard.

**machine-readable serial handoff on enroll — F7's machine twin, flagged (i009-r12-6):** F7 gives a bare
`rhx enroll <brain>` a HUMAN-readable STDERR serial line. a MACHINE wrapper (a supervisor that spawns
`rhx enroll` non-interactively to keep a long-running background clone reachable for later cron wiring) has no
clean parse of that human prose — its only handle is scraping the F7 line. `--output json` was added to the
four TALK verbs precisely because the wish's motive is machine consumption; its ABSENCE on `enroll` itself is
an inconsistency worth a deliberate scope call. the best-guess for THIS behavior: enroll's `--output json`
(emit `{ serial, socketEligible, actorHash }` to STDOUT on spawn) is FLAGGED for the council, NOT built here —
because enroll's STDOUT is the invisible human mirror (uc1 byte-for-byte fidelity), so a machine-handoff JSON
must go to a SEPARATE channel (a `--handoff-fd` or a written `identity.json` path echoed to STDERR), a design
choice with more than one clean shape. the honest interim: a machine wrapper reads the clone's `identity.json`
(already written at bake under the actor dir) or parses the F7 STDERR line; a first-class `enroll --output
json` is the council's call, one clean-rework invoker line if adopted.

