| 3(i054) | bare `rhx enroll` is create-always with no HARD cap — a misconfigured cron accrues billed brains (arch-hazards-behavior blocker.3) | HELD — a WISHER-SCOPE fulcrum; a hard cap fights the wish's own motive | a bare enroll spawns a fresh clone BY DESIGN, and a GENUINE parallel-clone burst is a VALID use — the wish's own explicit parallel-clone motive — so a hard ConstraintError cap would BLOCK a legitimate use, a scope decision above a text review's authority (rule.always.converge-with-reviewers: a wisher scope call is flagged, not decided by the driver). the SOFT accrual-WARN is BUILT as the v1 best-guess (computeCloneAccrualWarn + CLONE_ACCRUAL_WARN_THRESHOLD, tested), which makes the accrual VISIBLE per rule.require.status-feedback with no block on a valid burst. the HARD-cap-vs-soft-warn choice is ledger row 5(i046→i049), explicitly flagged for the wisher council — the driver best-guesses soft + flags, per rule.always.defer-fulcrums-to-last, rather than unilaterally impose a hard cap that fights the wish |
| 1(i057) | invokeEnroll's i049 accrual step INLINED the impure live-count pipeline (`getAllClonesForActor → getCloneReachState per clone → count LIVE`) in the contract-layer invoker — the decode-friction the never-run mech-decode-friction lens finally caught (r3 blocker + nitpick, one item) | RESOLVED — folded in (i057-r03-1) | extracted the count into a NAMED communicator leaf `getOneCloneLiveCountForActor` (`{ actorDir } → number`), which invokeEnroll now composes, so the invoker reads as narrative and never inlines the pipeline (rule.forbid.inline-decode-friction / rule.require.named-transformers). the SAME extraction discipline the blueprint already applied to computeCloneUnreachableHint (out of invokeCloneSay/Get) and computeCloneSlugDecision (out of genClone) — the accrual step was the one invoker pipeline that had escaped it. the impure enumerate+tally lives in the leaf (a `get`-derivation communicator, socket-probe i/o), the pure count-vs-threshold stays computeCloneAccrualWarn; getOneCloneLiveCountForActor.integration proves the tally (two-live/one-dead → 2, all-dead → 0, none → 0, fallback excluded), computeCloneAccrualWarn.test the boundary |
